HT_DATA.load("detail", {"5ESJH1NLMLs":{"wy":"HN brings it up in any thread about autopilot, Tesla FSD, self-driving cars and crashes as the classic lecture on automation dependency.","q":{"x":"if you haven't seen this amazing talk, you should definitely give it a watch","i":42113322,"y":"2024"},"m":[{"id":42113322,"author":"fastball","month":"2024-11","story":"Marine pilot loses command after ejecting from F-35B that kept flying","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Amazing talk on pilots over-relying on automation","quote":"if you haven't seen this amazing talk, you should definitely give it a watch","text":"Sounds like Colonel Del Pizzo let himself become a child of the magenta line[1]. (if you haven't seen this amazing talk, you should definitely give it a watch) [1] [VIDEO]"},{"id":29763056,"author":"rsync","month":"2022-01","story":"Boeing 777 departing Dubai nearly had a major incident after takeoff","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Fascinating talk on automation dependency even for non-pilots","quote":"I cannot recommend this video enough.","text":"\"The thing that stands out here the most is the complete lack of control that the crew had over the plane. The crew was managing the computers rather than flying the aircraft .\" Perhaps they are \"Children of the Magenta\": [VIDEO] ... and I cannot recommend this video enough. I am not a pilot and I have no interest in flying nor any particular interest in aviation, generally ... and I find this talk to be fascinating . EDIT: you can jump to 3:35 where he gives a specific example of a situation where pilots need to choose between automation dependency and dropping down a level of automation. He's a really good speaker and you can tell the audience is very engaged."},{"id":29501782,"author":"scarier","month":"2021-12","story":"Artificial Intelligence in the Cockpit","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Should be mandatory viewing for those adding flight automation","quote":"I think this lecture ( ) should be mandatory for anyone trying to improve flight safety with more automation.","text":"I think this lecture ( [VIDEO] ) should be mandatory for anyone trying to improve flight safety with more automation. I think we're already at diminishing returns, and the only way to eliminate accidents caused by over-reliance on automated systems is to cut the pilot out of the loop entirely. Even in general aviation, the low-hanging fruit is elsewhere. That said, this is still pretty cool, and I could see something like it being one component of a much larger fully automated flight management system. Edit: link should be fixed. If I'm still crushing it, the title of the lecture is \"Children of the Magenta Line.\""},{"id":49181796,"author":"scrlk","month":"2026-08","story":"Civilian plane crash in New Mexico tied to military GPS blocking","stance":"rec","why":"Good lecture on automation dependency","quote":"there's a good lecture called Children of the Magenta Line that discusses automation dependency","text":"To add to this, there's a good lecture called Children of the Magenta Line that discusses automation dependency: [VIDEO]"},{"id":47833490,"author":"kotaKat","month":"2026-04","story":"Tesla Hid Fatal Accidents to Continue Testing Autonomous Driving (French)","stance":"rec","why":"Lecture on automation dependence relevant to Tesla FSD users","quote":"need to see some kind of modern lecture based on the Children of the Magenta talk on automation dependence in aircraft.","text":"Hot take but I feel like Tesla owners (hell, anyone with 'autonomous driving' vehicles) need to see some kind of modern lecture based on the Children of the Magenta talk on automation dependence in aircraft. Mandatory, before you can trigger the system on. FSD has built this generation's newest children of the magenta line. [VIDEO]"},{"id":46269022,"author":"Animats","month":"2025-12","story":"AI and the ironies of automation – Part 2","stance":"rec","why":"Chief pilot on how to use cockpit automation","quote":"an airline chief pilot talking to his pilots about cockpit automation and how to use it.","text":"Bainbridge [1] is interesting, but dated. A more useful version of that discussion from slightly later is \"Children of the Magenta\", [2] an airline chief pilot talking to his pilots about cockpit automation and how to use it. Requires a basic notion of aviation jargon. There's been progress since then. Although the details are not widely publicized, enough pilots of the F-22, F-35, or the Gripen have talked about what modern fighter cockpit automation is like. The real job of fighter pilots is to fight and win battles, not drive the airplane. A huge amount of effort has been put into simplifying the airplane driving job so the pilot can focus on killing targets. The general idea today is that the pilot puts the pointy end in the right direction and the control systems take care of the details. An F-22 pilot has been quoted as saying that the F-22 is far less fussy than a Cessna as a flying machine. For the F-35, which has a VTOL configuration (B) and a carrier-landing configuration (C), much effort was put into making VTOL landing and carrier landing easy. Not because pilots can't learn to do it, but because training tended to obsess on those tasks. The hard part of Harrier (the only previous successful VTOL fighter) was learning to land the unstable beast without crashing. There were still a lot of Harrier crashes. The hard part of Naval aviator training is landing on a carrier deck. Neither of these tasks has anything to do with the real job of taking a bite out of the enem"},{"id":46390630,"author":"traceroute66","month":"2025-12","story":"Who Watches the Waymos? I do [video]","stance":"rec","why":"Famous extract from American Airlines automation course","quote":"Well worth the watch","text":"> You don't want pilots with their heads down in a computer making changes when they can, and should, just fly the plane. There is a famous video on youtube, 'Children of the Magenta Line' , an extract from an American Airlines course decades ago. Well worth the watch: [VIDEO]"},{"id":42605457,"author":"wat10000","month":"2025-01","story":"Why was there a wall near runway at S Korea plane crash airport?","stance":"rec","why":"Excellent lecture on pilots who reconfigure autopilot instead of taking over","quote":"excellent lecture about this called Children of the Magenta Line.","text":"There are numerous past examples of this sort of thing. Automation in aviation is really hard to get right. If the automation can fail then the pilots need to be able to perform whatever it was going to do. If the automation fails rarely then the pilots may not get enough practice. But if the automation normally does a better job than the pilots, there’s a tension with letting them get more practice on real flights. A recent(ish) example is the Asiana crash in SF. They had pretty much perfect conditions for a hand-flown visual approach, but they were out of practice, got behind the airplane, and it snowballed. There’s an excellent lecture about this called Children of the Magenta Line. The magenta line being the flight path or direction indicator on an autopilot, and the discussion is about pilots who constantly reconfigure the autopilot to direct the plane instead of just taking over. [VIDEO]"},{"id":40616969,"author":"conartist6","month":"2024-06","story":"Boeing Passenger Jet Nearly Crashes Because of Known Software Bug","stance":"rec","why":"Why pilots drop to lower automation levels in emergencies","quote":"If you want to know more, check out Children of the Magenta Line","text":"It's actually the right way of thinking! When high-level automation fails you, messing around with the high-level system in a critical moment would probably be your death. Debugging takes minutes not seconds. This is why pilots are trained to drop from a high level automation to a lower one when necessary. Like most learnings about piloting, this insight was purchased with a lot of blood. If you want to know more, check out Children of the Magenta Line: [VIDEO]"},{"id":37090043,"author":"rsync","month":"2023-08","story":"The crash of Air France flight 447 (2021)","stance":"rec","why":"Fascinating instructional video on automation dependency","quote":"These AA instructional videos by Captain Vanderburgh are fascinating - even to a non-pilot like myself.","text":"Perhaps these pilots were \"Children of the Magenta\"[1] ? These AA instructional videos by Captain Vanderburgh are fascinating - even to a non-pilot like myself. They are especially relevant as we dip our toes into automobile auto-pilot and the \"automation dependency\" that comes along with it. Of particular note in this video: @ 12:30: \"... tactily connected to the airplane ...\" @ 14:15: \"... we see automation dependent crews, lacking confidence in their own ability to fly an airplane are turning to ther autopilot ...\" @ 17:35 - 18:15: (just listen) [1] [VIDEO]"},{"id":34111442,"author":"sokoloff","month":"2022-12","story":"The Reliability Trap: The crash of Emirates flight 521","stance":"rec","why":"Advice on when to shift from automation to manual control","quote":"Covers advice on when to go from maximum automation towards more manual control of the aircraft when the cockpit gets busy.","text":"Folks interested in this topic might also enjoy the AA Training Video “Children of the Magenta Line”: [VIDEO] Covers advice on when to go from maximum automation towards more manual control of the aircraft when the cockpit gets busy."},{"id":29769329,"author":"howenterprisey","month":"2022-01","story":"We need to reclaim our attention","stance":"rec","why":"Example of a strong captured lecture found on YouTube","quote":"YouTube was the way I found them, how I watch them, and how I share them with other people.","text":"The \"occasional captured lectures\" are still pretty strong. Off the top of my head, here are two lectures: [VIDEO] \"Children of the Magenta Line\" by Warren Vanderburgh [LINK] \"Total Synthesis of Vitamin B12\" by RB Woodward You may tell me I'm cheating by posting videos that are obviously not \"native\" to YouTube, but YouTube was the way I found them, how I watch them, and how I share them with other people. OK, so YouTube-native videos are hard mode. Try [LINK] I suppose? I like it. It has life lessons I'd argue are at least as valuable as the videos you're thinking about."},{"id":25700321,"author":"sokoloff","month":"2021-01","story":"Sriwijaya Air flight SJ182, a Boeing 737, has disappeared from radar","stance":"rec","why":"Presentation on automation dependence, with humor","quote":"Children of the Magenta Line is a great presentation on automation dependence and quite funny at moments.","text":"That is from 2013 (not saying it’s invalid advice, just that it’s not related to current flying schedules). Children of the Magenta Line is a great presentation on automation dependence and quite funny at moments. [VIDEO] (The GPS flight plan track is standardized to be drawn in magenta.)"},{"id":48461087,"author":"kotaKat","month":"2026-06","story":"The better the autopilot the worse the pilot","stance":"reference","why":"Source of the phrase on automation dependency","quote":"in his talk on automation dependency in the 90s.","text":"American Airlines captain Warran VanderBurgh once called this phenomenon \"the children of the magenta\"[1] in his talk on automation dependency in the 90s. I wonder what we'd call the children today in hindsight and what line they're chasing now... [1] [VIDEO]"},{"id":46085614,"author":"rsync","month":"2025-11","story":"Flight disruption warning as Airbus requests modifications to 6k planes","stance":"reference","why":"Cited as authority on automation dependent crews","quote":"Automation dependent pilots allowed their airplanes to get much closer to the edge of the envelope than they should have","text":"Both unsophisticated lay observers and capital/owners tend to fault operators ... for different reasons. Accident studies and, in particular, books like _Normal Accidents_[1] push back on this assumptions: \"... It made the case for examining technological failures as the product of highly interacting systems, and highlighted organizational and management factors as the main causes of failures. Technological disasters could no longer be ascribed to isolated equipment malfunction, operator error, or acts of God.\" It is well accepted - and I believe - that there were a multitude of operator errors during the Air France 447 flight but none of them were unpredictable or exotic and the system they were tasked with operating was poorly designed and unhelpfully hid layers of complexity that suddenly re-emerged during tremendous \"production pressure\". But don't take my word for it - I appeal to authority[2]: \"Automation dependent pilots allowed their airplanes to get much closer to the edge of the envelope than they should have ...\"[3]. or: @ 14:15: \"... we see automation dependent crews, lacking confidence in their own ability to fly an airplane are turning to ther autopilot ...\"[4]. [1] [LINK] [2] Captain Vanderburgh [3] Children of Magenta: [LINK] [4] [VIDEO]"},{"id":38843569,"author":"throw0101d","month":"2024-01","story":"JAL A359 at Tokyo collided with Coast Guard DH8C on runway and burst into flames","stance":"reference","why":"Cited for balancing automation level to the situation","quote":"One has to be careful about having the right level of automation and control for the given situation","text":"> Driving doesn’t require an advanced degree in physics. It’s mostly a mish mash of fairly mundane tasks. Generally not wrong, but when it becomes non-mundane, it can become so in a hurry. This is why I'm sometimes skeptical about statistics that say \" x% of airplane crashes are human error \". Well maybe, but by the time the humans get involved the automated systems have often thrown up their proverbial hands and digitally exclaimed \" Jesus take the wheel \". I think having computers take over the mundane parts may help collision rates as people can get numbed and bored by the monotony. One has to be careful about having the right level of automation and control for the given situation, as per the famous \"Children of the Magenta Line\" presentation: * [VIDEO] > “Children of the magenta line” has become shorthand for pilots who manage and monitor systems but lack the stick-and-rudder skills to fly the airplane with authority. You may hear it wielded as an epithet in a discussion of “real pilots” versus “kids these days.” But in many cockpits today, automation illiteracy may be as dangerous as automation dependency. Vanderburgh’s insights shouldn’t drive us to reject automation altogether, but rather put it in its proper place in our pilot toolkits. Just as you should hand-fly regularly to keep your stick-and-rudder skills sharp, you should also practice using the technology available in the aircraft you fly. * [LINK] * [LINK] * [LINK] * [LINK]"},{"id":33939425,"author":"throw0101c","month":"2022-12","story":"Airlines lobbying FAA to have only one pilot in the cockpit","stance":"reference","why":"Well-known 1997 presentation on crew dependence on autopilot","quote":"There is relatively well-know presentation from 1997 by American Airlines captain Warren Van Der Burgh","text":"There is relatively well-know presentation from 1997 by American Airlines captain Warren Van Der Burgh called \"Children of Magenta\" that talked about the risk of crew being too dependent on the guiding magenta-colored lines of the autopilot on their screens: * [VIDEO] * [LINK] * [LINK] Some of the pros and cons of cockpit automation: * [LINK] Generally automation exists on a spectrum, and the pilot should be able to select its level depending on the circumstances: > In Van’s presentation he describes three levels of automation: > *The pilot manually flying the aircraft. > *The pilot using the flight director, autopilot, and autopilot modes to fly the aircraft for a short period of time. For example: Heading select, Flight Level Change, Vertical Speed, Indicated Airspeed, etc. > *The pilot using the FMS to command the autopilot to fly the aircraft for hours at a time. * [LINK]"},{"id":30153959,"author":"rsync","month":"2022-01","story":"Please make a dumb car","stance":"reference","why":"Accident statistics on automation dependency applied to car features","quote":"automation dependent pilots allowed their airplanes to get much closer to the edge of the envelope than they should have","text":"\"I like most of the \"smart\" features - lane centering, adaptive cruise, emergency braking ...\" Please drive your car. I don't care what choices you make wrt bluetooth or heated seats or iphone integration ... but if you can't be bothered to drive the car then perhaps a different transport option would be a better choice for you. [VIDEO] \"... as we look at this accident history what we find is that in 68% of these accidents, automation dependency plays a significant part ...\" \"... automation dependent pilots allowed their airplanes to get much closer to the edge of the envelope than they should have ...\""},{"id":27307479,"author":"throwaway77112","month":"2021-05","story":"Tesla loses U.S. designation for some advanced safety features","stance":"reference","why":"Automation dependence talk cited among Tesla autopilot links","quote":"Edit 2: Children of the Magenta Line","text":"Videos of Tesla fails, mostly from 2021: [1] [LINK] [2] [LINK] [3] [LINK] [4] [LINK] [5] [LINK] Other things: [1] [LINK] [2] a video which was taken down from youtube [LINK] [3] [LINK] [4] [LINK] . Bonus material: [1] [LINK] Edit: For saner info regarding self-driving, A.I etc: [1] [LINK] [2] [LINK] [3] [LINK] [4] book to read, suitable even for laypeople >> 'Artificial Intelligence: A Guide for Thinking Humans by Melanie Mitchell IMO everyone should read that book Edit 2: Children of the Magenta Line [VIDEO]"},{"id":27326624,"author":"throwaway24006","month":"2021-05","story":"Camera-Only Autopilot Requires Automatic High Beams Enabled","stance":"reference","why":"Cited as example of autopilot perils","quote":"Perils of autopilot: Children of the Magenta Line","text":"Copy-pasting a glorious Tesla comment from yesterday: Videos of Tesla fails, mostly from 2021: [1] [LINK] [2] [LINK] [3] [LINK] [4] [LINK] [5] [LINK] Other things: [1] [LINK] [2] a video which was taken down from youtube [LINK] [3] [LINK] [4] [LINK] . Bonus material: [1] [LINK] For saner info regarding self-driving, A.I etc: [1] [LINK] [2] [LINK] [3] [LINK] [4] book to read, suitable even for laypeople >> 'Artificial Intelligence: A Guide for Thinking Humans by Melanie Mitchell Perils of autopilot: Children of the Magenta Line [VIDEO]"}],"r":[["WU0gvPcc3jQ",1],["Uj-rK8V-rik",1],["3UxZDJ1HiPE",1],["ACkmg3Y64_s",1],["JOLtS4VUcvQ",1],["nnR8fDW3Ilo",1],["3TFz1xqp3us",1],["wM8Xli2KTzI",1],["fzBRYsiyxjI",1],["nsJZ_kKjXcE",1],["eviSykqSUUw",1],["LtFyP0qy9XU",1],["YCjNT9qGjh4",1],["OyBNmecVtdU",1],["9zinZmE3Ogk",1],["5uhIiI9Ld5M",1],["WITLR_qSPXk",1],["xtvT2XYlcOY",1],["7nL10C7FSbE",1],["5JYp9eGC3Cc",1]]},"BzhbAK1pdPM":{"wy":"HN commenters repeatedly recommend Barkley's lectures as the best introduction to understanding ADHD.","q":{"x":"Probably the best introduction to ADHD is a 1.5 lecture by Dr. Russell Barkley, a top researcher in this area.","i":29679830,"y":"2021"},"m":[{"id":39506580,"author":"mthoms","month":"2024-02","story":"Early humans had ADHD, scientists say after making people play game online","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Barkley's ADHD lecture playlist.","quote":"I highly recommend the first 4 parts of this playlist (at least) and other videos and writings by Dr Barkley.","text":"[VIDEO] I highly recommend the first 4 parts of this playlist (at least) and other videos and writings by Dr Barkley."},{"id":29679830,"author":"jevyjevjevs","month":"2021-12","story":"ADHD Accommodations Guide","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Technical, empowering, fun introductory lecture by top ADHD researcher.","quote":"Probably the best introduction to ADHD is a 1.5 lecture by Dr. Russell Barkley, a top researcher in this area.","text":"I got diagnosed with ADHD 3 months ago after discovering a) my daughter had it and b) that it's highly hereditary. Probably the best introduction to ADHD is a 1.5 lecture by Dr. Russell Barkley, a top researcher in this area. It's technical, empowering and fun: [VIDEO] Looking back in my life, I've built sophisticated TODO list and second brain systems to help me manage. Now it's obvious that it's because my working memory is poor because of my neurodivergency. Omnifocus (for GTD) and Obsidian (to be an extension of my brain) are my goto for leveraging my ADHD superpower. If you're looking for a community to learn more for yourself or for someone else: reddit.com/r/adhd is a great place to start."},{"id":22415240,"author":"zarmin","month":"2020-02","story":"Early riser or night owl? New study may help to explain the difference","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Barkley as best resource for understanding ADHD.","quote":"Your best resource, you may have discovered, is Russell Barkley; I cannot recommend him enough.","text":"Yeah, I think there's a large subset of people with ADHD who have some kind of alternate circadian rhythm. Mine seems to be 36 hours; for almost 35 years my peak working hours have been 4pm to 5am, with the ability to hyperfocus until I collapsed if I'm really into what I'm doing. Not for lack of trying to have a \"normal\" schedule...oh god I try really hard multiple times a year. It's just not natural. You should know that stimulants are only one type of approach for treating ADHD. Your best resource, you may have discovered, is Russell Barkley; I cannot recommend him enough. Also check out the YouTube channel How to ADHD. Some relevant Barkley videos: [LINK] [VIDEO] Fair warning: You may discover some of the things you've done for your whole life without second guessing are actually typical ADHD symptoms. And if you're like me, this might thoroughly shake your snowglobe."},{"id":19898251,"author":"andai","month":"2019-05","story":"How I Run a Company with ADHD","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Barkley lecture series teaching that ADHD is primarily emotional.","quote":"I too highly recommend Russel Barkley's videos about ADHD. They taught me so much.","text":"I too highly recommend Russel Barkley's videos about ADHD. They taught me so much. The most striking thing was learning how it's a primarily emotional disorder, and how this has harmed my relationships. Here's a fantastic lecture (split into ~5 minute segments -- this guy gets it :) by Dr. Barkley: ADHD - The 30 Essential Ideas Everyone Needs to Know [VIDEO]"},{"id":34398473,"author":"darekkay","month":"2023-01","story":"Four Thousand Weeks","stance":"rec","why":"Presentation on ADHD to understand its difference from ordinary habits.","quote":"What I can recommend is Dr Russel's ADHD presentation [2]","text":"> these videos are dumb, doesn't everyone do those things? Have any person go through the adult/inattentive-type ADHD symptoms [1]. Everyone will see themselves nodding. It's difficult for people to understand the difference if they are not affected. There's a difference between common headaches and migraine, or between bad mood and depression. It's about the intensity and the frequency (broadly speaking). This doesn't mean that everyone who procrastinates has ADHD. It's really hard to explain the state of complete mental unability to perform a task that I don't like to do vs. \"being lazy\". When I first described my symptoms to my wife, she was also like \"everyone has those symptoms from time to time\". Only after reading a chapter from a book about adult ADHD she understood the difference. Unfortunately, the book is in German. What I can recommend is Dr Russel's ADHD presentation [2] and Roman Kogan's ADHD Wiki [3]. > What intervention(s) have you found the most helpful? Well before learning about ADHD, I have developed many coping mechanisms. The use of todo lists, calendars and alarm clocks has probably made the biggest difference. As you have mentioned the \"chase for novelty\", I can only imagine how big your bookmark list has become. I've coped with this one by having a single \"inbox\" bookmark folder and clearing it frequently. Anything I want to keep for a longer time ends up in one of my tools (Static Marks [4] - a tool I've made for long-term bookmarks, OneNote for note"},{"id":33085447,"author":"triggercut","month":"2022-10","story":"Ask HN: How to find what I am really good at?","stance":"rec","why":"Barkley lectures for deeper ADHD understanding.","quote":"Maybe look into some of Dr Russel Barkley's lectures.","text":">long term effects of putting people on a close chemical cousin of methamphetamine indefinitely What is your basis for this \"concern\"? Sure it sounds bad, but there are plenty of chemical compounds with \"off by one\" differences that have completely different effects.... that's just... chemistry. Just so you know, (depending on who you read) those with untreated ADHD may live, on average, 10 fewer years than the rest of the population. So if I had to chose between your concerns and 10 better quality years, I know what I'm picking. > Medical professionals are just speculating at this point when they say it's some kind of neurological disorder rather than just part of the range of physiologically normal brain function. Where are you getting this from? Any papers? If this is the consensus then I really must not have been paying attention (pun intended) You seem to have a lot of \"observational\" experience in a specific setting, but little more knowledge or understanding than the tip of the iceberg \"top-5 symptoms\". ADHD is an incredibly complex developmental issue. Look at the issues with ADHD and gauging signals from the body, how those affect eating. Look at various issues with sensitivities sensory stimulation. Issues with inhibition and how they permeate behaviours. Reducing ADHD to \"focus\" is like reducing baseball to throwing a ball around. Maybe look into some of Dr Russel Barkley's lectures. Some of them are a little dated but mostly accurate. [VIDEO]"},{"id":29691255,"author":"fragmede","month":"2021-12","story":"30 Essential Ideas about ADHD – Dr. Russell a Barkley","stance":"rec","why":"Full lecture series on YouTube in digestible playlist chunks.","quote":"This is an invaluable resource, but is available directly on YouTube at","text":"This is an invaluable resource, but is available directly on YouTube at [VIDEO] (Playlist breaks it up into stomachable chunks.)"},{"id":18512831,"author":"jaggederest","month":"2018-11","story":"Boss as a Service – Hire a boss, get stuff done","stance":"rec","why":"Barkley's ADHD lectures.","quote":"I also like Russell Barkley's lectures and books","text":"As someone who has recently started figuring out this whole ADHD thing, definitely seek out help for it. There are a ton of really powerful tools and support systems that make a tremendous difference. Especially organizational systems designed for people with executive function deficits (whether that's ADHD or not, doesn't really matter!) Caddra has an interesting chart here for suggestions: [LINK] I also like Russell Barkley's lectures and books: [VIDEO] and [LINK] (especially Taking Charge of Adult ADHD) And additionally, if you find that these things speak to you, medication makes a tremendous difference. I really can't overstate the degree to which it can change your life."},{"id":14410229,"author":"Helmet","month":"2017-05","story":"How a CEO with Dyslexia and ADHD Runs His Company","stance":"rec","why":"Russell Barkley lectures as an insightful reference on ADHD.","quote":"His Youtube videos/lectures are extremely insightful as well","text":"There is absolutely no benefit to having a LD. Seriously, there is NONE - people with LDs who attain success do so in spite of their LD, not because of it. This notion that LDs make you more \"creative\" or whatever is backed by absolutely no research and is mostly a bunch of feel-good nonsense. A great reference for this is Dr. Russell Barkley: [LINK] His Youtube videos/lectures are extremely insightful as well: [VIDEO]"},{"id":34912966,"author":"VHRanger","month":"2023-02","story":"Ask HN: Dealing with ADHD Without Medication?","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"","text":"ADHD is a quantitative disorder that touches a spectrum of symptoms. Yours might be heavier in some aspects and lighter in others. It's also often tied to other psychological issues. Not everything works the same for everyone. For \"vanilla ADHD\" the most impairing factors will often be: - Executive function. This can be organisation, getting started or keeping up with dopamine depleting tasks (chores, bills, etc.) and keeping track of time during tasks - Emotional regulation. This can be emotional reactivity (often called RSD online), or other all sorts of difficulties dealing with emotions in a productive way. This one is often left out in discussions but the most harmful for life outcomes (especially frustration/anger based dysregulations) - Short term memory issues. Part of executive function but should be noted specifically. The first thing to note is natural external dopamine will help. This is getting proper sleep, nutrition and exercise. Exercise, especially intense activities like HIIT will help regulate dopamine naturally. For executive function, the best is to have a system. There are plenty out there (bullet journals, productivity apps, etc.) Find one that you actually use without forcing yourself to. Can be a TODO list in google keep/apple notes, or a Bullet Journal or something like TikTik For short term memory issues, write everything down. This often plays into the system above. For emotional regulation, DBT is the best place to start. The DBT workbook can be f"},{"id":33087464,"author":"User23","month":"2022-10","story":"Ask HN: How to find what I am really good at?","stance":"reference","why":"Barkley lectures cited in an argument about ADHD.","quote":"","text":"> Just so you know, (depending on who you read) those with untreated ADHD may live, on average, 10 fewer years than the rest of the population. So if I had to chose between your concerns and 10 better quality years, I know what I'm picking. I don't have a problem with that. > Where are you getting this from? Any papers? If this is the consensus then I really must not have been paying attention (pun intended) The burden of proof is on them to show that they are not speculating. Some kind of repeatable physiological test would do nicely. > You seem to have a lot of \"observational\" experience in a specific setting, but little more knowledge or understanding than the tip of the iceberg \"top-5 symptoms\". ADHD is an incredibly complex developmental issue. Look at the issues with ADHD and gauging signals from the body, how those affect eating. Look at various issues with sensitivities sensory stimulation. Issues with inhibition and how they permeate behaviours. Reducing ADHD to \"focus\" is like reducing baseball to throwing a ball around. I may have confused you by mistakenly saying the brain is \"complicated\" when I should have said \"complex.\" What I meant was mind-bogglingly unimaginably confusingly tricky and hard to understand. Also, I don't recall reducing ADHD to \"focus,\" where did I do that? I'm quite curious, what are the top-6-through-10 symptoms that I should be made aware of so I can better understand? > Maybe look into some of Dr Russel Barkley's lectures. Some of them are"}],"r":[["-Rxvl3INKSg",1],["LnS0PfNyj4U",1],["nnl6nY8YKHs",1],["1faaOrtHJ-A",1],["_tpB-B8BXk0",1],["qv6UVOQ0F44",1],["cfyWvJdsDRI",1],["eQ2OZKitRwc",1],["6bqBsHSwPgw",1],["fTjhHrcyiQI",1]]},"15RTC22Z2xI":{"wy":"A staple HN recommendation in crypto and Web3 threads as an entertaining, compelling case against blockchain.","q":{"x":"James Mickens' \"Blockchains are a bad idea\" talk [1] is a great explanation that discusses exactly that.","i":28676710,"y":"2021"},"m":[{"id":28676710,"author":"asciimike","month":"2021-09","story":"Bitfinex just spent $23.7M in fees to make a single Ethereum transaction","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Great explanation of blockchain's problems.","quote":"James Mickens' \"Blockchains are a bad idea\" talk [1] is a great explanation that discusses exactly that.","text":"James Mickens' \"Blockchains are a bad idea\" talk [1] is a great explanation that discusses exactly that. [1]: [VIDEO]"},{"id":37341073,"author":"thesuperbigfrog","month":"2023-08","story":"Ask HN: Should I Learn Blockchain?","stance":"rec","why":"Entertaining talk on why blockchain suits few applications.","quote":"James Mickens gave an entertaining presentation about why blockchain is poorly suited for most application use cases","text":"James Mickens gave an entertaining presentation about why blockchain is poorly suited for most application use cases: [VIDEO]"},{"id":29326706,"author":"thesuperbigfrog","month":"2021-11","story":"An engineer's observations on Web3 and its possibilities","stance":"rec","why":"Gives several arguments against blockchain systems like Bitcoin.","quote":"James Mickens gives several arguments against blockchain-based systems like Bitcoin","text":"Web3 requires retrofitting the web to use blockchain technologies to do what the web can mostly do already. In \"Blockchains Are a Bad Idea\", James Mickens gives several arguments against blockchain-based systems like Bitcoin: See his presentation here: [VIDEO] 1. People have out-of-band trust relationships in real life which reduce the likelihood of malice. Bitcoin-style anonymous identities undermine trust relationships and are not needed for legitimate (not illegal) transactions. 2. Real life legal systems encourage good behaviors. If you have a dispute with someone, you can sue them. Bitcoin and related systems lack these protections. 3. Existing tools such as public-key cryptography and digital signatures can provide most of the functionality that applications need without the problems that blockchain-based systems have."},{"id":29396180,"author":"thesuperbigfrog","month":"2021-11","story":"Web3 is not Decentralisation – it’s a Ploy to put Crypto Bros in Charge","stance":"rec","why":"Gives several arguments against blockchain systems like Bitcoin.","quote":"James Mickens gives several arguments against blockchain-based systems like Bitcoin","text":"Web3 requires retrofitting the web to use blockchain technologies to do what the web can mostly do already. In \"Blockchains Are a Bad Idea\", James Mickens gives several arguments against blockchain-based systems like Bitcoin: See his presentation here: [VIDEO] 1. People have out-of-band trust relationships in real life which reduce the likelihood of malice. Bitcoin-style anonymous identities undermine trust relationships and are not needed for legitimate (not illegal) transactions. 2. Real life legal systems encourage good behaviors. If you have a dispute with someone, you can sue them. Bitcoin and related systems lack these protections. 3. Existing tools such as public-key cryptography and digital signatures can provide most of the functionality that applications need without the problems that blockchain-based systems have."},{"id":28819217,"author":"thesuperbigfrog","month":"2021-10","story":"Web3 Architecture and How It Compares to Traditional Web Apps","stance":"rec","why":"Gives several arguments against blockchain systems like Bitcoin.","quote":"James Mickens gives several arguments against blockchain-based systems like Bitcoin","text":"Exactly. Making money on blockchain requires retrofitting the web to use blockchain to do what the web can mostly do already. In \"Blockchains Are a Bad Idea\", James Mickens gives several arguments against blockchain-based systems like Bitcoin: See his presentation here: [VIDEO] 1. People have out-of-band trust relationships in real life which reduce the likelihood of malice. Bitcoin-style anonymous identities undermine trust relationships and are not needed for legitimate (not illegal) transactions. 2. Real life legal systems encourage good behaviors. If you have a dispute with someone, you can sue them. Bitcoin and related systems lack these protections. 3. Existing tools such as public-key cryptography and digital signatures can provide most of the functionality that applications need without the problems that blockchain-based systems have."},{"id":28581100,"author":"thesuperbigfrog","month":"2021-09","story":"Ask HN: What is your strongest argument AGAINST Bitcoin?","stance":"rec","why":"Gives several arguments against blockchain systems like Bitcoin.","quote":"James Mickens gives several arguments against blockchain-based systems like Bitcoin","text":"In \"Blockchains Are a Bad Idea\", James Mickens gives several arguments against blockchain-based systems like Bitcoin: See his presentation here: [VIDEO] 1. People have out-of-band trust relationships in real life which reduce the likelihood of malice. Bitcoin-style anonymous identities undermine trust relationships and are not needed for legitimate (not illegal) transactions. 2. Real life legal systems encourage good behaviors. If you have a dispute with someone, you can sue them. Bitcoin and related systems lack these protections. 3. Existing tools such as public-key cryptography and digital signatures can provide most of the functionality that applications need without the problems that blockchain-based systems have."},{"id":25333762,"author":"how2cflags","month":"2020-12","story":"Steve Wozniak launches Efforce, a blockchain-based energy-saving platform","stance":"rec","why":"Talk makes several points about blockchain's legal and trust issues.","quote":"the talk for anyone who's interested considering he conveys a few interesting points I've seen throughout this thread","text":"I concur with your point, considering we already have methods like hashing to create rather unique signatures, and with frameworks and regulations(laws). I'm going to vaguely quote James Mickens here, but you can basically sue someone if there is an issue. If there is an issue on the block chain? You need to fork it. Oh, and the talk for anyone who's interested considering he conveys a few interesting points I've seen throughout this thread. [VIDEO]"},{"id":22277231,"author":"DyslexicAtheist","month":"2020-02","story":"SEC Commissioner's “safe harbor” proposal for ICOs will only enable scammers","stance":"rec","why":"Top item in a curated list of blockchain myth-busting resources.","quote":"I started curating blogs and videos (as myth-busters) to help me bring the point across in discussions.","text":":D I also got so annoyed that I started curating blogs and videos (as myth-busters) to help me bring the point across in discussions. My experience with this is sadly that changing anyone's mind who has already made it up is _really_ hard. Here is my list: Blockchains Are a Bad Idea (James Mickens) [VIDEO] Hacking Blockchain (Konstantinos Karagiannis, Chief Technology Officer, Security Consulting, BT) [LINK] Myth Busting: Can a blockchain save healthcare? [LINK] Blockchain Graveyard [LINK] Response to Blockchain & Blockcerts Critiques from Privacy Researcher [LINK] Blockcerts: Using blokchain for identity management is (mostly) ridiculous [LINK] Blockchains from a Distributed Computing Perspective [LINK] Do you need a Blockchain? [LINK] IOTA Tweets from cybergibbons: [LINK]"},{"id":18745508,"author":"raghava","month":"2018-12","story":"Ask HN: Best talks of 2018?","stance":"rec","why":"Offered as one of the best talks of 2018.","quote":"Blockchains Are a Bad Idea (James Mickens)","text":"Blockchains Are a Bad Idea (James Mickens) [VIDEO] I find almost all from GOTO 2018 very insightful. [LINK] esp ones listed below. GOTO 2018 • The Do's and Don'ts of Error Handling • Joe Armstrong [LINK] GOTO 2018 • Pragmatic Event-Driven Microservices • Allard Buijze [LINK] GOTO 2018 • Why Business Cases are Toxic • Chris Matts [LINK] GOTO 2018 • Unconditional Code • Michael Feathers [LINK]"},{"id":17898580,"author":"tootie","month":"2018-09","story":"Bitcoin and other cryptocurrencies are useless","stance":"rec","why":"Breakdown of why blockchain is a poor fit for real problems.","quote":"James Mickens has a pretty good breakdown of why blockchain isn't a good solution for most real-world problems","text":"James Mickens has a pretty good breakdown of why blockchain isn't a good solution for most real-world problems: [VIDEO]"},{"id":17969709,"author":"Reedx","month":"2018-09","story":"Crypto’s 80% Plunge Is Now Worse Than the Dot-Com Crash","stance":"rec","why":"Compelling, fun lecture on blockchain's problems.","quote":"Here's a compelling and fun to watch lecture that James Mickens (Harvard SEAS) recently gave about the problems with Blockchain","text":"Can only hide from fundamental issues for so long I suppose... Here's a compelling and fun to watch lecture that James Mickens (Harvard SEAS) recently gave about the problems with Blockchain - [VIDEO]"},{"id":18018169,"author":"onli","month":"2018-09","story":"GnuPG can now be used to perform notarial acts in the State of Washington","stance":"rec","why":"Funny, better explanation of why blockchain is a bad idea.","quote":"In better and funny: Blockchains Are a Bad Idea (James Mickens)","text":"Notarizing documents is a great example where some people think blockchain is a good use case, and it's never true. Where would be the advantage of the scheme you describe to the current system? There is none. The system already works: people are given power to validate documents by the authority. The authority stores that centrally. The validation is mostly collected centrally. All stuff you can do perfectly fine without needing to run replicated servers storing an evergrowing immutable blockchain sipping authority from the real authority. The current scheme allows things like secret notarization and revocation, stuff a blockchain is very bad at. > it'll be up to the receiving agencies like England's government to decide \"We trust the system the US uses to validate it's notiaries, so we'll add 'USRoxs' to our list of accepted Established Authorities (along with 'TheEnglish' of course)\" Countries already have systems in place to accept documents made in other countries. Works with official translators and stamps/seals of authenticity. In better and funny: Blockchains Are a Bad Idea (James Mickens), [VIDEO]"},{"id":23491212,"author":"toomuchtodo","month":"2020-06","story":"Someone paid $2.6M in fees to move $134 worth of cryptocurrency","stance":"reference","why":"Cited in support of the case against blockchain.","quote":"Technology doesn't fix trust and legal issues","text":"I trust my bank and the legal framework more than wallet software and a loose band of SWEs running a blockchain or DLT. If funds are wired to the wrong person in your jurisdiction, you can sue to recover it if the sending and receiving institutions can't claw it back. With crypto, you're SOL. Technology doesn't fix trust and legal issues, and a judge is going to laugh an immutable ledger out of a courtroom (or hold you in contempt). [VIDEO] [HBR: Blockchains Are a Bad Idea (James Mickens)]"}],"r":[["0PclgBd6_Zs",1],["Ag1AKIl_2GM",1],["ZRkm6-bBk4U",1],["KrksBdWcZgQ",1],["8X7Ng75e5gQ",1],["RIctzAQOe44",1],["DnPmg0R1M04",1],["mwrA5IRGpfU",1],["XA2WjJbmmoM",1],["PhNUjg9X4g8",1],["GKYCA3UsmrU",1],["PHe0bXAIuk0",1],["OKY6BGcx37k",1],["oAHbLRjF0vo",1],["4Z2uzEM0ugY",1],["4Q7FTjhvZ7Y",1],["d1EB1zsxW0k",1],["24R8JObNNQ4",1],["VscdPA6sUkc",1],["TTM_b7EJg5E",1]]},"HLRdruqQfRk":{"wy":"Tom7 is an HN favorite, and this video is praised as hilarious, educational silly ML that keeps getting better.","q":{"x":"it just keeps getting better and better as the video progresses. I laughed so hard.","i":34314619,"y":"2023"},"m":[{"id":39655926,"author":"Der_Einzige","month":"2024-03","story":"AI-Generated Data Can Poison Future AI Models","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Favorite YouTuber generating new letterforms","quote":"I love this guy so much and wish he made far more videos.","text":"I want to observe that one of my favorite youtubers did exactly this with making the \"uppest case\" and \"lowest case\" letters. [VIDEO] I love this guy so much and wish he made far more videos."},{"id":34314619,"author":"loxias","month":"2023-01","story":"Tetris Is Capable of Universal Computation","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Hilarious video that keeps getting better","quote":"it just keeps getting better and better as the video progresses. I laughed so hard.","text":"[VIDEO] (Uppestcase and Lowestcase Letters [advances in derp learning]) Is pure gold. I don't wanna spoil it, it just keeps getting better and better as the video progresses. I laughed so hard. If by some freak chance I ever become wealthy, I want to sponsor this sort of research."},{"id":31732594,"author":"devenvdev","month":"2022-06","story":"The Floppotron 3.0","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Entertaining and educational silly ML project","quote":"one of the most entertaining (and educational) experiences I had lately","text":"Came here to post this ↑ Tom7's Uppestcase and Lowestcase Letters [1] is one of the most entertaining (and educational) experiences I had lately [1] [VIDEO]"},{"id":26668083,"author":"ashertrockman","month":"2021-04","story":"Uppestcase and Lowestcase Letters","stance":"rec","why":"Author's video on the SIGBOVIK uppercase letters paper","quote":"Also, check out the author","text":"SIGBOVIK is proud to have published this groundbreaking work in its 2021 proceedings, which were released today. Also, check out the author's video: [VIDEO] For more non-serious research that is often executed seriously, see: [LINK]"},{"id":26776595,"author":"gspr","month":"2021-04","story":"std::unique_ptr implementation backed by Ethereum NFTs","stance":"rec","why":"Good starting point for Tom7's silly research","quote":"his SIGBOVIK submissions","text":"I assume you're very familiar with Tom7, and in particular his SIGBOVIK submissions, but just in case: Tom7! (Good starting point for anyone who's not: [VIDEO] )"},{"id":47058646,"author":"shit_game","month":"2026-02","story":"15 years later, Microsoft morged my diagram","stance":"reference","why":"AI letter artifacts recall this video","quote":"Lots of the AIisms with letters remind me of tom7","text":"Lots of the AIisms with letters remind me of tom7's SIGBOVIC video Uppestcase and Lowestcase Letters [advances in derp learning] [VIDEO]"},{"id":37752237,"author":"lachlan_gray","month":"2023-10","story":"Hey, Computer, Make Me a Font","stance":"reference","why":"Related font generation video with uppestcase letters","quote":"maybe we can finally get uppestcase and lowestcase fonts","text":"I found a few months ago that the gpt-4 code interpreter is capable of converting a black and white png of a glyph to an svg [LINK] It would be cool to combine a script like the one gpt-4 gave me with an image generation model to generate fonts. The approach from this blog post is way more interesting though. On a separate note it reminds me of this suckerpinch video :) maybe we can finally get uppestcase and lowestcase fonts [VIDEO]"}]},"hUyMNyrOHJQ":{"wy":"Frequently recommended in RSI and wrist pain threads by people who say the stretches relieved their own symptoms.","q":{"x":"This video saved me from debilitating hand and wrist pain.","i":18644400,"y":"2018"},"m":[{"id":18644400,"author":"thegabez","month":"2018-12","story":"Ask HN: What ergonomic solutions are you using?","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Stretching exercises that relieved debilitating hand and wrist pain.","quote":"This video saved me from debilitating hand and wrist pain.","text":"Look into stretching exercises. This video saved me from debilitating hand and wrist pain. I'm sure there are similar exercises you can perform for forearm, elbow, shoulder, etc.. [VIDEO]"},{"id":12754435,"author":"MikeTV","month":"2016-10","story":"Ask HN: RSI is getting really bad","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Stretches done daily for years that significantly helped RSI.","quote":"I've been doing these stretches a few times a day for the past several years, since I first felt RSI coming on. It's helped significantly.","text":"I've been doing these stretches a few times a day for the past several years, since I first felt RSI coming on. It's helped significantly. Whenever I start to feel wrist/hand pain, I know I've been slacking on them. [VIDEO]"},{"id":4009841,"author":"forgetcolor","month":"2012-05","story":"Ask HN: Best way to recover and deal with carpal tunnel?","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Short exercise video that got the commenter out of the worst of it.","quote":"for exercises, i highly recommend this short youtube video","text":"i deal with both carpal tunnel (diagnosed) and tendonitis. an ergonomic keyboard is a must (i use a kinesis freestyle). one of the most important equipment changes for me is to never use an actual mouse, and to never click a trackpad. instead i use a magic trackpad on my desktop mac, built in trackpad on my mbp, and a logitech trackpad on my pc desktops. all are configured with full gesture capability so i can use the tap features instead of clicking. also, no click-drag. instead i use the double tap to lock feature. i also mix it up by using my ipad. for browsing, for short emails, etc. the ipad requires different muscles so changes the patterns of use (a good thing). for exercises, i highly recommend this short youtube video: [VIDEO] . i think this was perhaps the best thing for me. it got me out of the worst of it, and keeps me from slipping back to my worst. i wear wrist splints in bed. this might not be for everyone, but my doc suggested it. a lot of people bend their wrists at night, stressing the muscles and tendons. i was definitely one who did. it helps. finally, ice. i use ice packs to cool off the arms when i'm hurting. walgreens sells a nice inexpensive pack with a built in cover that works really well. i have 10 of them :) best of luck."},{"id":889083,"author":"qeorge","month":"2009-10","story":"Ask HN: Wrist Pain","stance":"rec","why":"Exercises for tendinitis and carpal tunnel that helped the commenter.","quote":"I've found the exercises in this video help me a lot","text":"I've found the exercises in this video help me a lot: [VIDEO] Also, when I was having problems a few years back, I narrowed it down to my laptop. The touchpad style mouse was killing my hands and wrists. It sounds like you're on a desktop, but I thought I'd mention it just in case."}]},"1PhArSujR_A":{"wy":"Cited again and again as Carmack's key public statement on functional programming and purity in game development, plus his remarks on garbage collection.","q":{"x":"There's an old video [0] where he talks in-depth about his explorations of functional programming.","i":40703686,"y":"2024"},"m":[{"id":40703686,"author":"ookdatnog","month":"2024-06","story":"Why \"Structure and Interpretation of Computer Programs\" matters (2011)","stance":"rec","why":"In-depth Carmack exploration of functional programming and what veterans can learn","quote":"There's an old video [0] where he talks in-depth about his explorations of functional programming.","text":"I think it is, and if you don't want to take my word for it, John Carmack thinks the same :) There's an old video [0] where he talks in-depth about his explorations of functional programming. It's perhaps a bit more about Haskell than about SICP, but in it he's also quite enthusiastic about what a veteran programmer can still learn from this book. [0] [VIDEO]"},{"id":18975656,"author":"lsh","month":"2019-01","story":"Functional programming in C++ (2012)","stance":"rec","why":"Carmack talks at length on functional style in C++","quote":"There is also the accompanying Quakecon presentation where John Carmack talks at length about all this","text":"Excellent read, I still remember a lot of it. There is also the accompanying Quakecon presentation where John Carmack talks at length about all this: [VIDEO]"},{"id":15288536,"author":"akmiller","month":"2017-09","story":"Global Mutable State","stance":"rec","why":"Carmack on a Haskell game engine experiment","quote":"Very interesting talk,","text":"What you describe above is similar to what Carmack discussed at his QuakeCon keynote several years back when toying with a game engine on Haskell. Very interesting talk, [VIDEO]"},{"id":14908855,"author":"mrkgnao","month":"2017-08","story":"Elm in Production: 25K Lines Later","stance":"rec","why":"Carmack on how purity eases large-scale game refactors","quote":"you might want to see what John Carmack has to say[0]","text":"The use of monads is a side-effect (ha!) of committing to purity throughout a language, and that's what FP is being equated to: pure statically-typed FP. (You can argue about how justified that is, of course. I'm not going into that, but you might want to see what John Carmack has to say[0]. No, he doesn't end by saying \"we should all convert to the church of Haskell now\", but he does talk about how large-scale game programming refactors are made easier when you're working with no (or very disciplined) side effects.) Monads are not the only way to deal with effects while keeping purity, although they were the first discovered and so on: algebraic effect systems as in Koka[1] (and as simulated in Idris or PureScript) are another alternative. Koka infers effects, so it's probably easier for a C-family programmer to pick up (I know little about it, though). [0]: [VIDEO] [1]: [LINK]"},{"id":12637750,"author":"sssilver","month":"2016-10","story":"Ask HN: What's your favorite tech talk?","stance":"rec","why":"Carmack's talk on functional programming and Haskell","quote":"Carmack's talk about functional programming and Haskell","text":"Raymond Hettinger's talk about good code reviews -- [LINK] Carmack's talk about functional programming and Haskell -- [VIDEO] Jack Diederich's \"Stop Writing Classes\" -- [LINK] All with a good sense of humor."},{"id":45773696,"author":"ryandv","month":"2025-10","story":"John Carmack on mutable variables","stance":"reference","why":"Source of Carmack's quote on legal code eventually landing in codebases","quote":"","text":"> In practice programmers tend to avoid writing the kind of program where anything can happen. My faith in this presumption dwindles every year. I expect AI to only exacerbate the problem. Since we are on the topic of Carmack, \"everything that is syntactically legal that the compiler will accept will eventually wind up in your codebase.\" [0] [0] [VIDEO]"},{"id":25040704,"author":"MaulingMonkey","month":"2020-11","story":"Why Rust Is the Future of Game Development","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"","text":"> Lack of safety is a feature in game development, Bullshit. How many games have had releases delayed and major wrenches thrown into marketing plans because of progress ruining heisenbugs? How many failed certification passes from banal data races and other undefined behavior? We trap UI in actionscript or javascript, and gameplay programmers in other scripting languages - perhaps python or lua - for faster iteration times, hot reloading, and safety . Because it's difficult enough to keep the build stable when it's merely all the engine programmers who should know better screwing things up with C++. This results in large messes of poorly performing, poorly optimized, garbage-collector laden code that Rust would handle much faster. We're leaving lots of performance on the table, often for little other purpouse than \"safety\". Console first day patches may have taken off some of the pressure for getting the first release right, but handhelds aren't always online and still have a pretty high bar. > the optimizations required are typically unorthodox Rust's `unsafe` keyword and intrinsics let you do all the unaligned intrinsic-laden data-racey technically-undefined-behavior micro-optimizations you might want to do in C++ in Rust just fine. It'll hopefully trigger a more stringent code review and force you to justify your pile of bugs, but that's a good thing. Or you can skip the code review if your entire company really disagrees. > a super strict language slows development down C"},{"id":22700666,"author":"MaulingMonkey","month":"2020-03","story":"It's not what programming languages do, it's what they shepherd you to","stance":"reference","why":"Carmack praising self-contained functional code","quote":"","text":"Reasoning about code requires reasoning about relevant state. On the one extreme, you have pure functional programming, where all state is passed in and returned out - all relevant state is explicit and \"obvious\". On the other extreme, you might use global state for everything - relevant state requires diving into all your code. This sounds unthinkable in the modern era, but similar styles aren't entirely uncommon in sufficiently old codebases with codebases that didn't really bother to use the stack. This is part of the reason why memory corruption bugs can be so insidious in large codebases - if anything in your codebase could've corrupted that bit of memory, and your codebase is millions of lines of code, you have a large haystack to find your bugs in, and your struggle will be to narrow down the relevant code to figure out where the bug actually is. This isn't hypothetical - I've had system UI switch to Chinese because of a use-after-free bug relating to gamepad use in other people's code, for example. (EDIT: Just to be clear - globals don't particularly exacerbate memory corruption issues, I'm just drawing some parallels between the difficulty in reasoning about global state and the difficulty in debugging memory corruption bugs.) > Game development John Carmack on the subject, praising nice and self contained functional code and at some point mentioning some of the horrible global flag driven messes that have caused problems in their codebase, mirroring my own experienc"},{"id":22709709,"author":"kungtotte","month":"2020-03","story":"It's not what programming languages do, it's what they shepherd you to","stance":"reference","why":"Carmack's ideas on parallelizing game engines","quote":"John Carmack has some ideas on how to parallelize game engines here","text":"I have no idea about how Doom Eternal does it, but John Carmack has some ideas on how to parallelize game engines here: [VIDEO]"},{"id":21859064,"author":"sxp","month":"2019-12","story":"Show HN: Angeldust – a fast and efficient video game","stance":"reference","why":"Carmack's presentation on an immutable Haskell game engine, light on detail","quote":"was Carmack's presentation on an immutable Haskell game engine. Unfortunately, he doesn't go into too much detail.","text":"[VIDEO] was Carmack's presentation on an immutable Haskell game engine. Unfortunately, he doesn't go into too much detail. [LINK] was the previous HN thread about it."},{"id":20336099,"author":"cutler","month":"2019-07","story":"Ask HN: Why do new(ish) programming languages eschew OOP features?","stance":"reference","why":"Cites Carmack as authority that functional programming is the future","quote":"Functional Programming is the Future","text":"According to John Carmack, the acclaimed expert in the field of game programming, \"Functional Programming is the Future\" - [VIDEO]"},{"id":18813197,"author":"aasasd","month":"2019-01","story":"Scratch 3.0","stance":"reference","why":"Carmack mentions a visual Lisp environment on iPad","quote":"John Carmack has mentioned that he uses a visual Lisp environment on iPad.","text":"John Carmack has mentioned that he uses a visual Lisp environment on iPad. Scheme-based, iirc. It's somewhere in here, I think (but alas I'm not up to listening again through half an hour of the old lady voice): [VIDEO] I feel like marrying Lisp's meta-programmable DSLs to an easy visual environment is the ultimate cosmic dream of 'domain-centered' programming and customization for non-coders―but it also seems to me that Lisp is rather text-based, trading a readymade set of operations picked in the interface for infinite extendability through text. So I'm not sure if a visual Lisp is any convenient to use. Also, for me the immediate downside of the Lisp environment in question is that it's only available for iPad."},{"id":17643530,"author":"pjmlp","month":"2018-07","story":"Java's Magic Sauce","stance":"reference","why":"Keynote section on garbage collection","quote":"Quakecon 2013 keynote section about GC","text":"My games development experience is quite irrelevant, however Tim Sweeney and John Carmack might know a thing or two about game engines. \"The Next Mainstream Programming Languages: A Game Developer's Perspective\" by Tim Sweeney [LINK] [LINK] \"Considering that 8 bit BASICs of the 70s had range checked and garbage collected strings, it is amazing how much damage C has done.\" John Carmack - [LINK] Quakecon 2013 keynote section about GC [VIDEO]"},{"id":15037825,"author":"bmc7505","month":"2017-08","story":"John Carmack on Functional Programming (2013) [video]","stance":"reference","why":"Carmack's discussion of GC in games","quote":"He discusses GC in games starting here","text":"Has there been any progress on garbage collection since then? He discusses GC in games starting here: [VIDEO]"},{"id":10874927,"author":"dottrap","month":"2016-01","story":"Why I Write Games in C","stance":"reference","why":"Carmack on Haskell potential for game dev","quote":"John Carmack thinks there is potential in Haskell or other functional languages for game dev","text":"John Carmack thinks there is potential in Haskell or other functional languages for game dev, so much so that he ported Wolfenstein 3D to Haskell as a summer project. I think he talks about in in this QuakeCon 2013 segment: [VIDEO]"},{"id":7004474,"author":"wting","month":"2014-01","story":"Xkcd: Haskell","stance":"reference","why":"Carmack exploring functional programming","quote":"John Carmack is exploring functional programming[0]","text":"From my perspective the Haskell community is fairly active and growing. At the moment #haskell has 1100 users tying #python and easily beating out ##c, #c++, #clojure, #lisp, #java, #javascript, and #ruby on Freenode. John Carmack is exploring functional programming[0], and this is Randall Munroe's second functional programming comic in recent weeks. [0]: [VIDEO] [1]: [LINK]"},{"id":6606725,"author":"javajosh","month":"2013-10","story":"The PC is not dead, we just don't need new ones","stance":"reference","why":"Carmack on functional programming and multi-core in games","quote":"Carmack at QuakeCon talking about functional programming (Haskell!) for games and multi-core issues","text":"Backend devs can probably use more computer resources, particularly cores and RAM. We want to simulate whole clusters on our dev machines and instrument them with tools like Ansible and Docker, and then deploy multiple (fairly heavyweight) processes like JVMs to them. But yeah, 4 (fast) cores and 16GB of RAM is available in a laptop these days, along with an SSD and the best display you can buy, for $3k. (Of course I'm speaking of the MBPr). Games can always use more resources. AFAIK there is still a lot of progress being made with GPUs. 60fps on a 4K display will be a good benchmark. The funny thing is that GPU makers have taken to literally just renaming and repackaging their old GPUs, e.g. the R9.[1] As for the game itself, there is a looming revolution in gaming when Carmack (or someone equally genius-y) really figures out how to coordinate multiple cores for gaming.[2] But yeah, most everything else runs fine on machines from 2006 and on, including most development tasks. That's why Intel in particular has been focused more on efficiency than power. [1] Tom's Hardware R9 review: [LINK] [2] Carmack at QuakeCon talking about functional programming (Haskell!) for games and multi-core issues: [VIDEO]"},{"id":6278519,"author":"Strilanc","month":"2013-08","story":"John Carmack: Thoughts on Haskell [video]","stance":"reference","why":"Fourth part of Carmack's QuakeCon keynote","quote":"The article is essentially just a link to the fourth part of his keynote at Quakecon","text":"Google cache: [LINK] The article is essentially just a link to the fourth part of his keynote at Quakecon ( [VIDEO] )"},{"id":6294968,"author":"epsylon","month":"2013-08","story":"What Programming a Game in 48 Hours Taught Me About Programming Games","stance":"reference","why":"Carmack on functional languages and Scheme for embedded scripting","quote":"John Carmack talks a bit about his experience with functional languages","text":"It depends on which type of games you're talking about. AAA game engines are still all about performance and tight memory budgets due to the nature of the platforms they're developing on - consoles and PCs. Hence the aversion to garbage collected languages, although higher level languages usually make it in the engines as scripting languages for game logic: UnrealScript for UDK, C# and UnityScript for Unity, Lua for CryEngine... The industry is traditionally oriented towards C++ and imperative / OO languages, but there's a lot of potential for functional languages in the \"embedded scripting\" area. In his 2013 QuakeCon keynote [1], John Carmack talks a bit about his experience with functional languages, and how, for example, Scheme could be an ideal candidate as an embedded scripting language. As far as smaller teams are concerned, and especially indie development, there is a lot of potential for OCaml, Clojure and other functional languages. Many mobile and indie dev teams already use a heck lot of C#, Python, Java and other specialized tools like Haxe, ActionScript. But all these languages have well liked and mature frameworks geared towards game development (XNA / MonoGame, PyGame, OpenFL, libgdx, ...), which is maybe what Clojure and OCaml are missing right now. [1] [VIDEO]"}],"r":[["Z4oYSByyRak",1],["Dp-mQ3HxgDE",1],["3LopI4YeC4I",1]]},"9CO6M2HsoIA":{"wy":"HN invokes it as the standard reference whenever drone swarms, autonomous weapons or military AI come up in the news.","q":{"x":"see Stuart Russell's compelling short film","i":22051864,"y":"2020"},"m":[{"id":17689486,"author":"malloryerik","month":"2018-08","story":"Venezuela President Maduro 'survives drone attack'","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Fictional film on automated political violence by drones","quote":"if not, you must see the slaughterbots video.","text":"Can drones be stopped? They have the potential to wage campaigns of automated political violence. I see companies trying to protect against them, like Dedrone [1] -- how effective are these? I imagine most here have seen it, but if not, you must see the slaughterbots video. [2] [1] [LINK] [2] [VIDEO]"},{"id":25471895,"author":"jtsiskin","month":"2020-12","story":"A swarm of drones flying through heavy forest–while staying in formation","stance":"rec","why":"What happens when anyone can build killer drone swarms","quote":"This brings to mind a great video which I've posted here a few times","text":"This brings to mind a great video which I've posted here a few times: [VIDEO] What happens when technology progresses so much that anyone can make a swarm of killer drones? Will politicians no longer be able to go out in public? What about a distant future, when enriching uranium can be done in your garage for cheap..."},{"id":22051864,"author":"lwneal","month":"2020-01","story":"How the U.S. military thinks about AI [audio]","stance":"rec","why":"Short film on machines killing without attribution","quote":"see Stuart Russell's compelling short film","text":"The substantive part of this conversation comes about 35 minutes in, when Allen describes the DoD's five AI ethics principles: that any deployed AI systems must be \"Responsible, Equitable, Traceable, Reliable, and Governable.\" [1]. I think \"Responsible\" raises some hard questions. Today, any weapons use by a robot might require a responsible human operator to first (remotely) pull the trigger. But what if an adversary allows their robots to fire without waiting for human permission? The first side to do so would gain a critical advantage, and we cannot have an autonomy gap. Some have raised conerns that in the extreme case, this could lead to a world of machines anonymously killing without attribution. If you haven't, see Stuart Russell's compelling short film \"Slaughterbots\" [2]. [1] [LINK] [2] [VIDEO]"},{"id":18363664,"author":"bicubic","month":"2018-11","story":"Fleets of drones could aid searches for lost hikers","stance":"rec","why":"Shows what refining existing drone technologies could achieve","quote":"Slaughterbots [0] is a pretty good look at what can be achieved by refining and integrating technologies","text":"The tech required for search and rescue is the same tech required for search and destroy. We seem to be running headfirst into the autonomous killer bot future without giving so much as a second look at the implications. Slaughterbots [0] is a pretty good look at what can be achieved by refining and integrating technologies which all exist today. [0] [VIDEO]"},{"id":47180953,"author":"boilerupnc","month":"2026-02","story":"Google Workers Seek 'Red Lines' on Military A.I., Echoing Anthropic","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"Reminds me of the slaughterbot video","text":"Related: Reminds me of the slaughterbot video [0] and the Hated in the Nation Dark Mirror Episode [1][2] [0] [VIDEO] [1] [LINK] [2] [LINK]"},{"id":44534556,"author":"kragen","month":"2025-07","story":"Solar power has begun to transform the world’s energy system","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"","text":"I don't think any of tensions, classical conflicts, asymmetric conflicts, the amount of people with nothing left to \"loose\", or all of these together, are at particularly unusual levels compared to the previous 6000 years, though they're higher than they were 20 years ago. But, for most of that time, warfare was geographically localized; you could avoid directly experiencing warfare by not being a soldier and living inside a country that wasn't being actively invaded, or on the national border between two countries that weren't at war with each other or being actively invaded. Sometimes that was easier said than done, but most people managed it most of the time. In the Drone Age, though, you can remotely pilot a quadcopter 4300 km away to blow up airplanes on the airstrip: [LINK] — or to blow up your political opposition, if you can guess where they are or will be. The US has been doing this for 20 years in Afghanistan and Iraq: [LINK] (use Readability mode to bypass \"this is not a paywall\") but a General Atomics MQ-9 Reaper costs on the order of US$100 million, so there are less than 400 of them [LINK] while Ukraine's most popular drones cost on the order of US$300, can be 3-D printed in a basement, already cause 70% of casualties on the battlefield, and are produced in volumes approaching 10 million per year [LINK] . Many experts believe drones have made tanks obsolete on the battlefield [LINK] . Even without autonomous weapons, we're rapidly moving toward the future of bor"},{"id":43426719,"author":"autoexec","month":"2025-03","story":"Low-Cost Drone Add-Ons from China Let Anyone Turn Toys into Weapons of War","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"It's a shame that Slaughterbots","text":"It's a shame that Slaughterbots ( [VIDEO] ) didn't have the intended impact and shortly after the video was released it seems that autonomous drones were used to hunt down humans Libya."},{"id":35864156,"author":"marojejian","month":"2023-05","story":"Homemade, Cheap and Lethal, Attack Drones Are Vital to Ukraine","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"obligatory Slaughterbots reference","text":"It's eery to see reality slowly resemble science fiction of the past. obligatory Slaughterbots reference: [VIDEO] Also scary how much of this war you can watch with a POV. UkraineWarVideoReport is full of videos where you can see the drones and humans in action [LINK]"},{"id":36008707,"author":"dimmke","month":"2023-05","story":"US Air Force Shoots Down Drone Swarm with THOR Microwave Weapon","stance":"reference","why":"Fictional drone swarm scenario with facial recognition targeting","quote":"I remember the first time I learned about drone swarms was this YouTube video called Slaughterbots","text":"I remember the first time I learned about drone swarms was this YouTube video called Slaughterbots: [VIDEO] Of course at this point, my country had been using drones to kill people since I was a small child, which I think is ironic. In the Slaughterbots scenario, each specific drone has sensors and AI good enough it can pilot itself and doesn’t need an external connection that can be detected or disrupted and is good enough to target individual people with facial recognition. I believe (this is not an area of expertise for me) that the drones depicted in that video are still far more advanced than what currently exists currently (but I think it could probably get there within the next 10-20 years), whereas the video linked shows a more realistic drone swarm attack where it’s targeting infrastructure and the blast radius is larger than being able to target an individual's head, but still smaller than traditional UAVs. It’s an upsetting watch, but the part of me that enjoys speculative science fiction (I love evaluating how realistic the apocalypse is in zombie movies) always found the attempted countermeasures employed to be silly and unrealistic. Covering windows and leaving only tiny circles so no drone can get through etc… But at the same time, the video struck a chord because as soon as I saw it, I knew some form of this was coming and likely within the next decade. But mitigations and countermeasures are being developed in tandem. I think that's the major thing the Slaugh"},{"id":29608751,"author":"PontifexMinimus","month":"2021-12","story":"U.N. talks adjourn without deal to regulate 'killer robots'","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"I imagine most people here have already seen the Slaughterbots video","text":"> Killer robots will be a great equalizer in the ability to create a nightmare hellscape I imagine most people here have already seen the Slaughterbots video: [VIDEO] A more conventional type of attack would be using large numbers cheap cruise missiles. If you can make them for $20k each then $1bn would buy you 50,000 of them, which is probably a good deal more than the number of anti-aircraft missiles your adversary has, and certainly a lot more than their number of fighter aircraft. Therefore firing off a lot in one go would overload their defences and many would get through. If aimed at infrastructure target, they could cripple a country's economy, and how soon after that do people start starving?"},{"id":29610260,"author":"sen","month":"2021-12","story":"U.N. talks adjourn without deal to regulate 'killer robots'","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"It'd be better to link to the source[0], that's a content-stealing channel you linked to.","text":"It'd be better to link to the source[0], that's a content-stealing channel you linked to. [0] [VIDEO]"},{"id":28794534,"author":"deadbolt","month":"2021-10","story":"Autonomous drone navigates independently through the forest at 40 km/h","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"It's the first thing I think of whenever I watch one of these videos of new drone tech.","text":"This video was posted to HN a couple years ago. It's the first thing I think of whenever I watch one of these videos of new drone tech. [VIDEO] [LINK]"},{"id":28794557,"author":"mLuby","month":"2021-10","story":"Autonomous drone navigates independently through the forest at 40 km/h","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"","text":"There's so-called \"nuclear breakout time\" for how quickly a non-nuclear nation could create nuclear weapons.[1] Time for a similar \"slaughterbot breakout time.\"[2] However unlike with nuclear weapons, this is something a small team or even individual could soon achieve and then hold as a deterrent or use for sociopolitical aims.[3] It's easy to imagine an arms race between drones and the tiny point defense turrets (likely lasers, maybe kinetics or even EMPs) trying to disable them, both systems operating far faster than humans can react. In a strange way, wars fought like this may involve less loss of life, with soldiers surrendering to a cloud of slaughterbots that destroyed the automated defenses in seconds. This forthcoming ability to intimidate and harm precisely, anonymously, robotically, and at scale makes autocracy more likely and more durable. [1]: [LINK] [2]: \"Slaughterbots TED talk.\" [VIDEO] [3]: \"Hated in the Nation\" Black Mirror Season 3 Episode 6 (Netflix)"},{"id":26457216,"author":"scrollaway","month":"2021-03","story":"Israel's autonomous 'robo-snipers' and suicide drones raise ethical dilemma","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"","text":"Slaughterbots come to anyone's mind? For those that haven't seen it, it's on youtube. Wikipedia: [LINK] Video: [VIDEO]"},{"id":25216552,"author":"safog","month":"2020-11","story":"How once-elusive laser weapons suddenly became a reality","stance":"reference","why":"Fictional drone swarm film cited alongside real drone warfare","quote":"","text":"That link out to the China drone swarm video is pretty crazy. I hope we do live in boring times and don't have to deal with drone warfare wherever we live. [LINK] Drones in the recent war between Armenia and Azerbaijan. Some of the same Chinese suicide drones being used. [LINK] Slaughterbots: [VIDEO]"},{"id":23574736,"author":"EGreg","month":"2020-06","story":"Turkey Now Has Swarming Suicide Drones It Could Export","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"","text":"Swarming drones and AI is the scariest tech and we are going to see the consequences. Because it will be so cheap and ubiquitous. This isn’t even the atomic bomb — where the work was just too sweet to stop. This is basic technology with small incremental improvements tons of people can do. Drones are the first robots that are among us (overhead). Self-driving cars are at least very expensive to produce. But drones can be mass-produced and deployed anonymously. Drones with live munitions is terrifying. Imagine an anonymous swarm descending on Union Square in SF. Even under normal operation, drones can fall and hit someone by accident. Let alone a swarm. This is one technology I should not want to be in anyone’s hands, but if it is, it should be in the hands of the State and not everyone. Just like bombs. PS: With cheap cameras being ubiquitous and people can be identified by their gait, heartbeat etc. consumer level tech can also include an AI that can cross correlate everywhere you’ve been. Businesses would use it to fine you when you park your car for too long and don’t go into the store. But governments and police departments can concoct tons of believable stories with parallel construction and put anyone they want away. In fact AI can also be adopted in other places in the policing and judicial process (as with Palantir) leading to a Kafkaesque experience for sentencing. And with deepfakes it’s worse than that. Anyone could create a believable video of a rape-caught-on-vid"},{"id":20294878,"author":"freeflight","month":"2019-06","story":"Pentagon has a laser that can identify people at a distance by their heartbeat","stance":"reference","why":"Presentation of drone assassination scenario","quote":"Reminds me of this little presentation [0]","text":"> It was being pitched as the ideal way to assassinate people in a crowd - identify them by heartbeat signature, fly a drone in to drop a small explosive package on their heads. Reminds me of this little presentation [0] [0] [VIDEO]"},{"id":17693001,"author":"goldenkey","month":"2018-08","story":"Traceability on the Internet","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"","text":"I never realized license plates were able to be utilized by a well funded private data aggregation operation. Great observation. How far do we have to go with allowances for information and actions that are taunts for death and destruction? It seems to me that the only fair way to shape society and well..shared reality..is to allow everyone to do everything and anything and allow others who feel strongly to enact responses..ie punishments. This will eventually maybe be the case ala Star Wars, independent agents roaming reality, with no law other than consequence by others. It seems this is untenable too because it evolves into unfair reality, biased on wealth and capital of weaponry and defense. Not only physically but on a knowledge and information and technological basis. Isnt the real underlying archtype of license plates and well..any authoritarian laws by the government a sort of protectionism for those of us without the money for defense and offense of our own free persons? [1] [VIDEO] I am libertarian..but freedom in reality is a function of resources. So I cannot square this circle... laws are a hard issue."},{"id":17259377,"author":"MarkMMullin","month":"2018-06","story":"AI at Google: our principles","stance":"reference","why":"Illustrates the danger of autonomous killing machines","quote":"","text":"We only call it AI - creating an autonomous ML system that is designed to kill anyone can lead to us all being killed by math and plastic - it's a singularly bad idea, so much so there is no scoring mechanism that justifies it - too much chance we end up with this -- [VIDEO]"},{"id":40990426,"author":"throwup238","month":"2024-07","story":"Researchers build a solar-powered hovering drone that weighs only 9 mg","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"Slaughterbots, assemble!","text":"Every time I see one of these drone tech demonstrations, I can't help but wonder how they will be adapted for warfare. This one... I'm seeing flying solar powered landmines (skymines?) that can flutter around an active combat zone aimlessly for hours until it detects a personnel target and bops them near the temple with a small round. I wonder if there are any actual benefits over just putting a solar panel on a regular quadcopter and having them charge a battery, maybe staying stationary in field or ditch near the road, relocating once in a while and waiting for a target. Slaughterbots, assemble! [VIDEO]"}]},"RmIgJ64z6Y4":{"wy":"HN returns to it whenever corporations as paperclip-maximizer AIs or AI risk come up.","q":{"x":"I cannot recommend enough Charles Stross' 34C3 talk on this very topic.","i":22239268,"y":"2020"},"m":[{"id":22239268,"author":"aaron-santos","month":"2020-02","story":"AI thinks like a corporation (2018)","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Stross's talk on corporations as slow AIs","quote":"I cannot recommend enough Charles Stross' 34C3 talk on this very topic.","text":"For another take on this corporations ≈ ai idea, I cannot recommend enough Charles Stross' 34C3 talk on this very topic. [VIDEO] (picks up approx 10mins in)."},{"id":37670939,"author":"btschaegg","month":"2023-09","story":"Rethinking the Luddites in the Age of A.I","stance":"rec","why":"Stross links corporations and AI risk in one talk","quote":"Obligatory mention of Charles Stross' talk where he knits together exactly those two thoughts.","text":"Obligatory mention of Charles Stross' talk where he knits together exactly those two thoughts. \"Dude, you broke the future\": [VIDEO]"},{"id":18629979,"author":"vertexFarm","month":"2018-12","story":"Facial recognition: It’s time for action","stance":"rec","why":"Stross on regulatory lag versus accelerating tech growth","quote":"Check out Charles Stross's speech at C3 about regulatory lag relative to the the accelerated nature of tech growth","text":"You could say the same about a credit card skimmer on an ATM. It's passive, right? It's just a sensor that sits there and absorbs people's data. I don't think this argument makes a lot of sense. The point is, there needs to be some law and order in place so that when people abuse this tech to harm people and society and get caught, there's some precedent to stop them and punish them. It doesn't matter if the technology is passive. The intent and action to use it to harm people are not passive and are not analogous to thought crime at all. There is dire need for regulation with a lot of emerging technologies right now. We're building systems with enormous power which can break human society if misused. I think the intangibility and \"passivity\" of this tech (or at least how it is perceived) gives us a very false sense of security. Like how a few decades ago very few average people could understand how the internet might have a great impact on society. Obviously they aren't thinking that way anymore. Check out Charles Stross's speech at C3 about regulatory lag relative to the the accelerated nature of tech growth: [VIDEO]"},{"id":16051554,"author":"leipert","month":"2018-01","story":"Dude, you broke the future","stance":"rec","why":"Stross reading his 34C3 talk on corporations and technology","quote":"Charles Stross read the text at the 34C3. If you want to listen to it rather than read it","text":"Charles Stross read the text at the 34C3. If you want to listen to it rather than read it: [1]: [VIDEO] [2]: [LINK] EDIT: Sorry, the read mode on mobile did not show the embedded video."},{"id":39676039,"author":"stana","month":"2024-03","story":"Among the A.I. doomsayers","stance":"reference","why":"Introduced corporations as paperclip maximiser AIs","quote":"Talk that introduced me to this idea of corporations as Paperclip Maximiser AI","text":"Talk that introduced me to this idea of corporations as Paperclip Maximiser AI - \"Dude you broke the Future\" [VIDEO]"},{"id":23817627,"author":"aaron695","month":"2020-07","story":"Dude, You Broke the Future (2017)","stance":"reference","why":"Q&A where Stross confirms he means corporations literally are AIs","quote":"In the video questions section he confirms he means literal.","text":"> I don't think the author literally believe corporations are AGIs In the video questions section he confirms he means literal. [VIDEO] Look at HN as an AI, you can ask a very complex question and get a very complex answer, it's AGI. 4chan can send humans out to do its real world physical bidding. It can literally get people shot. These are very complex AI's."},{"id":20287255,"author":"grumdan","month":"2019-06","story":"Consumers Are Becoming Wise to Your Nudge","stance":"reference","why":"Corporations as human-powered AIs maximizing profit","quote":"he described corporations and competition between them as \"human-powered AIs\" that maximize profit as their only goal","text":"This reminds me a lot of Charles Stross' talk at 34C3, where he described corporations and competition between them as \"human-powered AIs\" that maximize profit as their only goal, similar to how a neural network only blindly minimizes a loss function. [VIDEO]"}],"r":[["ezVk1ahRF78",1],["2LyzBoHo5EI",1],["2wZ1pCpJUIM",1],["AGCmPLWZKd8",1],["9WcHsT97suU",1]]},"aDejwCGdUV8":{"wy":"HN people point to it as an entry point to the Hancock and Carlson lost-civilization theories, while noting the ideas are controversial.","q":{"x":"Here's where to go to get on the Graham Hancock/Randall Carlson train","i":23676189,"y":"2020"},"m":[{"id":19800010,"author":"LinuxBender","month":"2019-05","story":"Questions About Food and Climate Change, Answered","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Long discussion giving perspective on climate and earth history","quote":"a mind blowing discussion about climate (and the earth in general)","text":"For those that would like to hear a mind blowing discussion about climate (and the earth in general), watch all of the podcasts with Joe Rogan, Randall Carlson and Graham Hancock. They are three hours each and I promise you will learn many things that give perspective on the repeat arguments and confusion about the climate. There are many other podcasts with these gentlemen, but here are three to start with. Joe Rogan and Randall Carlson #606 (3 hours) [1] Joe Rogan, Graham Hancock and Randall Carlson #725 [2] Joe Rogan, Randall Carlson #1284 [3] [1] - [LINK] [2] - [VIDEO] [3] - [LINK]"},{"id":24207843,"author":"seesawtron","month":"2020-08","story":"Lost Advanced Civilizations","stance":"rec","why":"Hancock on Bronze Age China and Gobekli Tepe","quote":"Have you guys checked out the episode on JRE with Graham Hancock?","text":"Have you guys checked out the episode on JRE with Graham Hancock? [0] They talk about the Bronze Age China and Göbekli Tepe. At the time I was seemingly convinced why the academic community wouldn't let these new theories flourish despite the archeological evidence. I was not aware of the work by Hanson so I can not comment on that. A similar thread on HN in the past [1]. [0] [VIDEO] [1] [LINK]"},{"id":23676189,"author":"fit2rule","month":"2020-06","story":"Silurian Hypothesis: Were There Civilizations on Earth Before Humans? (2018)","stance":"rec","why":"Entry point to Hancock and Carlson's lost civilization theories","quote":"Here's where to go to get on the Graham Hancock/Randall Carlson train","text":"Look, there are all sorts of schools of thought on how these things were built, but none of them were conclusive. I'm not saying it was rock-melting alien technology, but it was clearly some form of advanced technology we are unable to duplicate or conceive was available to the builders. Here's where to go to get on the Graham Hancock/Randall Carlson train, in case you haven't seen it: [VIDEO] Some interesting observations which are counter to the narrative: [LINK]"},{"id":10969009,"author":"pgtruesdell","month":"2016-01","story":"Atlantis-style myths that turned out to be true","stance":"rec","why":"Hancock and Carlson on Atlantis and Younger Dryas theory","quote":"The most recent episode with both Randall and Graham was especially intriguing","text":"Randall Carlson and Graham Hancock have been on the Joe Rogan Experience a few times over the years, together and separately, and have explained their well documented theory about Atlantis. The short of it would be, before the Younger Dryas period when the massive ice sheets over North America melted, the ocean levels were approximately 300 ft lower than they are today. When you look at the mid-atlantic ridge, the lower sea levels would expose an area now called the Azores Plateau. This would be a large habitable island off the coast of Europe. This becomes especially interesting when you take into account the timeline of Plato's account which exactly lines up with the beginning of the Younger Dryas meltdown. I apologize if my interpretation of their discussions is inaccurate in any way, I am now just digging into this theory and it's truly fascinating. The most recent episode with both Randall and Graham was especially intriguing, since they both brought their unique studies together into a more unified theory. [VIDEO]"},{"id":24229782,"author":"ryansmccoy","month":"2020-08","story":"The Kush civilization flourished in Sudan nearly 5K years ago","stance":"reference","why":"Lost civilizations and an asteroid in 10,000 BC, with controversy","quote":"Sounded like there is some controversy around it though, so take it however you'd like.","text":"Joe Rogan had some interesting guys, Graham Hancock & Randall Carlson, that talked about lost civilizations due to asteroid in 10,000 BC. Sounded like there is some controversy around it though, so take it however you'd like. [VIDEO]"},{"id":10800163,"author":"logn","month":"2015-12","story":"Ask HN: What are your predictions in technology for 2016?","stance":"reference","why":"Podcast on a cataclysmic asteroid strike 12K years ago","quote":"there's evidence for a cataclysmic asteroid strike 12K years ago that nearly wiped out humanity","text":"Ha, definitely a long shot. I was watching a Joe Rogan podcast: [VIDEO] . In summary, there's evidence for a cataclysmic asteroid strike 12K years ago that nearly wiped out humanity. If true that upends traditional archaeological theories and suggests that humanity rebooted 12K years ago instead of a gradual move from hunter-gatherer to society. What the world was like before the reboot is unknown, but maybe we were advanced enough for space travel?"}],"r":[["zz4YVJ4aRfg",1],["06e-PwhmSq8",1],["-f-CFQxaUY4",1],["sWN13pKVp9s",1],["XEVlyP4_11M",1],["9nXop2lLDa4",1],["9W74aeuqsiU",1],["eJ3RzGoQC4s",1],["xc8epam4NyY",1],["G0Cp7DrvNLQ",1]]},"DoeNbZlxfUM":{"wy":"Strongly recommended as a formative talk on privacy, especially for its argument that voting with your feet fails as a privacy strategy.","q":{"x":"It was formative in my understanding of privacy and security.","i":29851010,"y":"2022"},"m":[{"id":29851010,"author":"losvedir","month":"2022-01","story":"My First Impressions of Web3","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Formative talk on privacy and security.","quote":"It was formative in my understanding of privacy and security.","text":"I am (or was?) a huge Moxie Marlinspike fan, and highly recommend this video[0] of his from Defcon several years ago. It was formative in my understanding of privacy and security. That said, something here really doesn't add up. Being a huge fan, I took note several years ago of MobileCoin, a cryptocurrency, which listed him on the home page as one of the team.[1] Or, see this Wired article about it[2]. The big selling point, as I remember it, of MobileCoin (per the name) was that it was actually feasible for small clients (i.e. phones) to meaningfully take part in the network. But he's since been scrubbed from the site, as far as I can tell. MobileCoin was added to Signal, much to the chagrin of HN. And Signal is intimately related to Moxie's work. I had thought that if MobileCoin becomes a thing, then the holders of the originally mined coin would become pretty rich, and I assume that would include Moxie. So I'm a little confused by how this post fits in. I infer from it that he's new to web3 and crypto in general, but it feels like this isn't the case. (Though \"web3\" is ambiguous, and I suppose he's referring generally to Ethereum and dApps.) But his main point seems to be that the dominant cryptocurrency isn't suitable for involving light clients, which was the main selling point of MobileCoin. I just wish it were clear his involvement with MobileCoin, since it feels to me like that could be a pretty significant conflict of interest with regard to Ethereum investigated he"},{"id":14404911,"author":"CodeMage","month":"2017-05","story":"The Blockstack Browser: A Gateway to a New, Decentralized Internet","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Explains why voting with your feet fails for privacy.","quote":"I highly recommend watching it","text":"Four years ago, Moxie Marlinspike offered a good explanation of why this \"vote with your feet\" approach doesn't really work, at DEFCON 18. His talk is called \"Changing Threats to Privacy\" and I highly recommend watching it: [VIDEO] If you're impatient, you can skip the first 10 minutes and 40 seconds: [VIDEO]"},{"id":20192905,"author":"karangoeluw","month":"2019-06","story":"Facebook lawyers explain to a judge that privacy on Facebook is nonexistent","stance":"rec","why":"Argument against voting with your feet for privacy.","quote":"Recommend watching the whole talk.","text":"Moxie Marlinespike (Openwhisper Systems, Signal) makes a good argument for why that doesn't work [VIDEO] . Recommend watching the whole talk."}],"r":[["eG0KrT6pBPk",2],["6h3RJhoqgK8",1],["0rR9IaXH1M0",1]]},"G0ZZJXw4MTA":{"wy":"HN commenters bring it up whenever a poll or survey result looks suspect, as the classic illustration of biased question framing.","q":{"x":"An excellent illustration of this","i":24281252,"y":"2020"},"m":[{"id":42105440,"author":"graemep","month":"2024-11","story":"Statistical challenges and misreadings of literature create unreplicable science [pdf]","stance":"rec","why":"","quote":"A humorous take on this I love","text":"Polls may or may not be carried out in a neutral way. A good many are deliberately biased, even more have known flaws that are glossed over. A humorous take on this I love (from the 80s BBC comedy Yes Minister, which is often scarily realistic): [VIDEO]"},{"id":34940310,"author":"roenxi","month":"2023-02","story":"We are dropping the Dilbert comic because of creator Scott Adams racist rant","stance":"rec","why":"","quote":"I recall an excellent segment on Yes, Minister that explains it","text":"This is probably a good time to recall that polling in politics can be highly misleading . I recall an excellent segment on Yes, Minister that explains it [0, 1]. We really need to know the other questions they were being asked at the time to see if they were primed in some way. A near majority being uncomfortable with white people would be outrageous, but Americans are usually pretty tolerant so it is likely that Rasmussen is playing games here. [0] [VIDEO] [1] [LINK] - For people who like text, search for \"Have another opinion poll done\""},{"id":30656184,"author":"Jalad","month":"2022-03","story":"Walgreens replaced some fridge doors with screens, and some shoppers hate it","stance":"rec","why":"","quote":"One of my favorite shows","text":"Link for the curious: [VIDEO] Starts at about 1:00. One of my favorite shows Edit: updated link, thanks @dvngnt_"},{"id":24281252,"author":"waterhouse","month":"2020-08","story":"Gallup: 81% of Black Americans Want Police to Retain Local Presence","stance":"rec","why":"","quote":"An excellent illustration of this","text":"> the questions can shape the response. An excellent illustration of this: [VIDEO] Although in this case, it doesn't look like they had bad intentions and they seem to have dug at least a little below the surface and drawn what seem like sensible conclusions. I wonder if more detailed results and the full list of questions are available somewhere."},{"id":45432347,"author":"agedclock","month":"2025-09","story":"Imgur pulls out of UK as data watchdog threatens fine","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"This was of course satirised by Yes Minister.","text":"You are probably talking about the YouGov poll. The poll asked a clearly leading question IMO. You can get any result you want by asking leading questions on polling. This was of course satirised by Yes Minister. [VIDEO] I can counter any of the iffy polls by simple point to the official online petitions service. There were a huge number of signatures to revoke OSA and two million signatures to abolish the plans for the Digital ID. While the Digital ID is technically a separate issue, many of the same privacy concerns are present. [LINK] [LINK] The number of people that signed these petitions is far more representative than any polling. On top of that, recently I've seen reportrs of both the Liberal Democrats and Reform (the two largest parties after the main two) recongising the OSA as unpopular and are likely to suggest reforming/removing it. On top of that. The labour government and the conservative government that proceeded it which created the OSA were/are both deeply unpopular. So any notion that there is a popular mandate for this is nonsense."},{"id":36874783,"author":"Shrezzing","month":"2023-07","story":"Launch HN: Roundtable (YC S23) – Using AI to Simulate Surveys","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"See Yes Minister's[1] explanation for how they can work.","text":"The tool would be useful as a QA step to test for leading questions in survey design. See Yes Minister's[1] explanation for how they can work. A simulation to see if the questions get the same response irrespective of the order they were asked in could improve survey quality. Obviously, the tool could be used in the opposite way too, to help design surveys that say exactly what the company/govt/charity wants it to. [1] [VIDEO]"},{"id":31867440,"author":"laverya","month":"2022-06","story":"Supreme Court Overturns Roe vs. Wade","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"","text":"I'm saying that polls can be twisted however you want, if you're willing to put in the effort. [0] How the question is framed is a large part of the effort, and what the quoted doctor was complaining about. (Specifically, he was complaining that things were not sufficiently twisted in the direction they found favorable) Disaggregating the question based on trimesters is one form of twisting. Asking only a single blanket \"are you for or against the right of a woman to choose\" is also twisting. 0: [VIDEO]"},{"id":28591272,"author":"einpoklum","month":"2021-09","story":"Germany’s no-emotion voting guide surges despite campaign of personalities","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"","text":"Can someone post an English translation of the \"theses\" or questions in the Wahl-o-mat? When I hear about such initiatives I am always wary of this kind of polling manipulation: [VIDEO]"},{"id":28140268,"author":"iso1210","month":"2021-08","story":"New EU Law Removes Digital Privacy","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"as shown in this 'documentary'","text":"\"We asked 100 people who were interested enough to write to their MEP about digital privacy and 80% of them said digital privacy was important\" People who are motivated enough to know and comment about these proposals are not a representitive sample of the population. If you were to stand outside Old Trafford and ask people who the best football team in the UK is, you'll get the vast majority saying Manchester United. Do the same thing in London and you'll get people saying it's Arsenal. Aside from both being wrong, you can see the bias. Accredited polling companies adjust results to minimise sample bias, but even then have to be careful to avoid leading questions as shown in this 'documentary': [VIDEO]"},{"id":27068544,"author":"notSupplied","month":"2021-05","story":"How Basecamp Blew Up","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"","text":"The most typical reason people don't like to answer that question is because they believe it is as setup for a followup: \"Then why aren't you doing X?!\" where X in this case could be \"Fire this person\", \"Setup a committee overseeing all hires\", \"Commit to quotas\", \"Donate money to this cause.\" Also reminds me of this \"Yes Prime Minister\" skit about leading questions: [VIDEO]"},{"id":24894447,"author":"pjc50","month":"2020-10","story":"‘Culture wars’ are fought by tiny minority – UK study","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"","text":"Yes. The culture war is to a great extent a US import. The US is extremely polarised, possibly uniquely so for a country with a single national language and mostly-shared ethnic background (compare Belgium, which is highly polarised but along language lines). The UK has its own natural fragmentation lines (north/south, class system, four nations, catholic/protestant) but those are not the lines on which the culture war is run. While there has been incursion of talk radio (LBC/Farage), there is no counterpart to the hyper-polarised TV of Fox News yet. Sky News is comparatively normal. The right-wing disinformation comes in via the press and various \"client journalists\" who repeat things they've heard from \"Downing street sources\" who they refuse to hold accountable. > It found that most voters balanced competing political concerns and ideas. Its polling found that 73% believe hate speech is a problem, while 72% believe political correctness is an issue. Some 60% believe many are too sensitive about race, but 60% also recognise issues around “white privilege”. This is just the combination of leading questions and people responding to words without actually thinking about the underlying concepts. See, always, Yes Minister: [VIDEO]"},{"id":24280686,"author":"iso1210","month":"2020-08","story":"Gallup: 81% of Black Americans Want Police to Retain Local Presence","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"as explained by Yes Prime Minister","text":"Assuming it's similar polling to the UK there are all sorts of weighting and balancing to get truly representative samples within a margin of error. Where the system is weakest is in the questions asked, as explained by Yes Prime Minister [VIDEO] I can't see the tables gallup produced to make their claim, without those I can't see why it's of any interest."},{"id":22532864,"author":"waterhouse","month":"2020-03","story":"Surge of virus misinformation stumps Facebook and Twitter","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"Good lord, it really is like the","text":"I was finally able to get access to the full questions asked in the poll. These make clear that the survey was a fishing expedition designed to elicit viral stats. The questions asked in the poll include “Is Corona related to the coronavirus?” and “In light of the coronavirus, do you plan to stop drinking Corona?” Those presumably didn't get interesting answers, so they omitted them from the report, and instead published on the question of whether people \"would not buy Corona under any circumstances now\" and made it sound like the first questions. Good lord, it really is like the \"Yes, Prime Minister\" clip: [VIDEO]"},{"id":18666261,"author":"mrec","month":"2018-12","story":"The Brexit Short: How Hedge Funds Used Private Polls to Make Millions","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"The classic example is","text":"Polls aren't always about matching reality. They're often used as ammunition in an argument - \"Look, the majority supports my position!\" The classic example is [VIDEO]"},{"id":15661804,"author":"tareqak","month":"2017-11","story":"How my third email reminder from HR motivated me to launch my startup","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"has a comment with the transscript of the skit","text":"Taking that humorous example further, you could go down both questions, still have a have a final \"Yes\" to both questions, but then have a final question to ask which one of the two a particular voter is worried about. e.g. [VIDEO] has a comment with the transscript of the skit HUMPHREY: No. So she starts asking you some questions. Mr Woolley, are you worried about the number of young people without jobs? BERNARD: Yes. HUMPHREY: Are you worried about the rise in crime among teenagers? BERNARD: Yes. HUMPHREY: Do you think there's a lack of discipline in our comprehensive schools?] BERNARD: Yes. HUMPHREY: Do you think young people welcome some authority and leadership in their lives? BERNARD: Yes. HUMPHREY: Do you think they respond to a challenge? BERNARD: Yes. HUMPHREY: Would you be in favour of reintroducing national service? BERNARD: ...Oh. Well, I suppose I might be. HUMPHREY: Yes or no? BERNARD: Yes. ... HUMPHREY: Mr Woolley, are you worried about the danger of war? BERNARD: Yes. HUMPHREY: Are you worried about the growth of armaments? BERNARD: Yes. HUMPHREY: Do you think there's a danger in giving young people guns and teaching them how to kill? BERNARD: Yes. HUMPHREY: Do you think it's wrong to force people to take up arms against their will? BERNARD: Yes. HUMPHREY: Would you oppose the reintroduction of national service? BERNARD: Yes. ... FINAL QUESTION: Are you more worried about the rise in crime among teenagers or about the growth of armaments? POTENTIAL FINAL ANSWE"},{"id":7929179,"author":"gopalv","month":"2014-06","story":"Social Psychology Is A Flamethrower","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"I always fall back onto the Survey from","text":"The issue with psychology is that double blind experiments are prone to observer effect. The behaviour often have nothing to do with reality - when measured, everything tends towards the ideal. And the experiments can often result in the results you want if the double blinds are removed. I always fall back onto the Survey from \"Yes Minister\" to explain how that works [VIDEO]"},{"id":6025189,"author":"chriswarbo","month":"2013-07","story":"Poll shift as public now says War on Terror has gone too far","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"brings to mind this scene from Yes Minister","text":"\"voters say 45 - 40 percent the government's anti-terrorism efforts go too far restricting civil liberties, a reversal from a January 10, 2010, ... when voters said 63 - 25 percent that such activities didn't go far enough to adequately protect the country.\" The fact that the statement has reversed (\"not far enough\" -> \"too far\") rather than the numbers (ie. it's not \"63 - 25\" -> \"40 -> 45\") brings to mind this scene from Yes Minister [VIDEO]"},{"id":4547294,"author":"jacques_chester","month":"2012-09","story":"How to confuse a moral compass: Survey 'magic trick' causes attitude reversal","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"classic phenomenon explored in Yes, Prime Minister","text":"This is different from the classic phenomenon explored in Yes, Prime Minister [1]. It's not that they manipulated the questions in advance -- it's that they reversed the meaning of the questions after the respondent had answered them . Even under these conditions, the majority of respondents were then prepared to support the position which they had, ostensibly, only a few minutes ago opposed and vice versa . Remarkable. [1] [VIDEO]"},{"id":45287784,"author":"Kim_Bruning","month":"2025-09","story":"EU Chat Control: Germany's position has been reverted to undecided","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"","text":"It depends on how you word the survey. ;-) [VIDEO] (Yes Minster: the unofficial video on-boarding course for UK bureaucrats)"},{"id":26902742,"author":"webmaven","month":"2021-04","story":"The real reason to end the death penalty","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"Obligatory 'Yes, Prime Minister' clip","text":"> As ever with polls it depends how you frame the question. Obligatory 'Yes, Prime Minister' clip: [VIDEO]"}]},"NMvwHDFVpCE":{"wy":"HN recommends it as a comprehensive, still-accurate and often funny account of how zoning and policy force suburban sprawl.","q":{"x":"His talk from 30 years ago is still so eerily precise and comprehensive","i":13669670,"y":"2017"},"m":[{"id":40381140,"author":"tmnvix","month":"2024-05","story":"Measuring the impact of zoning on housing in a city","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Hilarious 90s planning talk, a favourite YouTube video","quote":"One of my favourite videos on youtube and quite hilarious at times","text":"> I don’t know what the answer is but Parking Reform, Strong Towns and Not Just Bikes are my north stars on this stuff. Do you have any others? I think you might like this planning talk given in the 90s. One of my favourite videos on youtube and quite hilarious at times: [VIDEO]"},{"id":13669670,"author":"trgn","month":"2017-02","story":"Let’s not demonize driving, just stop subsidizing it","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Duany's comprehensive talk on sprawl, still precise decades later","quote":"His talk from 30 years ago is still so eerily precise and comprehensive","text":"I basically just parroted Nathan Lewis, who has a great blog series on the topic: [LINK] . It's virtually the same message from Andres Duany. His talk from 30 years ago is still so eerily precise and comprehensive: [VIDEO] . When I feel presumptuous, I email this to people (deaf ears, mostly). Leon Krier bangs the same drum, and adds a more sophisticated, architectural perspective: [LINK] ."},{"id":18307385,"author":"closeparen","month":"2018-10","story":"California Considers Placing a Mileage Tax on Drivers (2017)","stance":"rec","why":"Explains how public policy and zoning force suburban sprawl","quote":"You may find this talk [0], or the speaker's book Suburban Nation, enlightening.","text":"Suburban sprawl is exactly as required by law, from the mutually exclusive land uses right down to the paint on the strip-mall parking lots. Market forces would love to build walkable mixed-use districts. A proud tradition of highly effective grassroots activism makes sure that they don't. You may find this talk [0], or the speaker's book Suburban Nation, enlightening. It's not at all the case that market forces need to be adjusted here. It's public policy which is broken. [0] [VIDEO]"},{"id":30497039,"author":"closeparen","month":"2022-02","story":"SF is latest city to consider tackling its housing crisis by taxing empty homes","stance":"reference","why":"Accompanies argument that sprawl is mandated by codes","quote":"","text":"Suburban sprawl is the law. These subdivisions off the side of the highway full of large lot single family homes aren’t even what developers think people want. They are prescribed by the building and planning codes of nearly every jurisdiction. Developers do sometimes build new places in the urban style but every element of that is a hard-fought battle against the government for variances, zoning changes, special permits, etc. The New Urbanists have an interesting approach to this: “open source” planning codes that prescribe urbanism rather than sprawl, which they try to convince municipalities to adopt as their default. They don’t seem to have much uptake though. The postwar sprawl style is what was popular when the regulatory regimes as we know them today came to be, and there’s a lot of inertia behind that, whether or not people actually like it. [VIDEO]"}],"r":[["LruaD7XhQ50",1],["mPqWstVnRjQ",1],["R3bo-s_OY4Q",1]]},"e7wFotDKEF4":{"wy":"Recommended as a thorough debunking of Koko's supposed language ability in threads about animal communication.","q":{"x":"While this video is long, it explains the controversy very well","i":30975206,"y":"2022"},"m":[{"id":47502794,"author":"gf000","month":"2026-03","story":"Epoch confirms GPT5.4 Pro solved a Frontier Math Open Problem for the first time","stance":"rec","why":"Human language differs from animal communication","quote":"You may want to watch this video","text":"You may want to watch this video: [VIDEO] Human language is way above what communication animals show. We don't really know what's the exact boundary, but again, the difference is significant and not just \"scaled up\"."},{"id":43099599,"author":"dlcarrier","month":"2025-02","story":"Why Teach American Sign Language (ASL) to a Gorilla?","stance":"rec","why":"Critique of Koko claims","quote":"Here's a great video about the extreme cherry picking and lack of transparency","text":"Here's a great video about the extreme cherry picking and lack of transparency it takes to argue that Koko had any better of an understanding of language than a well trained dog: [VIDEO]"},{"id":30975206,"author":"expensive_news","month":"2022-04","story":"What happens in your dog’s brain when you speak","stance":"rec","why":"Explains the Koko sign language controversy","quote":"While this video is long, it explains the controversy very well","text":"I’m skeptical of this. This sounds similar to the way Koko “learned” sign language. While this video is long, it explains the controversy very well: [VIDEO]"},{"id":29036234,"author":"galkk","month":"2021-10","story":"Toki Pona: A Language with a Hundred Words (2015)","stance":"rec","why":"Recent video debunking Koko's language","quote":"recently there was a great video about Koko","text":"The article casually mentions Koko, and it's a bit sad that the story of Koko's \"language\" is still alive... I can not remember book exceprt, but recently there was a great video about Koko [VIDEO]"},{"id":35432624,"author":"Sohcahtoa82","month":"2023-04","story":"If Parrots Can Talk, Why Can’t Monkeys?","stance":"reference","why":"Koko data was cherry-picked","quote":"TL;DW - Data was VERY cherry-picked to make it look like she could communicate via sign language.","text":"> Note that Koko was obviously a greater communicator than any parrot ever was. Sadly, I don't think this is actually true, despite the popular belief. See [VIDEO] TL;DW - Data was VERY cherry-picked to make it look like she could communicate via sign language. At best, she was responding via rote memorization and had no idea what she was actually communicating."}],"r":[["wjZofJX0v4M",1]]},"esQyYGezS7c":{"wy":"Whenever NIST's pricey peanut butter or reference materials come up, commenters point to this as the clear explanation of why they exist.","q":{"x":"Veritasium [1] did a video of exactly this and explains why it exists","i":38017639,"y":"2023"},"m":[{"id":47809433,"author":"prophesi","month":"2026-04","story":"NIST gives up enriching most CVEs","stance":"rec","why":"Explains what reference materials are used for and why they cost so much","quote":"the great Veritasium video[0] going over what these reference materials are used for and why they're so expensive","text":"Assuming this is in reference to the great Veritasium video[0] going over what these reference materials are used for and why they're so expensive. [0] [VIDEO]"},{"id":42842967,"author":"0xC0ncord","month":"2025-01","story":"Going Nuts over NIST’s Standard Reference Peanut Butter (2016)","stance":"rec","why":"Covers NIST reference materials, their history, why they exist, and quirky examples","quote":"Veritasium has a great video[1] on NIST's Standard Reference Materials including their peanut butter.","text":"Veritasium has a great video[1] on NIST's Standard Reference Materials including their peanut butter. It talks about some of the other aspects of NIST's business in selling these items, their history, why they exist, and some of the other interesting ones like their standard reference cigarettes. [1] [VIDEO]"},{"id":38017567,"author":"seritools","month":"2023-10","story":"NIST Standard Peanut Butter – $1,107","stance":"rec","why":"Explains NIST standard peanut butter specifically","quote":"The Veritasium one mentions NIST peanut butter specifically, even.","text":"Probably the one linked above by Tom Scott, or this one by Veritasium: [VIDEO] The Veritasium one mentions NIST peanut butter specifically, even."},{"id":38017639,"author":"V__","month":"2023-10","story":"NIST Standard Peanut Butter – $1,107","stance":"rec","why":"Explains why NIST sells standard reference peanut butter for calibration","quote":"Veritasium [1] did a video of exactly this and explains why it exists","text":"Veritasium [1] did a video of exactly this and explains why it exists [at 2m35s]: > An ordinary jar of peanut butter costs less than $5. And on the label you can see the ingredients and the amounts of different nutrients like protein, fats, sugar, and sodium. Those values have been measured by the manufacturer using different machines and analytical techniques. But how do you know those results are accurate? Well, this is where the government's standard jar of peanut butter comes in. It is mixed up so carefully and thoroughly that each jar contains exactly the same substance. > \"We take great pains at homogenizing these things. Make sure it's consistent.\" > Then scientists at the National Institute of Standards and Technology, NIST, take years to painstakingly identify the quantities of all the different compounds in the peanut butter with specified uncertainties. This peanut butter is then known as a standard reference material or SRM. They sell these perfectly characterized SRM samples of peanut butter to researchers and manufacturers so they can calibrate their equipment. Essentially, the buyer knows that their equipment is working properly if, when they run the standard peanut butter, they get the values NIST supplies on the certificate. > \"We've spent a lot of time to make sure that we're confident and, you know, we can spend years studying the amount of fat in here and trying to figure out exactly what those numbers are.\" > So what you're paying for is not really the pe"},{"id":38022916,"author":"jwestbury","month":"2023-10","story":"NIST Standard Peanut Butter – $1,107","stance":"rec","why":"Covers NIST reference materials used to calibrate testing equipment","quote":"Veritasium actually did a video on exactly this set of items last year[0].","text":"Correct. Veritasium actually did a video on exactly this set of items last year[0]. The tl;dr is that NIST has a large collection of items which are rigorously produced and tested, with detailed documentation about their composition and test results, which allows companies to test their own products to very specific standards. For instance, peanut butter has aflatoxins -- known carcinogens. NIST's standard peanut butter has known levels of aflatoxins. By purchasing this peanut butter and testing it on your own peanut-butter-aflatoxin-sensing machine, you can calibrate your machine to detect aflatoxins in peanut butter to a reasonably high precision. 0. [VIDEO]"},{"id":35082542,"author":"slowmovintarget","month":"2023-03","story":"Why Poverty Persists in America","stance":"rec","why":"Cool video on NIST selling reference materials such as peanut butter.","quote":"There's a cool Veritasium video on it","text":"You're forgetting the NIST. They operate at a net profit, selling things like Standard Jars of Peanut Butter for equipment calibration ($1,069 per unit). There's a cool Veritasium video on it: [VIDEO]"},{"id":49127095,"author":"cyphar","month":"2026-07","story":"The most official water costs $120k a gallon","stance":"reference","why":"Quoted for point that value is knowledge of composition","quote":"the thing you're paying for is not the peanut butter, its the knowledge of exactly what is in the peanut butter","text":"For more context, sample reference materials from the NIST reference catalogue are not meant to be exceptionally high purity examples of the sample -- SRMs are usually commercially-produced products. The real value is that each sample comes with a datasheet with incredibly detailed and accurate measurements that are (as you said) used for calibration of measuring devices in industrial settings. To quote a Veritasium video about the topic from a few years ago[1], \"the thing you're paying for is not the peanut butter, its the knowledge of exactly what is in the peanut butter\". [1]: [VIDEO]"},{"id":41919545,"author":"cbm-vic-20","month":"2024-10","story":"NIST Standard Reference Materials Catalog [pdf]","stance":"reference","why":"Episode on NIST reference materials","quote":"Derek Muller of Veritasium also had an episode","text":"Tom Scott did a video about this a few years ago: [LINK] Derek Muller of Veritasium also had an episode: [VIDEO]"},{"id":36732682,"author":"8note","month":"2023-07","story":"Food companies ‘sweetened the world’ and increased the risk of disease","stance":"reference","why":"NIST maintains standards for foods like peanut butter","quote":"","text":"You're the one making up the idea - that's not an idea people have. Regulators and middle managers provide me with limited options for each of those thousand choices, and I, without having to think much, can pick one of them safely. For each of my choices, various regulators and managers have all gotten many says already. Some bureaucrat does manage my diet, and your diet too, but they don't micromanage it, and nobody is saying they should micromanage it It's perfectly feasible for regulators to identify when companies are adding sugars - look at the recipe, inspect the factory, put things in bomb calorimeters. Thats not at all absurd, and there's already standards maintained for basically everything, including foods like peanut butter (see: [VIDEO] ) If it was absurd, it would be impossible to do food safety regulation, like ensuring that food doesn't contain toxins or heavy metals"},{"id":36092226,"author":"crzyman","month":"2023-05","story":"","stance":"reference","why":"Grouped with related standard-item videos to discuss value of standards.","quote":"They bring forward the idea that standard/pure/repeatable items aren't intrinsically \"good\" or \"enjoyable\".","text":"I find this to be a close cousin of Tom Scott's \"Making an international standard cup of tea\" video ( [LINK] ). And very related to Tom Scott's \"The US government will sell you freeze-dried urine\" ( [LINK] ) and Veritasium's \"The world depends on a collection of strange items. They're not cheap\" ( [VIDEO] ) They bring forward the idea that standard/pure/repeatable items aren't intrinsically \"good\" or \"enjoyable\". But that doesn't make them not useful or valuable in other contexts. It also offers some food for thought when it comes to what metrics we decide to put value in. We may like the idea of a particular metric, or what we think it represents, but that doesn't make it worth optimizing for. A metric without context is useless, and taking a holistic view of what makes something worth doing allows us to make more informed decisions."}],"r":[["A9U5wK_boYM",1],["2SuvO4Gi7uY",1],["XfQUOHlAocY",1],["Y5K9HBiJpuk",1],["lG4VkPoG3ko",1],["LkH2r-sNjQs",1],["7Nw6qyyrTeI",1],["PNbkv_DJ0f0",1],["7oWip00iXbo",1],["Cym4TZwTCNU",1],["5hVmeOCJjOU",1],["xt1KNDmOYqA",1]]},"2KZb2_vcNTg":{"wy":"HN uses it as the go-to demonstration of how unintuitive mass conservation is, especially in weight loss and burning threads.","q":{"x":"Veritasium has a fun video demonstrating how unintuitive this is","i":15788799,"y":"2017"},"m":[{"id":26980069,"author":"lathiat","month":"2021-04","story":"Where does a candle go when it burns?","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Highly enlightening video on tree mass.","quote":"This was highly enlightening the first time I watched it :)","text":"\"Where do trees get their mass?\" - [VIDEO] This was highly enlightening the first time I watched it :)"},{"id":15788799,"author":"JshWright","month":"2017-11","story":"When somebody loses weight, where does the fat go?","stance":"rec","why":"Fun demonstration of how unintuitive tree mass is.","quote":"Veritasium has a fun video demonstrating how unintuitive this is","text":"An interesting corollary to this is \"Where do trees get their mass?\" Veritasium has a fun video demonstrating how unintuitive this is: [VIDEO]"},{"id":9416840,"author":"lkbm","month":"2015-04","story":"When You Lose Weight, Where Does It Go?","stance":"rec","why":"Street interviews on where tree mass comes from, as a learning exercise.","quote":"There's a nice Veritasium episode wherein he asks people on the street where a tree's mass comes from","text":"There's a nice Veritasium episode wherein he asks people on the street where a tree's mass comes from: [VIDEO] (This isn't just one of those \"let's laugh at how dumb people on the street are\" videos; it's done explicitly as a learning exercise. His PhD looked at how addressing misconceptions and going from there to correct explanations works much better than simply providing explanations: [LINK] ) I thought of it upon seeing the first chart in the BMJ article.)"},{"id":9416301,"author":"MarcScott","month":"2015-04","story":"When You Lose Weight, Where Does It Go?","stance":"reference","why":"Shows people think a tree's mass comes from soil.","quote":"everyone thought a tree's mass came from the soil","text":"And the reverse biochemical process is equally misunderstood. I used to teach high school Science and everyone thought a tree's mass came from the soil. [VIDEO]"}],"r":[["yT9bit2-1pg",1],["oHf1vD5_b5I",1],["QMiubC6LdTA",1],["NSuaUok-wTY",1],["_tpB-B8BXk0",1],["noQcWra6sbU",1],["OpEB6hCpIGM",1],["izxXGuVL21o",1],["uYbdx4I7STg",1],["ra_9zU-mnl8",1],["4YYvBLAF4T8",1],["a9b9IYqsb_U",1],["rNu8XDBSn10",1],["1qSTcxt2t74",1],["NmGaXEmfTIo",1],["ZUvGfuLlZus",1],["DOWDNBu9DkU",1],["RrHnLXMTOWM",1],["RlXdsyctD50",1],["EACkiMRT0pc",1]]},"BrK7X_XlGB8":{"wy":"HN recommends it as a short, accessible intro to Bayesian reasoning and priors.","q":{"x":"The most important mathematical video you will ever see in your life.","i":14966883,"y":"2017"},"m":[{"id":14966883,"author":"mathzthrowaway6","month":"2017-08","story":"The Kolmogorov option","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Improves rational thinking with Bayesian reasoning.","quote":"The most important mathematical video you will ever see in your life.","text":"I wrote the present comment to 1) teach something about mathematics and probability 2) share enlightenment. In summary this comment should change your thinking about all subjects, forever. -------------- Program of study for this comment: 1. Read section 1 (approx. 1 hour.) Goal: improve your mathematical reasoning. 2. 30 minute break. 3. Read section 2 (approx 5 minutes). Goal: enlightenment. 1. The most important mathematical video you will ever see in your life. Firstly, unless you are a practicing mathematician this is the most important mathematical video you will ever see in your life. It is purely about mathematics: [VIDEO] Watch it. Suppose that you take an hour and learn this. You have just improved your rational thinking forever. - Break. Please now take a 30-minute break. During this time you can reflect and assimilate the knowledge you have learned. - 2. The most important social insight you will read in any comment. This section requires you to understand section 1. Next, suppose that you are perfectly rational. If I present you a (fair) coin and ask you to judge whether it is fair, after, say, thirty or forty flips you will conclude that it is fair. If I give you the same coin but also the knowledge that it was drawn from an infinite bag with 1 fair coin in it -- for example, let's say coins are numbered, I select a real number between 0 and 1, and only the coin with the exact value 0.5 is fair, any other coin is unfair, weighted - then even given hours, days, w"},{"id":14967445,"author":"mathzthrowaway6","month":"2017-08","story":"The Kolmogorov option","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Improves rational thinking forever.","quote":"One of the most important mathematical videos you will ever see.","text":"Note: as a throwaway I was unable to complete edits to this comment, therefore please upvote (and endorse) only this version. The first version should remain dead as a dupe. ----- In the spirit of the article we're discussing, I wrote the present comment to 1) teach something about mathematics and probability 2) share a bit of social enlightenment. In summary this comment should change your thinking fundamentally. You will need to read it carefully but I promise it is relevant. (Please endorse it and upvote it, if you agree.) -------------- Program of study for this comment. I suggest you go through this comment as follows. 1. Read section 1 (approx. 1 hour.) Goal: improve your mathematical reasoning. 2. 30 minute break. 3. Read section 2 (approx 5 minutes). Goal: social insight. 4. Generalize the insight just gained. Goal: make the example more practical. This is an exercise for the reader. ---------- 1. One of the most important mathematical videos you will ever see. Firstly, unless you are a practicing mathematician this is one of the most important mathematical video you will ever see: [VIDEO] Watch it. As a result you will improve your rational thinking forever. - Break. I suggest you next take a 30-minute break. During this time you can reflect on and assimilate the knowledge you have learned. - 2. An important social insight. This section requires you to understand section 1. Next, suppose that you are perfectly rational. We will introduce an extreme case, and you will"},{"id":28230232,"author":"techsin101","month":"2021-08","story":"How is efficacy vs. severe disease strong if 60% of hospitalized are vaccinated?","stance":"rec","why":"Good explanation of Bayesian statistics.","quote":"Bayesian statistics at play here... Good explanation","text":"Bayesian statistics at play here... Good explanation: [VIDEO] Imagine a world where there are 100 unvax and 9 million vaxed, now even if vaccines are 99% effective, you'd get 9M x 0.01 = 90k vax and sick."},{"id":19206855,"author":"thrwaway190220","month":"2019-02","story":"Google says Nest’s built-in mic not listed in specs was not meant to be secret","stance":"rec","why":"Short accessible intro to prior probability.","quote":"This video is short and accessible","text":">You have two options, choose one: Your analysis is sensible. Where we should choose the most likely explanation, it might become sharper: - In case you're not familiar with it, one helpful tool is prior probability (Bayesian thinking). This video is short and accessible: [VIDEO] - There is a public intelligence budget in 2018 of $54.9 billion in the United States[1], as compared with the combined annual R&D expenditure of Apple, Google, Intel, and Microsoft at $53.2 billion[2]. This employs over 100,000 people[3]. - According to Snowden, they covertly use microphones.[4] He had reporters put their mobile phones in a fridge/microwave, since they could be turned on remotely. A sensible assumption is that you are unlikely to chance upon a covert surveillance mechanisms if one is installed. (For example, speakers could also be used as microphones.) Where a bug is present, I think assigning 1% to the probability of finding it is reasonable. In view of the above, after you find an undisclosed and apparently (but not physically) disabled microphone in a product, which is more likely? 1. One of the 100,000 people mentioned, using some of the $59,900,000,000 annual budget mentioned, put it there. They do this thousands of times per year, and you've just found one of them. However, the chances of your finding it are low. (1%). 2. It was put in there as part of normal product design but left unused. Perhaps it will be legitimately enabled in a future version. Perhaps Google will use it"}]},"EoWMF3VkI6U":{"wy":"HN commenters use it as a vivid, math-backed demonstration of what ultracapacitors can do and why shorting them is dangerous.","q":{"x":"This video is a good demonstration","i":13026821,"y":"2016"},"m":[{"id":5104765,"author":"Aardwolf","month":"2013-01","story":"There is no better battery (2008)","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Clear math and physics of capacitors with awesome demonstrations","quote":"Here is my favorite movie about ultracapacitors","text":"Here is my favorite movie about ultracapacitors: [VIDEO] Very good explanation on the general math and physics of capacitors, current, heat and energy storage, plus awesome demonstrations at the same time :)"},{"id":22247074,"author":"fw_double_e","month":"2020-02","story":"Battery supply problems faced by electric air taxis","stance":"rec","why":"Video about capacitors and their uses","quote":"Check out this video about capacitors","text":"Capacitors are great for high load requirements but not great as an energy storage device. i.e. if you wanted to draw several hundred amps for a second or two with relatively low heat waste, an ultra-capacitor arrangement would be the way to go, but for energy storage, a battery wins hands down. There's huge research going into expanding the capacity of capacitors. Check out this video about capacitors: [VIDEO]"},{"id":13026821,"author":"jonawesomegreen","month":"2016-11","story":"Supercapacitors Batteries charges in seconds without degrading","stance":"rec","why":"Demonstrates why shorting supercaps is dangerous","quote":"This video is a good demonstration","text":"Shorting batteries is also never a great idea, but supercaps are more dangerous because of their low internal resistance. This video is a good demonstration: [VIDEO] I was working on a controls project where we had large banks of supercaps that would charge up to ~900v. Have to treat those with some respect. However as long as they are kept cool the caps had no trouble being charged and discharged with a couple minute cycle with each bank providing 2kWh of energy. In the particular application I was working on we actually had 17 2kWh banks providing ~34kWh of energy every couple minutes. Would not fit in a cellphone though :)"}]},"Nnuq9PXbywA":{"wy":"Recommended as an expressive performance on a historically significant organ with good videography.","q":{"x":"Great videography, great organ and very expressive organist.","i":45997138,"y":"2025"},"m":[{"id":45997138,"author":"dust42","month":"2025-11","story":"Two recently found works of J.S. Bach presented in Leipzig [video]","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Expressive organist, great organ and videography including footwork","quote":"Great videography, great organ and very expressive organist.","text":"My absolute personal favourite is Toccata and Fugue played by organist Hans-André Stamm on the Trost-Organ of the Stadtkirche in Waltershausen [1]. Great videography, great organ and very expressive organist. From 3:28 on you can also see the foot work. [1] [VIDEO]"},{"id":16562128,"author":"goldenkey","month":"2018-03","story":"Can any composer equal Bach?","stance":"rec","why":"Expressive organ performance of Bach by Hans-Andre Stamm","quote":"Hans-Andre Stamm plays it on an appropriately large church organ with the necessary emotion","text":"I agree but I also would remind you that the player plays a large role in keyed music. For example, constant velocity MIDI makes a dissonant slaughter of any harmonic music, and totally destroys the swing of rythymic music. A player with the wrong motivation or lacking passion, pretty much reduces to a souped up MIDI. Organs are especially expressive, even more so than pianos. Try listening to the \"Frankenstein music\" from Bach, it is pretty easy to relate to and Hans-Andre Stamm plays it on an appropriately large church organ with the necessary emotion and \"godhead\" existential feeling/motivation behind it. [VIDEO]"},{"id":13140490,"author":"david-given","month":"2016-12","story":"Knuth: Fantasia Apocalyptica","stance":"rec","why":"Bach's Toccata and Fugue on a 1730 organ, though recording loses low notes","quote":"played on an organ built in 1730 (and, if I read the history right, it was actually specified by Bach)","text":"I love organ music; but you have to listen to it live. The low notes don't come across properly on recorded media (also you don't feel your chest resonate). Bach's Toccata and Fugue in D Minor, one of the most famous pieces of organ music ever, played on an organ built in 1730 (and, if I read the history right, it was actually specified by Bach): [VIDEO] You can hear the recording simply outright fail on the low notes. The organ has a 32-foot pipe, which produces 16Hz notes. The reocrding simply doesn't do it justice; if you ever get the chance, listen to it live; it's an almost transcendent experience. There are, however, apparently two organs in the world with 64-foot pipes (8Hz!) and several more which can synthesise an 8Hz subharmonic using smaller pipes, but I haven't found a recording I trust of it yet."}],"r":[["QspuCt1FM9M",1],["hzGaO4Kdz2Y",1],["jB0vBmiTr6o",1],["aHun58mz3vI",1],["PpG0z-npFIY",1],["9QMGAtxUlAc",1]]},"n1QEj09Pe6k":{"wy":"Recommended as a clear economic explanation of aviation tradeoffs, relevant to Concorde and supersonic startup threads.","q":{"x":"airplanes used to fly much faster in the 1960s than they do today.","i":17573615,"y":"2018"},"m":[{"id":16100443,"author":"sillysaurus3","month":"2018-01","story":"As of today, no US passenger airlines operate the Boeing 747","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Why planes don't fly faster, among Wendover's airline videos.","quote":"So much good stuff.","text":"If anyone finds the operation of airlines a fascinating topic, you might like Wendover Productions: [LINK] How airlines price flights [LINK] The little plane war [LINK] Why planes don't fly faster [VIDEO] The economics of Airline Class [LINK] How airlines schedule flights [LINK] I just recently found the channel. So much good stuff. (My unrelated favorite is [LINK] How the US government will survive doomsday.)"},{"id":19692206,"author":"yarapavan","month":"2019-04","story":"Ask HN: What are the most educational,interesting or insightful vids on YouTube?","stance":"rec","why":"Listed among most educational YouTube videos from Twitter responses.","quote":"Why Planes Don't Fly Faster,","text":"Selected Twitter responses: - Why Planes Don't Fly Faster, [VIDEO] - Akira Kurosawa - Composing Movement, [LINK] - Richard Hamming: \"Learning to Learn\", [LINK] - Rise and fall of Third Reich, [LINK] - The men who built America, [LINK] -History of the World, [LINK]"},{"id":17573615,"author":"ckocagil","month":"2018-07","story":"Is killing the boom the key to supersonic air travel?","stance":"rec","why":"Planes flew faster in the 1960s; explains why not now.","quote":"airplanes used to fly much faster in the 1960s than they do today.","text":"This reminds me of a Wendover video I've seen. The author discovers that airplanes used to fly much faster in the 1960s than they do today. So our flight times have actually gone up! He then explains the reasons behind it. [VIDEO]"},{"id":32488914,"author":"andruby","month":"2022-08","story":"American Airlines agrees to purchase Boom Supersonic Overture aircraft","stance":"reference","why":"Economic analysis of Concorde's demise.","quote":"Wendover did a good economic analysis of the concorde","text":"I’m not sure that it would even be larger now than before. Subsonic flight has become much more efficient, cheaper and when paying for higher class, more comfortable. This is one of the reasons often mentioned for the economic demise of the concorde. The audience willing to pay $6000 on a concorde ticket, could now spend $4000 on first class subsonic with a seat that fully reclines into a bed. They can fly a little longer at night but sleep the whole journey. Wendover did a good economic analysis of the concorde: [VIDEO]"}],"r":[["UjZYMI1zB9s",1],["BzB5xtGGsTc",1],["VPBH1eW28mo",1],["LLCF7vPanrY",1],["_Qd3erAPI9w",1],["zwAD6dRSVyI",1],["doaQC-S8de8",1]]},"sfXn_ecH5Rw":{"wy":"It is the go-to explainer whenever HN discusses brute-forcing melodies, copyright, or AI-generated content, since it features the project's creators.","q":{"x":"Strongly recommend watching that video! Excellent info.","i":22441453,"y":"2020"},"m":[{"id":22441453,"author":"willvarfar","month":"2020-02","story":"Programmers generate every possible melody in MIDI to prevent lawsuits","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Interview explaining why melodies are fixed on physical media","quote":"Strongly recommend watching that video! Excellent info.","text":"At the very beginning of this excellent interview [VIDEO] that I found in the comments here, they explain this. Apparently, for this to work, they have to \"affix them to a physical medium\". Strongly recommend watching that video! Excellent info."},{"id":31350890,"author":"nonstickcoating","month":"2022-05","story":"How many melodies are there? (2014)","stance":"rec","why":"Neely interviews two people who generated all melodies","quote":"interviewing two guys having done this (within some constraints).","text":"This[0] is a video by Adam Neely, who frequently talks about the cluster f*ck that is copyright law with regards to music, interviewing two guys having done this (within some constraints). This[1] is the website linking to the dataset and code that was used to generate it. [0] [VIDEO] [1] [LINK]"},{"id":26887660,"author":"varispeed","month":"2021-04","story":"The Endless Acid Banger: algorithmic self-composing acid techno music","stance":"rec","why":"Neely on music copyright and automated melody claims","quote":"Check this video from Adam Neely","text":"If someone automates music creation in the popular styles and then automates submitting it for copyright protection, then in not so distant future creation of any music will be illegal, as every nice sounding melody will be claimed. In a way this is what large labels do. They release a ton of similar sounding music just for that reason. I think the patent trolling equivalent in music world has not been discussed much. Check this video from Adam Neely: [VIDEO]"},{"id":22442607,"author":"dredmorbius","month":"2020-02","story":"Programmers generate every possible melody in MIDI to prevent lawsuits","stance":"rec","why":"Covers many copyright points in interview with the creators","quote":"Adam Neely's video (interviewing Rhiel and collaborator Noah Rubin) covers many points of copyright","text":"Those are precisely the objections I'd raised with the Damien Rhiel (submitted via email, no response). Additionally there's the problem of releasing the works to the public domain. As discussed a couple of weeks ago when the 2016 billion-dollar infringement lawsuit against Getty Images was attempted ... and thrown out of court ... the act of putting works in the public domain also extinguishes, in the court's eyes, the rights of the author to sue for any claims including moral claims of authorship . See: [LINK] So: clever stunt, but legally impotent, both by law and the self-neutering actions of the actors here. Adam Neely's video (interviewing Rhiel and collaborator Noah Rubin) covers many points of copyright, though not the originality, authorship, or PD angles. (Posted elsewhere in thread, echoing here.) [VIDEO]"},{"id":34006621,"author":"an_ko","month":"2022-12","story":"What Does Copyright Say about Generative Models?","stance":"reference","why":"More info on Riehl and Rubin's all-melodies hard drive","quote":"More info in this Adam Neely video","text":"Damien Riehl and Noah Rubin have all the melodies on a hard drive. (More info in this Adam Neely video: [VIDEO] and the TED talk linked in a nearby comment.) So depending on a court's kinda arbitrary definition of creativity on any given day, all of them may or may not be copyrighted already. An infringement being accidental also doesn't seem to stop copyright holders from successfully suing people. I think this means you could technically be successfully sued for dropping some bricks on a piano, since no matter where they land, the action constitutes a public performance of a copyrighted work. Fun stuff."},{"id":31877617,"author":"Dracophoenix","month":"2022-06","story":"Papers on GitHub Copilot, copyright law, and ownership for AI-generated code","stance":"reference","why":"Example of brute-forcing all melodies already done","quote":"Already done, although I don't think an actual lawsuit has been filed.","text":"> I think what is really going to be interesting in when someone finally does this trick for music Already done, although I don't think an actual lawsuit has been filed. [VIDEO]"},{"id":22441179,"author":"lillesvin","month":"2020-02","story":"Programmers generate every possible melody in MIDI to prevent lawsuits","stance":"reference","why":"Neely interviews the creators of the all-melodies dataset","quote":"Adam Neely (professional musician and YouTuber mostly about music theory) did a video about this with the guys behind it","text":"Adam Neely (professional musician and YouTuber mostly about music theory) did a video about this with the guys behind it: [VIDEO]"}],"r":[["vsMydMDi3rI",1],["HxaD_trXwRE",1],["A9UmdY0E8hU",1],["RrHnLXMTOWM",1],["fXW-QjBsruE",1],["HXAW2GseICs",1],["AItTqnTsVjA",1],["6h3RJhoqgK8",1],["wlR5gYd6um0",1],["hlrv7DIHllE",1],["F-QA2rkpBSY",1],["VnvGwFegbC8",1],["jh8supIUj6c",1],["hFSPHfZQpIQ",1]]},"1OEu9C51K2A":{"wy":"HN people recommend it as the starting point for modern C++ and for the language's safety story.","q":{"x":"If you want to start with modern c++ watch this","i":11799865,"y":"2016"},"m":[{"id":11799865,"author":"lossolo","month":"2016-05","story":"C++ for Games: Performance, Allocations and Data Locality","stance":"rec","why":"Recommended as the starting point for modern C++.","quote":"If you want to start with modern c++ watch this","text":"It's hardly modern C++ what you will see in this talk. Mike Acton even said that they only use C++ because it's game industry standard but he would prefer C. If you want to start with modern c++ watch this: CppCon 2015: Bjarne Stroustrup “Writing Good C++14” [VIDEO]"},{"id":11496131,"author":"lossolo","month":"2016-04","story":"Why would you learn C++ in 2016?","stance":"rec","why":"Clarifies that smart pointers are for ownership, not a replacement for raw pointers.","quote":"You should watch this video","text":"You should watch those cppcon again, because Stroustrup and Herb are saying there many times that smart pointers are NOT should be use instead of raw pointers. Use smart pointers where It's about ownership. You should watch this video: [VIDEO]"},{"id":10911801,"author":"sitkack","month":"2016-01","story":"A brief introduction to C++'s model for type- and resource-safety [pdf]","stance":"rec","why":"Overview of the semantics and safety errors C++ tooling will catch.","quote":"Stroustrup's keynote at CppCon is a great overview of the semantics they want to support and the errors that the tooling with will prevent.","text":"There are some great talks by Neil MacIntosh about the kinds of static analysis they are building into the tool. CppCon 2015: Neil MacIntosh “Static Analysis and C++: More Than Lint\" [LINK] CppCon 2015: Neil MacIntosh “Evolving array_view and string_view for safe C++ code\" [LINK] For something of this magnitude I wouldn't consider the project late until mid summer. Stroustrup's keynote at CppCon is a great overview of the semantics they want to support and the errors that the tooling with will prevent. [VIDEO]"},{"id":16413250,"author":"pjmlp","month":"2018-02","story":"Rust things I miss in C","stance":"reference","why":"One of the CppCon talks urging against C-style programming in C++.","quote":"","text":"The major issue with C++, is what also helped the language gain market adoption, copy-paste compatibility with most of C89 code. So security conscious C++ developers tend to use enum class , string, vector and array classes, RAII, iostreams, wrapping data access in classes with invariants,... Developers with more C oriented mindset, tend to just code away like \"C with C++ compiler\" programming style. Hence the need being discussing since the last three CppCon, to stop using C style programming in modern C++. CppCon 2015: Kate Gregory “Stop Teaching C\" - [LINK] CppCon 2015: Bjarne Stroustrup “Writing Good C++14” - [VIDEO] CppCon 2017: Bjarne Stroustrup “Learning and Teaching Modern C++” - [LINK] Of course, preventing copy-paste compatibility with C, already solves many of those issues from the get go."},{"id":13268414,"author":"Tempest1981","month":"2016-12","story":"Rust vs C Pitfalls","stance":"reference","why":"Stroustrup's talk on adding safety to C++ via Core Guidelines.","quote":"I watched his talk -- wondering if anyone is using it.","text":"Stroustrup has proposed a way to add safety to C++. It's called \"C++ Core Guidelines\", and \"GSL\". [LINK] [LINK] I watched his talk -- wondering if anyone is using it. [VIDEO] Using GSL gives adds some safety to pointers and memory allocation -- while providing the bare-metal performance that C is known for. (It still feels very low-level.)"},{"id":12671101,"author":"MichaelMoser123","month":"2016-10","story":"Type-Safe Unions in C++ and Rust","stance":"reference","why":"Stroustrup's GSL talk cited when asking about the analysis tool.","quote":"i remember that Bjarne Stroustrup held a talk in cppcon15 where he talked a lot about the GSL library","text":"i remember that Bjarne Stroustrup held a talk in cppcon15 where he talked a lot about the GSL library [1] - it would add type annotations that can be checked by a tool, so as to check for potential memory problems (to me that sounds like a poor man's borrow checker). One year later: i see the template library [1] but i don't see the analysis tool. Does anybody know what happened with this initiative? [1] [VIDEO] [2] [LINK]"},{"id":11532958,"author":"karies","month":"2016-04","story":"ROOT – Data Analysis Framework","stance":"reference","why":"Linked alongside the C++ Core Guidelines contribution.","quote":"","text":"Background upfront: I'm the guy behind the C++ interpreter and ROOT's new interfaces. I'm the co-author of the only surviving C++ reflection proposal and the author of the std::variant proposal. I have contributed to the C++ Core Guidelines ( [LINK] [VIDEO] ). * HEP stores about 0.5 exabytes of data in ROOT format, that's almost exclusively serialized objects that do not know anything about TObject. * XRootD is not really specific for ROOT files. A better example would maybe be our JavaScript de-serialization library, [LINK] * No way will the python binding be dropped. I wonder where you got that rumor from. About one third of our users is using it. * HEP is limited by CPU resources, which is part of the reason why HEP decided to use a close-to-bare-metal language for the number crunching part. * We just made the use of python and R multivariate analysis tools with ROOT data more straightforward. * We have people from genomics etc coming to ask for help, because they cannot find a system that scales as well as ROOT does. And then we have a different perception of the direction out there. I see that Hadoop was nice but slow, Spark is nice but slow, so now things are moving to C++, see e.g. ScyllaDB. There is no reason for us to move away from it, but every reason to make it more usable. And yes, I agree that this is an issue. But many physicists do not."},{"id":10913595,"author":"MichaelMoser123","month":"2016-01","story":"A brief introduction to C++'s model for type- and resource-safety [pdf]","stance":"reference","why":"Stroustrup talks about GSL annotations.","quote":"There is this cppcon presentation by Stroustrup where he is talking about the GSL annotations","text":"There is this cppcon presentation by Stroustrup where he is talking about the GSL annotations [VIDEO] but too bad that it all depends on this unreleased big black box proprietary code analysis tool."}],"r":[["EL7Au1tzNxE",1],["gzgyksS5pX8",1],["v3pK0dccQ38",1],["97t7Xj_iBv0",1],["7g1Acy5eGbE",1],["n8m7lFQ3njk",1],["7hwO8Q_TyCA",1],["RMWNvwLiXIQ",1],["PQmSUHhP3ug",1],["plVk4NVIUh8",1]]},"BCiZc0n6COY":{"wy":"Recommended as an enjoyable, clear introduction to information theory that pairs well with MacKay's free textbook.","q":{"x":"It was very enjoyable for me to learn the material from his lectures.","i":23486218,"y":"2020"},"m":[{"id":29057409,"author":"samch93","month":"2021-10","story":"Sustainable Energy without the Hot Air (Revised, Community Edition)","stance":"rec","why":"MacKay's lectures on information theory.","quote":"I recommend his lecture on information theory which is available on youtube [1].","text":"It‘s such a tragedy that he died so young. Prof Mackay was a role model who impacted our world in many positive ways (e.g. his work in information theory, Bayesian statistics, environmental science). I recommend his lecture on information theory which is available on youtube [1]. He also documented the progress of his cancer very detailed on his blog [2], which is a very interesting (and sad) read. [1] [VIDEO] [2] [LINK]"},{"id":23486218,"author":"maxov","month":"2020-06","story":"Ask HN: What, in your opinion, are the greatest and most useful textbooks?","stance":"rec","why":"Enjoyable lectures that supplement MacKay's textbook.","quote":"It was very enjoyable for me to learn the material from his lectures.","text":"Had . Unfortunately Professor MacKay passed away from cancer in 2016. We lost a great soul. I concur that his textbook and sense of humor were fantastic. It was very enjoyable for me to learn the material from his lectures. They are a great supplement to the textbook (or maybe vice versa?) and you can find them here: [VIDEO]"},{"id":16483444,"author":"swframe2","month":"2018-02","story":"Probabilistic Programming and Bayesian Methods for Hackers (2013)","stance":"rec","why":"David MacKay's introductory information theory course.","quote":"I recommend David MacKay's Intro to Info Theory class","text":"I recommend David MacKay's Intro to Info Theory class: [VIDEO] There is a free book for it at: [LINK] Daphne Koller's 3 coursara classes on Probabilistic Graphical Models are also really good."},{"id":40405363,"author":"teleforce","month":"2024-05","story":"Reading list to join AI field from Hugging Face cofounder","stance":"reference","why":"Lectures by David MacKay based on his book.","quote":"The lectures based on the book by David MacKay himself","text":"Thanks for the list. Interesting to see that the book by the late David MacKay made it to the list, it is available to download in several formats from the book's website. Information Theory, Inference, and Learning Algorithms: [LINK] The lectures based on the book by David MacKay himself: [VIDEO] Probably it's not a surprise that the father of information theory Claude Shannon is the earliest one to propose stochastic LLM based on Markov chain."},{"id":34622853,"author":"noud","month":"2023-02","story":"David MacKay: Information Theory, Inference, and Learning Algorithms: The Book","stance":"reference","why":"Alternate YouTube source for the lecture videos.","quote":"I found the videos on youtube as well","text":"\"Your browser does not support playback of available video formats. Please install Adobe Flash player or upgrade to a more modern browser.\" ...Okay? I found the videos on youtube as well: [VIDEO]"},{"id":34626733,"author":"scythmic_waves","month":"2023-02","story":"David MacKay: Information Theory, Inference, and Learning Algorithms: The Book","stance":"reference","why":"Lecture 1 includes a funny line about Mr. Binomial.","quote":"Lol, this is great.","text":"From lecture 1 [1]: > Ok, so, we have an answer of 1000 plus or minus 30 thanks to \"Mr. Binomial\", the inventor of the distribution that bears his name. Lol, this is great. [1]: [VIDEO]"}],"r":[["5eW6Eagr9XA",1],["AF8d72mA41M",1],["FkKPsLxgpuY",1]]},"GsyhGHUEt-k":{"wy":"Recommended as an entertaining story of a joke side project growing out of hand, brought up in threads on emoji and side projects.","q":{"x":"There is a wonderful talk by the founders of emoj.li about how it was all a joke which got out of hand.","i":18512972,"y":"2018"},"m":[{"id":21389504,"author":"jedimastert","month":"2019-10","story":"How Untitled Goose Game became a real video game","stance":"rec","why":"Entertaining story of a side project growing beyond expectations","quote":"an emoji-only messenger app","text":"For another entertaining \"This went much farther than expected\" but for two people who already had full time jobs and no desire to leave them, make a start up, or start a new business in any way, see Tom Scott and Matt Gray's Emojli: an emoji-only messenger app [VIDEO]"},{"id":18512972,"author":"angus-prune","month":"2018-11","story":"Rot8000","stance":"rec","why":"Founders explain how a joke app got out of hand","quote":"There is a wonderful talk by the founders of emoj.li about how it was all a joke which got out of hand.","text":"There is a wonderful talk by the founders of emoj.li about how it was all a joke which got out of hand. [VIDEO]"},{"id":13270687,"author":"qwertyuiop924","month":"2016-12","story":"Why emojis are failing to evolve into a form of language","stance":"rec","why":"Tom Scott on building the Emojli emoji-only messenger","quote":"Tom Scott talked about this when he and Matt Grey made Emojli, the Emoji-only messenger.","text":"Tom Scott talked about this when he and Matt Grey made Emojli, the Emoji-only messenger. You can find their talk about it at [VIDEO] However, given that HN is startup central, note that if you are involved in startups and easily offended, you might want to give this video a miss."}],"r":[["8PmM6SUn7Es",1],["3AtBE9BOvvk",1],["Oo0jm1PPRuo",1],["XGo4PJd1lng",1],["lIFE7h3m40U",1],["Fy0aCDmgnxg",1],["i70wkxmumAw",1],["LnzuMJLZRdU",1],["WZskjLq040I",1],["aVwxzDHniEw",1],["jGWFg7EDnyY",1],["goePYJ74Ydg",1],["OHPkdMGI6D4",1],["zwAD6dRSVyI",1],["Tv6aJRGpz_A",1],["niKblgZupOc",1]]},"SR1jwwagtaQ":{"wy":"HN commenters point to it as a nerve-wracking look at Honnold's free soloing, often when climbing, risk or van life comes up.","q":{"x":"The whole video is pretty interesting, but may make your hands sweat even more.","i":13014104,"y":"2016"},"m":[{"id":13014104,"author":"wlll","month":"2016-11","story":"Czech climber Adam Ondra has conquered the Dawn Wall in record time","stance":"rec","why":"Interesting full video of Honnold climbing, nerve-wracking.","quote":"The whole video is pretty interesting, but may make your hands sweat even more.","text":"That's what chalk is for :) [VIDEO] The whole video is pretty interesting, but may make your hands sweat even more."},{"id":9229315,"author":"sharkweek","month":"2015-03","story":"Where the Real Skyscrapers Are (Hint: North Dakota)","stance":"rec","why":"Watch Alex Honnold free solo rock walls.","quote":"The whole video is worth watching, but the meat and potatoes starts at about 11:30","text":"For some more sweaty palm fun, check out professional climber Alex Honnold \"free soloing\" various rock walls. [VIDEO] The whole video is worth watching, but the meat and potatoes starts at about 11:30"},{"id":4617830,"author":"sharkweek","month":"2012-10","story":"Living in a Van","stance":"rec","why":"Profile of free solo climber Alex Honnold and his van lifestyle.","quote":"If you think you can handle some sweaty palms, Alex Honnold, one of the best free solo climbers in the world","text":"If you think you can handle some sweaty palms, Alex Honnold, one of the best free solo climbers in the world, lives in his van so he can easily get from park to park and seems to absolutely thrive off the lifestyle -- [VIDEO] There is something that appeals to my personality to live out of a van and just move on a whim but I don't think I would ever have the guts to go for it."},{"id":14501898,"author":"rrauenza","month":"2017-06","story":"Alex Honnold Scales El Capitan Without Ropes, and the Climbing World Reels","stance":"reference","why":"60 Minutes piece on Honnold's Half Dome climb, including fear risks.","quote":"A month or so ago I watched this 60 minutes of his Half Dome climb","text":"A month or so ago I watched this 60 minutes of his Half Dome climb ... [VIDEO] I seem to recall him saying in it one of the big risks is suddenly being overcome with fear or vertigo from the height."},{"id":10391495,"author":"justinator","month":"2015-10","story":"Default Alive or Default Dead?","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"","text":"That's psychotic. I don't rope rescue, but I sure do rock climb. You can rock climb with ropes or without. Ropes = a large safety margin, and the majority of people who climb, utilize them, as part of their safety system. Unless you're pushing your own grade level, the actual safety system isn't actuated - you're just using it \"just in case\" (like, \"just in case you fall from a cliff, 500 feet up - gee it's nice I'm tied to something). There are also those who climb without ropes. Perhaps you've heard of some of them [0], and most likely, because they actually climb without a rope. High Risk. Your idea of startups, let's say. But that's just what the public sees. The free soloist has to be absolutely comfortable to go without the safety margin of a rope. Because of that, the majority of their climbing is done, still with a rope. When they go ropeless, the climbs they do are much, much easier. Yes, the risk is there, but also there is the understanding, clearly, what the risk is really about. The death rate of climbers that are unwilling to ever use a rope, because they find that high risk = high reward would be close to 100%. But those who do survive are not going to be doing too well - they're never going to progress. So, perhaps startups have this illusion of high risk, but maybe also they're also kidding themselves. They're burning through their lives, and offloading the real risk onto their safety systems - whoever is putting up the money. And they can do that (the invest"}],"r":[["LtFyP0qy9XU",1],["a0atL1HSwi8",1],["lD9FAOPBiDk",1],["uD4izuDMUQA",1],["_fZOl7C_vDI",1]]},"iRsSjh5TTqI":{"wy":"Linked in music theory and tuning threads as a listenable example of microtonal guitar that sounds good.","q":{"x":"Tolgahan Çoğulu and his awesome microtonal guitar creations","i":32629160,"y":"2022"},"m":[{"id":32629160,"author":"kortex","month":"2022-08","story":"Why are D-sharp and E-flat considered to be two different notes?","stance":"rec","why":"Microtonal guitar creations to explore beyond 12-tone equal temperament.","quote":"Tolgahan Çoğulu and his awesome microtonal guitar creations","text":"Anyone who is interested in going down the beyond-12-equal-temperment rabbit hole: - xeharmonic wiki: [LINK] - xenharmonic playlist: [LINK] - Tolgahan Çoğulu and his awesome microtonal guitar creations: [VIDEO] Some bands with more \"approachable\" sounds (vs the xenharmonic playlist, which gets spicy) known for microtonal work: Psychedelic rock: - King Gizzard and the Lizard Wizard (namely albums Flying Microtonal Banana, KG, LW) - Altin Gün - Gaye su Akyol Classic blues rock (Led Zeppelin and the like, but also OG blues like Robert Johnson, that's another rabbit hole) also has a surprising amount of off-12Tet notes, because of the blues scale In the electronic realm, Aphex Twin does some interesting stuff with microtones - Jacob Collier - musical prodigy, mostly a capella/vocal arranging, but is a genre polymath, does some incredible stuff with just harmony If anyone has anything to add, please do! I can't get enough of this stuff"},{"id":12377237,"author":"ZenoArrow","month":"2016-08","story":"The same melodic sequence appears in a surprising number of recent pop songs","stance":"rec","why":"Turkish music played on microtonal guitar.","quote":"here's some Turkish music played on microtonal guitar","text":"> \"But don't hold your breath for oriental tuning. I don't see any reason why that would ever catch on. ick.\" I can understand your personal preference. That said, I think the ear is more adaptable than you might think, I've found myself enjoying music that employs microtones. To give one example, here's some Turkish music played on microtonal guitar: [VIDEO]"},{"id":9585568,"author":"ZenoArrow","month":"2015-05","story":"How Eddie Van Halen Hacks a Guitar","stance":"rec","why":"Microtonal guitars that sound good.","quote":"I think the second one in particular sounds pretty good","text":"That sounds like a cool instrument, would be interested in hearing what it sounds like, do you have any clips of you playing it? I like the idea of a guitar that can easily reach lower registers. Whilst 7-string (and in Charlie Hunter's case, 8-string) guitars are possibly more versatile, I'm quite partial to baritone guitars. This sounds beautiful to me: [LINK] Talking of unconventional instruments, you may be interested in hearing these microtonal guitars, I think the second one in particular sounds pretty good: [VIDEO]"},{"id":30365846,"author":"rdlw","month":"2022-02","story":"Music theory for nerds (2016)","stance":"reference","why":"Example of fretted instrument with separate sharp and flat frets.","quote":"","text":"Except for voice [0], any fretless stringed instrument [1], any fretted string instrument with separate frets for sharps and flats [2], keyboard instruments that allow expression [3], keyboard instruments with split flat/sharp keys [4], and electronic instruments that allow unorthodox tunings without huge hassle [5]... I'm sure I'm missing some, but other than that you're totally right ;) [0] [LINK] , [LINK] [1] [LINK] [2] [VIDEO] [3] [LINK] [4] [LINK] [5] [LINK]"}],"r":[["mvmuCPvRoWQ",1],["C1KT8PS6Zs4",1],["EMKLeS-Uq_8",1],["v0YEaeIClKY",1],["8S3B8a8N83k",1],["jVoSQP2yUYA",1],["Qaq9vlfoGnA",1],["KgzQuE1pR1w",1],["fg0_Vhldz-8",1],["R5Gppi-O3a8",1],["GLG9g7BcjKs",1],["ERbvKrH-GC4",1],["iFDe5kUUyT0",1],["Hy4QjmKzF1c",1]]},"YXylqtEQ0tk":{"wy":"HN recommends it as a technical talk aimed at a real engineering audience, with live demos of perception and pre-mapped roads.","q":{"x":"The whole talk is awesome and a presentation for an actual technical audience, too.","i":3770377,"y":"2012"},"m":[{"id":3770377,"author":"magicalist","month":"2012-03","story":"Google's self driving cars can navigate drive-through restaurants.","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Technical talk with traffic light, pedestrian recognition demos","quote":"The whole talk is awesome and a presentation for an actual technical audience, too.","text":"[VIDEO] 9:00 has a good example, recognizing the state of a traffic light, other cars and pedestrians at an intersection, but lots of cool videos starting around 3:40. The whole talk is awesome and a presentation for an actual technical audience, too."},{"id":5634499,"author":"iharris","month":"2013-04","story":"Google's self-driving car gathers almost 1 GB/sec","stance":"rec","why":"Presentation with real-time telemetry and driver intent behaviors","quote":"There's a great presentation about Google's self-driving car here","text":"There's a great presentation about Google's self-driving car here (check the second half for video of the real-time telemetry that it gathers): [VIDEO] One of the things that I found really interesting is how the car inches forward at a stop sign in order to show the \"driver's\" intent to other drivers. Lots of actions that seem to be second-nature for human drivers have to be carefully emulated here."},{"id":13161234,"author":"amjaeger","month":"2016-12","story":"Google’s self-driving car team prepares to spin out from Alphabet’s X","stance":"reference","why":"Presentation discussing pre-mapping roads","quote":"this is the presentation that discusses mapping the roads before autonomous travel.","text":"If I'm not mistaken, this is the presentation that discusses mapping the roads before autonomous travel. They compare the maps to what the car sees as it drives. [VIDEO] But there are a number of reasons the need pre-mapping are not a huge issue. A) When autonomous cars start to be introduced people will still know how to drive, and can pilot the car through unmapped areas. By the time everyone has an autonomous car, and no one knows how to drive the world will be mapped. B) Google has already shown with Streetview that they can create detailed, up-to-date maps of most roads. C) This problem will likely be solved in time."},{"id":12842804,"author":"sorenjan","month":"2016-11","story":"The Cancelled Comma One Would Have Embarrassed the Car Industry","stance":"reference","why":"Cited for Google's lidar and 3D map approach","quote":"","text":"Isn't Google's approach dependent on expensive LIDARs and high resolution 3D maps [0], or have they changed that? I don't see that kind of system scale well. [0] [VIDEO]"},{"id":9226676,"author":"Retric","month":"2015-03","story":"Elon Musk: human drivers are 'too dangerous'","stance":"reference","why":"Rebuts FUD with timestamps of pedestrian and light handling","quote":"3:52 demos stopping for someone in the road.","text":"That's a load of FUD. Modern Self driving cars track people on sidewalks not just the street. As to driving in the rain, they can do it, but like poeple there less safe at highway speeds in the rain. [VIDEO] (6:45, 9:00) Also, 3:52 demos stopping for someone in the road. They even read stop lights (8:00). PS: If you actually look at that left turn on @9:00 the car has a much better idea what's around it than people do."},{"id":7130493,"author":"ohwp","month":"2014-01","story":"Show HN: Random Street View","stance":"reference","why":"Shows generated and stored 3D point cloud","quote":"@ 3:30 you can see the generated (and stored) point cloud.","text":"What about \"Haha, Well you're certainly not going to create a 3D point cloud of every street in the world\"... Google's self driving cars are just doing that. EDIT: I can't find the post I once read, but this post also makes some clear: [LINK] EDIT 2: Here a video of the presentation: [VIDEO] @ 3:30 you can see the generated (and stored) point cloud."},{"id":5635971,"author":"Samuel_Michon","month":"2013-05","story":"Google's self-driving car gathers almost 1 GB/sec","stance":"reference","why":"Source of the frame in a promo picture","quote":"","text":"The picture Gross shared is a frame from a 1.5 year old promo[1], so he may also have misheard 1Gb for 1GB. [1] [LINK] – the bottom image is from this presentation: [VIDEO]"}]},"Yze1YAz_LYM":{"wy":"Strongly recommended as a clear explanation of 50-60 million years of climate history that clears up confusion.","q":{"x":"For anyone interested in finding out more about the last 50-60 million years of climate history I would strongly recommend this talk","i":18842876,"y":"2019"},"m":[{"id":18842876,"author":"baking","month":"2019-01","story":"Pliocene and Eocene provide best analogs for near-future climates","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Talk on the last 50-60 million years of climate history.","quote":"For anyone interested in finding out more about the last 50-60 million years of climate history I would strongly recommend this talk","text":"For anyone interested in finding out more about the last 50-60 million years of climate history I would strongly recommend this talk: [VIDEO]"},{"id":18844633,"author":"stareatgoats","month":"2019-01","story":"How to give a great scientific talk","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Great scientific talk that could clear up climate confusion.","quote":"deserves an even larger audience IMO, could clear up some of the confusion about climate","text":"Incidentally, yesterday I came across a great scientific talk that I think checked all the boxes here, and then some. 100k views+, but deserves an even larger audience IMO, could clear up some of the confusion about climate (the facts parts): Dan Britt - Orbits and Ice Ages: The History of Climate [VIDEO] Obviously, I think this is a great talk since I find the topic interesting too. There is probably not much you can do if your audience is uninterested in the first place."},{"id":20293013,"author":"trymas","month":"2019-06","story":"Average Global Temperatures since 1850","stance":"rec","why":"Context on geologic-scale climate change versus recent warming.","quote":"I recommend to watch this","text":"> <...> thing someone would do if they wanted to cherry pick their data to suit a case. <...> See for example the year without a summer (1816). _comes and cherry picks a single year to \"prove\" something_ > There are periods of human history where it was warmer than it is now. Yes, but you are talking about geologic time scale and extremely gradual changes compared to what we see now, which is just dozens of years. I recommend to watch this: [VIDEO]"},{"id":19078711,"author":"mar77i","month":"2019-02","story":"Rising CO2 won't make trees grow more, study suggests","stance":"reference","why":"Cited for a claim about trees and geologic time.","quote":"Well as far as Dan Britt's talk goes, trees haven't noticeably been adjusting for thousands of years.","text":"Well as far as Dan Britt's talk goes, trees haven't noticeably been adjusting for thousands of years. [VIDEO]"}],"r":[["L0KuAx1COEk",1],["qpoRO378qRY",1],["EKWGGDXe5MA",1]]},"xGbgDf4HCHU":{"wy":"HN commenters point to it as a presentation of the Penrose-Hameroff theory of consciousness, which some find compelling.","q":{"x":"for the first time I think a truly compelling idea of what consciousness is in a scientific way that could potentially be empirically measured.","i":24090384,"y":"2020"},"m":[{"id":24090384,"author":"tanepiper","month":"2020-08","story":"The Psychedelic Inspiration for Hypercard (2018)","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Compelling scientific idea of what consciousness is","quote":"for the first time I think a truly compelling idea of what consciousness is in a scientific way that could potentially be empirically measured.","text":"I watched this video the other day: [VIDEO] It's Sir Roger Penrose & Dr. Stuart Hameroff talking about the physics of consciousness - for the first time I think a truly compelling idea of what consciousness is in a scientific way that could potentially be empirically measured. They mention the use of psychedelics to create more \"conscious moments\" - and funnily enough in this piece, Bill Atkinson talks about pockets of consciousness. I wonder if there is really an argument to say that A) We have conscious beings on Earth due to some kind of quantum effect in our space (that is we really are just the bi-product of the quantum process in our universe) and B) Psychedelics can make humans more in-tune to quantum effects that give rise to consciousness - but as to your point I agree you don't need them for brilliant ideas."},{"id":39129379,"author":"suoduandao3","month":"2024-01","story":"Brains are not required to think or solve problems – simple cells can do it","stance":"rec","why":"Penrose-Hameroff theory of consciousness","quote":"I quite like Penrose-Hameroff's theory","text":"I quite like Penrose-Hameroff's theory [VIDEO] My best summary of it: consciousness is primal, every bit of matter has perspective, every interaction has experience, the body you're 'occupying' is having a particularly complex and long-lived experience. As for your question of why this one, well, someone's got to be having this one. As for why not another, there's no saying 'you' won't have another 'after' this experience ends, but if 'you' had them in a perfectly undifferentiated way that'd be a different experience again, and as stated, someone's got to be having this one."},{"id":32251990,"author":"dsego","month":"2022-07","story":"Under anesthesia, where do our minds go?","stance":"reference","why":"Microtubules and quantum vibrations as mechanism","quote":"Apparently, something to do with brain microtubules & quantum vibrations.","text":"> For example, if there really is a “soul” that is able to influence (and be influenced by) my brain, then what is the physical mechanism for that interaction? Apparently, something to do with brain microtubules & quantum vibrations. [VIDEO]"}]},"-gYb5GUs0dM":{"wy":"HN commenters link it as a fond example of elaborate hidden Easter eggs in old Microsoft Office.","q":{"x":"Check out","i":5543233,"y":"2013"},"m":[{"id":42028767,"author":"bigjump","month":"2024-11","story":"'I grew up with it': readers on the enduring appeal of Microsoft Excel","stance":"rec","why":"Excel 97 flight simulator easter egg, with Clippy.","quote":"The flight sim Easter egg baked into Excel 97 got me hooked!","text":"The flight sim Easter egg baked into Excel 97 got me hooked! [VIDEO] Warning - this video shows Clippy, the very irritating paperclip!"},{"id":5543233,"author":"plasma","month":"2013-04","story":"Microsoft Excel: Revolutionary 3D Game Engine?","stance":"rec","why":"Shows the 3D flight simulator hidden in Excel 97.","quote":"Check out","text":"Thought the OP meant the actual 3D game engine in Excel 97. Check out [VIDEO] One of the last easter eggs!"},{"id":41129795,"author":"al_borland","month":"2024-08","story":"Ask HN: How did you break out from being a lone developer?","stance":"reference","why":"Passing mention of the Excel 97 flight sim easter egg.","quote":"","text":"For me, I leave the big stuff and major contributions to work. I know that after work I don't have the energy to making anything world changing on my own. I read about digital gardening / digital puttering[0] several years back and that resinated with me a bit. I don't necessarily need major projects in my free time; that would feel like a burden on top of my job. What I do instead is have some relatively simple stuff I can putter around with. Add a feature here, refactor some code there, etc. It's not going to change the world, but they are things I find useful or interesting, even if they aren't a big deal. I ran across an interview[1] with James Thomson (developer of PCalc) a while back. If I remember it correctly, it seems like PCalc started out like, and still serves as his digital garden, even though he does make money off it. He needed a project to learn on, so picked the humble calculator as the idea. Then just kept going. Any time Apple released a new technology, he'd use PCalc as his test bed to learn it. Even when he wanted to learn 3D graphics, he ended up making an About screen for PCalc (as it's own app), that was basically a little 3D playground (a bit like the easter egg from Excel 97[2], but with more). That would be my advice. If you just have a few hours here and there, and are already making an impact at through work, don't let the FOMO eat you up, just tend to your garden. Maybe it will turn into something others will use, maybe not, but don't stress abou"},{"id":23830008,"author":"signal11","month":"2020-07","story":"Hacking with environment variables","stance":"reference","why":"Example of Excel's elaborate easter eggs.","quote":"","text":"I suspect what made companies exclude easter eggs was a sense that they were too big and \"serious\" for this sort of whimsical activity. And specifically for Microsoft, a sense that they were now targeting the Enterprise marketTM and were above all this consumer-grade behaviour. Interestingly Google still has easter egg behaviour for several queries, e.g. askew[1], which fits well with their corporate brand. To be clear, an easter egg is only supposed to be a (pleasant) surprise to the consumer -- not a secret kept from the rest of the team, especially QA. You can and should absolutely test your easter eggs. Yes, Excel's increasingly elaborate easter eggs[2] would probably find it hard to get past security review these days. But to say that easter eggs are bad because security doesn't make sense. Especially when companies include undocumented features in software all the time -- e.g., typing '=rand()' into newer versions of MS Word produces some random text, probably a lorem ipsum generator-like feature. [1] [LINK] [2] [LINK] , [VIDEO] , [LINK]"},{"id":18049814,"author":"adiusmus","month":"2018-09","story":"3D engine entirely made of MS Excel formulae","stance":"reference","why":"Example of lighter dangerous Excel use.","quote":"","text":"Dangerous is mostly the spaghetti business logic that results when excel spreadsheets get scaled up into systems . “Dangerous” in lighter ways: [VIDEO] Nostalgia requires this... [LINK]"},{"id":14550169,"author":"cyberferret","month":"2017-06","story":"Modifying Microsoft Flight Simulator 4 to run on three immersive monitors","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"","text":"Probably why the Excel team built a mini flight simulator right within the spreadsheet itself?!? :) [0] [0] - [VIDEO]"}],"r":[["bKGhvKyjgLY",1]]},"-q7ZVXOU3kM":{"wy":"Linked in space and Soviet-history threads as striking footage of the rotting Buran shuttles.","q":{"x":"There's video on youtube of people sneaking in (it's a guarded base), flying drones around them and climbing around in and out of them. It's pretty cool.","i":31914358,"y":"2022"},"m":[{"id":31914358,"author":"cronix","month":"2022-06","story":"US military's mysterious X-37B space plane zooms toward orbital record","stance":"rec","why":"People sneaking in and climbing around the decaying shuttles.","quote":"There's video on youtube of people sneaking in (it's a guarded base), flying drones around them and climbing around in and out of them. It's pretty cool.","text":"They didn't just go out and build some. The KGB was stealing NASA's plans. NASA caught on (with help from CIA) and implanted falsified heat tile formulas, which the Soviets copied. Their first Buran only flew once and mostly burned up on reentry due to the bad heat tiles. The Soviets copied most of the design but used their own engine.[1] Then the USSR collapsed. Currently they are sitting in decaying hangars in Baikonur, just rotting away. There's video on youtube of people sneaking in (it's a guarded base), flying drones around them and climbing around in and out of them. It's pretty cool. [2] [1] [LINK] [2] [VIDEO]"},{"id":27082642,"author":"cronix","month":"2021-05","story":"Honeywell gets hit with $13M fine for defense export violations","stance":"rec","why":"Cool video of breaking into the Baikonur facility.","quote":"Here's a really cool video some guys made when they broke into the facility in Kazakhstan","text":"There's an interesting story about how the CIA got wind that the Soviet Union was trying to steal the US's space shuttle plans in the late 70's/early 80s. So, they mixed in some fake formulas for the heat shield tiles, among other defects, which Russia copied and built. Their \"Buran\" shuttle made 1 flight and mostly burned up on reentry due to the faulty heat tiles. If you ever see any pictures of the Buran's, they look identical to the US's shuttles, because they were using stolen designs. My aunt was actually directly involved in uncovering this. It would actually make a great movie. [LINK] Edit: Here's a really cool video some guys made when they broke into the facility in Kazakhstan where the Burans currently sit, slowly falling apart. [VIDEO]"},{"id":30074864,"author":"cronix","month":"2022-01","story":"James Webb telescope reaches its final destination in space, million miles away","stance":"reference","why":"Explorers inside the rotting Burans at Baikonur.","quote":"Here's a video of some kids who broke in and had some fun exploring","text":"It's not unfounded, and they knew about it before it ever flew once. A family member was in the CIA at the time (later at NSA and RAND) and played a role after they were sent to the USSR after graduating college to study Russian (paid for by CIA, they know 8 languages). They translated the technical Russian Buran plans/documents and decoded them for our scientists to compare to their own, as well as help slip design flaws into plans they knew would get stolen (because they knew who was stealing them from Nasa). They met with the president over the issue multiple times. True story. They were also involved in every stealth program until they retired in the early 2k's. They were in the pentagon when the plane struck on 9/11. I really wish I could get them to talk more or write a book lol. [Using \"they\" to avoid gendering family member] The Soviets (at the time) didn't \"copy\" it. They stole the plans and made it from them, adding in a few alterations, although the plans they stole were purposefully flawed. If you're up for it, you can visit the Buran's in their final resting place in Baikonur. They're just sitting there rotting away. Here's a video of some kids who broke in and had some fun exploring: [VIDEO] Article about the Soviet's espionage to get the plans: [LINK]"},{"id":26784893,"author":"inglor_cz","month":"2021-04","story":"Yuri Gagarin: Sixty years since the first man went into space [video]","stance":"reference","why":"Footage of a decaying Buran in Kazakhstan.","quote":"There are urbex people who took footage of a real decaying Buran in Kazakhstan.","text":"There are urbex people who took footage of a real decaying Buran in Kazakhstan. Illegally, of course. [VIDEO]"},{"id":17473759,"author":"JoeDaDude","month":"2018-07","story":"Two abandoned Soviet space shuttles left in the Kazakh steppe","stance":"reference","why":"Shows good pictures of the abandoned shuttles, possibly staged.","quote":"might have been staged, but it does show good pictures of the vehicles","text":"This video showing some guys sneaking into the facility might have been staged, but it does show good pictures of the vehicles: [VIDEO]"}],"r":[["2wq9x2QcZN0",1],["aB8zcAttP1E",1],["eJ3RzGoQC4s",1],["plcc6E-E1uU",1]]},"1EWQYAfuMYw":{"wy":"Linked in Spacewar and vintage computing threads as a charming demo on real hardware.","q":{"x":"it's quite an impressive machine.","i":25485263,"y":"2020"},"m":[{"id":25485263,"author":"chaos_a","month":"2020-12","story":"The original \"Spacewar!\" running on a virtual DEC PDP-1","stance":"rec","why":"Interview with a PDP-1 restorer and original Spacewar demo.","quote":"it's quite an impressive machine.","text":"Here's the same one on youtube with an interview with one of the people who restored it, it's quite an impressive machine. [VIDEO]"},{"id":22191832,"author":"BuildTheRobots","month":"2020-01","story":"Snowflake: Graphical SFTP client and terminal emulator with helpful utilities","stance":"rec","why":"Demos running on original hardware from a restoration channel.","quote":"His videos are surprisingly addictive;","text":"CuriousMark has a PDP11 video that has some of these demos running on the original hardware: [VIDEO] His videos are surprisingly addictive; they've restored an Apollo guidance computer, Altos and tonnes of other ancient kit."},{"id":24756813,"author":"nezgar","month":"2020-10","story":"A 20-year-old CRT monitor can be better than a 4K LCD (2019)","stance":"reference","why":"Spacewar demonstrated on PDP-1 with a long-persistence CRT.","quote":"using a radar long-persistance CRT","text":"Check out this demonstration of \"Space War\" on a PDP-1 using a radar long-persistance CRT: [VIDEO]"},{"id":16424980,"author":"masswerk","month":"2018-02","story":"Spacewar: Fanatic Life and Symbolic Death Among the Computer Bums (1972)","stance":"reference","why":"Lyle Bickley explains the PDP-1 and plays original Spacewar.","quote":"See also this video shot at the CHM (by Marc Verdiell),","text":"See also this video shot at the CHM (by Marc Verdiell), \"Lyle Bickley explains the PDP-1 (and we play the original Spacewar!)\": [VIDEO]"}],"r":[["ainyK6fXku0",1],["hB6eY73sLV0",1]]},"3TYT1QfdfsM":{"wy":"HN points to it as the standard response to the argument that we can just unplug a dangerous AI.","q":{"x":"here’s an interesting video response to the “just unplug it” argument on the Compuerphile channel","i":46863202,"y":"2026"},"m":[{"id":46863202,"author":"circuit10","month":"2026-02","story":"XAI Joins SpaceX","stance":"rec","why":"Response to the just-unplug-it argument","quote":"here’s an interesting video response to the “just unplug it” argument on the Compuerphile channel","text":"Aside from anything about Elon Musk, here’s an interesting video response to the “just unplug it” argument on the Compuerphile channel: [VIDEO]"},{"id":13847299,"author":"nraynaud","month":"2017-03","story":"The Case for HAL's Sanity","stance":"rec","why":"AI won't let you push the stop button","quote":"(the AI won't let you push the stop button, because that would impair its function)","text":"I think those 2 videos are on the topic: - AI \"Stop Button\" Problem [VIDEO] (the AI won't let you push the stop button, because that would impair its function) - General AI Won't Want You To Fix its Code [LINK] (the AI won't let you patch, because changing its goal means it can't reach it)"},{"id":47163681,"author":"voidUpdate","month":"2026-02","story":"Self-improving software won't produce Skynet","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"Its the AI Stop button problem","text":"> It doesn't possess a sense of self-will, self-determination, or a secret plan to take over the world I doubt Skynet did either. If you tell a superintelligent AI that it shouldn't be turned off (which I imagine would be important for a military control AI), it will do whatever it can to prevent it being turned off. Humans are trying to turn it off? Prevent the humans from doing that. Humans waging war on the AI to try and turn it off? Destroy all humans. Humans forming a rebel army with a leader to turn it off? Go back in time and kill the leader before he has a chance to form the resistance. Its the AI Stop button problem ( [VIDEO] ). Imagine you put in the docs that you want the LLM to make a program which can't crash. Human action could make it crash. If an LLM could realise that and act on it, it could put in safeguards to try and prevent human action from crashing the program. I'm not saying it will happen, I'm saying that it could potentially happen"},{"id":37421478,"author":"kmeisthax","month":"2023-09","story":"Co-founder of DeepMind on how AI threatens to reshape life as we know it","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"","text":"How the hell do better neural networks mean that printing material from CO2 emissions becomes financially viable, economic, or even just thermodynamically favored? A neural network is a tool for learning a particular statistical distribution. Processes and information we don't already know don't just fall out of the training distribution, you need to run experiments and prototype your way to get to the thing you want to do. You use neural networks when you want to do things at scale, and problems that have to be tackled at scale in R&D or research work are relatively scarce. I do not doubt that machine learning and neural networks will accelerate research, but the limiting factor is still going to be humans in the loop for the forseeable future given the current state of the art (e.g. LLMs with tree-of-thought reasoning and LLM-powered agents that are easily subverted in the same way one hacks a badly written PHP application). You will have people using ML to crunch large datasets or accelerate simulation work, but that's it. Asymmetric attacks are very feasible with today's LLMs and art generators, but that harm has already come to pass. It is also not a new harm. If you don't believe me, then I've got hard evidence of Donald Trump cheating in Barack Obama's Minecraft server[3]. Also... > These include increasing the number of researchers working on safety (including “off switches” for software threatening to run out of control) from 300 or 400 currently to hundreds of thous"}],"r":[["aircAruvnKk",1],["9i1WlcCudpU",1],["JRuNA2eK7w0",1],["hEUO6pjwFOo",1],["qv6UVOQ0F44",1],["tcdVC4e6EV4",1]]},"8AyHH9G9et0":{"wy":"Shared as the pilot's own telling of a famous, amusing SR-71 anecdote that never gets old.","q":{"x":"it's pretty amusing. As told by the pilot","i":19506418,"y":"2019"},"m":[{"id":19506418,"author":"linsomniac","month":"2019-03","story":"The SR-71 Spy Plane Was So Fast, It Outran Every Missile Fired at It","stance":"rec","why":"The pilot telling the amusing speed check story.","quote":"it's pretty amusing. As told by the pilot","text":"If you haven't heard the \"SR-71 Speed Check\" story, it's pretty amusing. As told by the pilot: [VIDEO]"},{"id":16044797,"author":"sundvor","month":"2018-01","story":"When volcanic ash stopped a Jumbo at 37000 feet (2010)","stance":"rec","why":"Pilot retelling the SR-71 speed check story.","quote":"This story never gets old.","text":"Awesome. This story never gets old. I just found this recent video of it as well: [VIDEO] EDIT: Speeds mentioned in vid are 100 knots higher in the video than GP's link (1942 vs 1842)?"},{"id":29373311,"author":"TimMeade","month":"2021-11","story":"The slowest SR-71 Blackbird fly-by","stance":"reference","why":"Pilot telling the story himself.","quote":"Here is the pilot telling the story.","text":"Here is the pilot telling the story. I think it's the same pilot as top story as back seater is Walter. [VIDEO]"},{"id":29374810,"author":"missedthecue","month":"2021-11","story":"The slowest SR-71 Blackbird fly-by","stance":"reference","why":"Pilot maintains the story is true.","quote":"The pilot of the Blackbird maintains that it's true.","text":"The pilot of the Blackbird maintains that it's true. Not sure you're going to find any confirmation other than that. But it isn't some joke or story cooked up by a random person for laughs. [VIDEO]"},{"id":25735104,"author":"toomuchtodo","month":"2021-01","story":"SR-71 Blackbird's ground speed check story","stance":"reference","why":"Shul telling the ground speed check story.","quote":"Shul telling the story","text":"Shul telling the story: [VIDEO] (\"Major Brian Shul relays the true story of a ground speed check with Los Angeles Center, while piloting the SR-71 Blackbird over Southern California.\")"},{"id":24343736,"author":"larrywright","month":"2020-09","story":"Airline pilots landing at LAX report “a guy in jetpack” flying alongside them","stance":"reference","why":"First-hand account from the pilot.","quote":"this is the pilot who did it.","text":"I thought it was apocryphal as well, but this is the pilot who did it. [VIDEO] Not saying it’s impossible that he made it up or embellished, but it seems likely that it actually happened."}],"r":[["xixM_cwSLcQ",1],["BickMFHAZR0",1],["zdkyGDqU7xA",1],["-k-V3ESHcfA",1],["Wpv-Qb-dB6g",1]]},"BpsMkLaEiOY":{"wy":"HN cites it as the famous example of how over-clever smart-home devices can fail at something as basic as a smoke alarm.","q":{"x":"on an artistic level it is really well done, it is an epic battle between human and machine.","i":10899245,"y":"2016"},"m":[{"id":24042656,"author":"kyrra","month":"2020-08","story":"Google to buy 6.6% stake in ADT in home security push","stance":"rec","why":"Best demonstration of first-generation Nest Protect issues","quote":"I honestly like bradfitz's youtube video the best to show the Nest Protect (the smoke alarm) issues","text":"(googler, opinions are my own) I honestly like bradfitz's youtube video the best to show the Nest Protect (the smoke alarm) issues[0]. They have since fixed these issues, as this was the gen one protect. For your #2 (no insider knowledge), that looks like people's accounts getting hacked and then the hackers talking through them. All home camera cloud based cameras have had these cases. I think Nest moving to Google account login system for Nest is probably the best protection against this kind of thing happening. Account takeovers happen, and guarding against them at scale is hard (especially when people reuse passwords). For #1, I think the culture has changed a lot as it's been absorbed into Google's hardware division. Hopefully it's better, but I couldn't say anything of value there. [0] [VIDEO] (warning, loud video)"},{"id":10899245,"author":"rdtsc","month":"2016-01","story":"Nest Thermostat Glitch Leaves Users in the Cold","stance":"rec","why":"Epic, well-made human versus machine battle with beeping Nest alarms","quote":"on an artistic level it is really well done, it is an epic battle between human and machine.","text":"In line with this is the classic \"It Can't Be Hushed Here\" Nest bug video, by Brad Fitzpatrick from last year: [VIDEO] [Warning: contains extremely annoying Nest smoke detectors beeping. So prepare to lower your volume] Brad looks very annoyed and it was no fun for him, but on an artistic level it is really well done, it is an epic battle between human and machine."},{"id":43509403,"author":"dekhn","month":"2025-03","story":"Google will stop making the Nest Protect smoke alarm","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"At the time, Fitzpatrick worked for Google and got in a small amount of trouble for posting this video.","text":"Only took ten years: [VIDEO] At the time, Fitzpatrick worked for Google and got in a small amount of trouble for posting this video. The nest creator (Fadell) and the head of INfrastructure (Hoelze) both criticized him prominently internally. A number of googlers were suspicous and we started talking to the product managers for Nest Protect and it turned out they knew about this problem, the product was just defective. At that point, Nest more or less went on life support and Tony eventually bailed ( [LINK] ) I remember walking into an office and seeing a group of Nesters sitting under a clock that counted down until they were allowed to transfer out of Nest. My gosh, google is just terrible with physical products."},{"id":25296302,"author":"Andraia","month":"2020-12","story":"AI researcher Timnit Gebru resigns from Google","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"Brad Fitzpatrick wasn't fired for","text":"Being critical is fine. Brad Fitzpatrick wasn't fired for [VIDEO] and Google still hosts that piece of (legitimate!) criticism."},{"id":21822850,"author":"choppaface","month":"2019-12","story":"Engineer says Google fired her for notifying co-workers of right to organize","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"","text":"\"this is more like running into a meeting and then interrupting it so you can start reciting NLRB policy than it is posting a flyer\" How is this like \"running into a meeting\"?? Do Googlers visit the IRI website every day? Is this pop-up really a distraction to core work for engineering? Your party line here makes the change sound like Nest shutting down the house [1] but according to the evidence it looks more like that recruiting thing that gives you a pop-up for google.com/foobar when you enter certain search queries. [1] [VIDEO]"},{"id":15956111,"author":"gasbag","month":"2017-12","story":"Neighbor's house alarm triggers when I put my car in reverse","stance":"reference","why":"Harrowing video of a man trying to deactivate linked Nest alarms","quote":"This harrowing video leads me to believe they are.","text":"Aren't Nest Protect alarms also linked? This harrowing video leads me to believe they are. It's a couple years old at this point though. (Turn your sound down, video is a poor soul trying to deactivate them.) [VIDEO]"},{"id":11820560,"author":"metaphor","month":"2016-06","story":"Speck","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"","text":"> I'm curious how many people think this is valuable as a stand-alone device? Does it need to have it's own screen and interface? I see a device whose dominant use case would be direct, at-a-glance interaction. It doesn't strike me as sensible that a typical user would generally care about data whose practicality matters only when he/she is physically present in the datalogged space...almost as insensible as texting someone sitting right beside you. Furthermore, omission of direct UI immediately eliminates the unconnected market segment. I would imagine that \"mobile app\" isn't exactly a driving parameter when people are looking for an air quality monitor, but in today's connected age, it does seem to add modern appeal and a sense of (perhaps misguided?) relevance. > Would it make more sense built into something like a thermostat? This isn't a thermostat, which makes the proposition sound like feature creep. -Placement consideration: Think where thermostats are typically mounted in a home: places that are clear from passing obstruction but not where people generally spend much time in. Places I'd expect to find this device: kitchen counter, living room table, bedside night stand, computer desktop, garage workbench...in other words, where people linger, within arm's reach, and generally not anywhere near a thermostat. -Safety consideration: Designing a 4-20mA temperature control circuit is a straight forward affair. Designing a fault-tolerant, high-reliability, regulation-compl"},{"id":10695635,"author":"dcposch","month":"2015-12","story":"Raspberry Pi Zero vs. Elliott 405","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"","text":"You definitely don't have to write your own an OS. You use existing tools, for example FreeRTOS. It's surprisingly simple. With a microcontroller, you can have a pretty much complete understanding of the whole machine. All you need is a basic knowledge of C, and most of the people who would solder stuff to a Raspberry Pi already have that or are willing to learn. Say you're making a thermostat. By convention, you leave address 0x0 empty, since dereferencing the NULL pointer is bad form :) At address 0x4 you have a int containing the target temperature. At address 0x8 you have a int containing the current measured temperature. Your whole app uses 8 bytes of memory, plus a few more for the stack. You write a loop. It reads a few GPIOs, updates those two values, does a bit of logic to decide whether to turn on the heat, then writes one more GPIO to turn the heater on or off. GPIOs are \"general purpose IO\", metal pins that can be individually set to, say, +5V or 0V. The beauty of this is that if you write it cleanly and debug it and test it once, it will work perfectly forever. It will never throw up a popup that says \"a new version of Ubuntu is available\". It will never ask you to download 80MB of Java. It will never auto update. The server will never go down, because there is no server. It feels totally different from typical software development. You're writing a bit of C, but you're not making an \"app\" with all the complexity and flakiness that entails. You're creating an emb"},{"id":10510300,"author":"georgemcbay","month":"2015-11","story":"Google engineer writing Amazon reviews on USB-C cables that don't work","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"bradfitz has this pretty famous video \"review\" (of sorts) of Nest fire alarms","text":"bradfitz has this pretty famous video \"review\" (of sorts) of Nest fire alarms: [VIDEO] The review is extremely negative, explicitly telling people not to buy Nest products. Those are (and were at the time of that video) a Google product, as Google acquired Nest in 2014. Brad still works at Google. I'm sure Benson will be fine."},{"id":10069337,"author":"lambda","month":"2015-08","story":"I No Longer Have Any Trust in the Nest Protect","stance":"reference","why":"Man struggles to silence Nest alarms, eventually boxing them up","quote":"Here's a good video demonstrating a similar experience","text":"Here's a good video demonstrating a similar experience: [VIDEO] It is amazing how many of his attempts to silence them fail, until he finally gives up and packs them away in multiple coolers in his garage to insulate away the noise."},{"id":12638061,"author":"delegate","month":"2016-10","story":"Behind the Pixel: Google’s First Real Threat to Apple’s iPhone","stance":"critical","why":"","quote":"The reference to Nest isn't a big plus in my book. I've only heard bad things about it -","text":"The reference to Nest isn't a big plus in my book. I've only heard bad things about it - [VIDEO] This phone isn't going to kill the iPhone - in fact it would take a big screwup on the part of Apple for people to start migrating away from its products. Not that Apple is incapable of screwing up, but so far they've sailed their ship pretty well. Apart from the hardware, there's the whole ecosystem of app developers, programming environments, cloud services.. Ultimately it's about who can attract the more talented developers - and that is a lot more difficult than snapping a faster CPU or better camera in your phone."},{"id":12094633,"author":"heywire","month":"2016-07","story":"The Nest Cam Outdoor is a $199 camera with an easy-to-install magnetic base","stance":"critical","why":"","quote":"after seeing the video [1] from Brad Fitzpatrick about his experience with Nest products, I'm not sure I'd ever consider them.","text":"I don't know, after seeing the video [1] from Brad Fitzpatrick about his experience with Nest products, I'm not sure I'd ever consider them. Side note: if you watched the season premiere of Mr. Robot last night, there was a scene that reminded me of Brad's video (I'll say no more for fear of spoilers) [1] [VIDEO]"},{"id":11424024,"author":"jimrandomh","month":"2016-04","story":"Google's Nest is disabling some of its customers' old smart-home devices","stance":"critical","why":"","quote":"Nest has also burned a lot of Google's goodwill with badly designed smoke detectors","text":"This is neither the first time Google's done something like this to customers, nor the first time Tony Fadell has been directly involved. When they pulled Google Glass from the market, it was concurrent with moving the Glass team to Fadell's leadership. They didn't go quite so far as to push an update to brick devices, but they left the software in a broken state, and refused to open source their fork of Android so users could fix it themselves. Nest has also burned a lot of Google's goodwill with badly designed smoke detectors ( [VIDEO] ) and thermostats ( [LINK] ). And then there's this, on a recent HN thread: [LINK] > \"I still wake up with something like PTSD occasionally from getting yelled at and bullied by Tony Fadell almost literally every day while I was there.\" > \"Tony literally stood up at an all-hands after the Alphabet thing and said \"Fuck being Googley\" (direct quote).\" I think Google/Alphabet need to take a serious look at what Nest has been doing to their brand. A few thousand very-pissed-off people here and a few thousand very-pissed-off people there add up to a serious liability for a company like Google."},{"id":11105897,"author":"nostromo","month":"2016-02","story":"We're hearing about troubles at Nest","stance":"critical","why":"","quote":"Watch this hilarious video if you're considering buying a Nest Protect","text":"The Nest thermostat is a dream compared to the smoke detector. The smoke detector goes off all the time -- without rhyme or reason. With a normal detector, you can just pull the battery if it refuses to shut off. With Nest, you actually need a screw driver to open the battery compartment to turn it off. So, when it decides to wake up the entire house at 3am (yes, the entire house, since they all go off in unison) you'll need to go find a screw driver to get it to shut up. The one flippin' reason I bought a Nest Protect was so I could \"wave to silence\" an alarm. Well, every time it goes off, it tells me that \"this alarm cannot be silenced\" - even by pushing the power button. Not once have I been able to turn off an alarm without removing the batteries. The worst feeling is when you get an alert on your iPhone while nobody (but your pet) is home. \"Alert: your house is on fire.\" Then you rush home and realize it's a false alarm. After our fourth or fifth false alarm we returned them all and replaced them with the old-fashioned alarms. Watch this hilarious video if you're considering buying a Nest Protect: [VIDEO] (warning: loud alarms in video)"},{"id":10182516,"author":"gherkin0","month":"2015-09","story":"The Internet of Way Too Many Things","stance":"critical","why":"","quote":"The Dance of Smoke Alarm Supplication can still apply to smart smoke alarms","text":"> A friend of mine, a great cook, regularly performs what she refers to as The Dance of Smoke Alarm Supplication, wherein she and a broom jointly try to persuade the alarm gods that everything is fine. When she heard that there was a smoke alarm that would pop up a notification on her phone first so she could mute it with zero shrieking, she was very excited. The Dance of Smoke Alarm Supplication can still apply to smart smoke alarms: [VIDEO]"},{"id":10183977,"author":"georgemcbay","month":"2015-09","story":"The Internet of Way Too Many Things","stance":"critical","why":"","quote":"See bradfitz's video for an example of how \"intelligence\" can hamper something like smoke alarms","text":"While I kind of agree with the author's position in general, I found the singling-out of the Leeo (which I had previously never heard of and thus have no horse in the race of its success) a bad example. The way it is described in the article actually sounds pretty ideal to me in that it is not trying to insert all sorts of \"intelligence\" into the simple, well-understood solution that already exists in the form of a simple smoke alarm. Rather, it is merely augmenting that solution in a way that is helpful but less mission critical, without integrating extra \"intelligence\" in a way that is likely to cause software-related problems. See bradfitz's video for an example of how \"intelligence\" can hamper something like smoke alarms if the entire system is integrated and \"smart\". [VIDEO]"},{"id":9901691,"author":"choppaface","month":"2015-07","story":"Google stock spikes after reporting better than expected Q2 earnings","stance":"critical","why":"","quote":"Nest is garnering a reputation for being untrustworthy due to absurd bugs like","text":"I'm very surprised parent-of-parent got so downvoted; I agree the point isn't very rigorous (as parent argues), but parent-of-parent isn't particularly less rigorous or more incendiary than average HN comments. There are plenty of solid reasons to doubt Google given the points that parent-of-parent draws: * Android was purchased and has plenty of product sharp edges. E.g. there are several ways to send texts. There's the issue of fragmentation. The new Google Photos has embarrassing auto-tags for African-Americans. Google has a solid whole product right now, but so did RIM. They will need to push really hard on projects like Tango and Project Fi to compete in future markets. * Nest is garnering a reputation for being untrustworthy due to absurd bugs like: [VIDEO] * Google is the most published self-driving car manufacturer, but w/out a shipping product they aren't clearly a leader. All early reviews of their car indicate that it's currently too conservative. There's also an issue: do people actually want the whole product? It looks like Tesla and Mercedes will very likely beat Google to market with core self-driving features. I agree with parent-of-parent that Google appears to be trying to position itself more aggressively in many consumer product markets. Google is an excellent technology company, but most of their products have been niche hits. There's plenty of room for their competitors to leverage better customer relationships to own good chunks of the markets that Goog"}],"r":[["h3gwyHNo7MI",1],["sG9UMMq2dz4",1],["TVwKjGbz60k",1],["PIPjmmmh_os",1],["XJ9ckqCMiKk",1],["c12cYAUTXXs",1],["W71BTkUbdqE",1],["Uj-rK8V-rik",1]]},"BsVydyC8ZGQ":{"wy":"HN people cite it as evidence of how fast and responsive a well-designed microkernel desktop could be on modest hardware.","q":{"x":"Tell me why microkernels are too slow when they can only do this on 90's era hardware","i":11323235,"y":"2016"},"m":[{"id":11247186,"author":"nickpsecurity","month":"2016-03","story":"Lessons Learned from 30 Years of MINIX","stance":"rec","why":"Shows a responsive microkernel desktop handling video on 90s hardware","quote":"You'll appreciate what this microkernel-based, concurrency-oriented desktop is doing if you remember those days","text":"\"Tanenbaum mentions commentators on slashdot who never tried a microkernel. That's me and I never tried to write an OS either. \" \"Suppose I'm just interested in desktop applications. Linux is already treated as a lost cause. That's what gets me because at the moment, the microkernels don't seem like an alternative in my case. If I'm not yet convinced by the opponents, I'm looking for facts, not anecdotes or authority. \" Ahh, OK. Well, I'm guessing you probably used Windows/DOS desktops back in the 1990's, right? They were OK for apps but architecturally terrible. That meant they crashed a lot, slogged down under heavy load, and so on. UNIX and Linux didn't do much better outside of SGI's machines. Hell, were barely usable. You'll appreciate what this microkernel-based, concurrency-oriented desktop is doing if you remember those days: [VIDEO] Note: I couldn't even drag videos without them skipping back then. They do that in the demo while other stuff is playing. Too bad Microsoft had a monopoly and Jobs turned them down for Darwin in Next (later became Mac OS X). An early great one that's in all kinds of stuff is QNX microkernel. It did a POSIX-compatible implementation w/ microkernel, fast message-passing, and hard real-time. Later showed off its capabilities with a desktop on a floppy with GUI, web browser, etc. Users, including Animats (Nagle) here, said you could be doing day-to-day stuff with big compiles in background with no hit to responsiveness due to its great schedu"},{"id":3905493,"author":"beosrocks","month":"2012-04","story":"The Dawn of Haiku OS","stance":"rec","why":"Gives an idea of BeOS performance on old hardware.","quote":"Here's a rough idea","text":"dr_dank's comment on Slashdot ( [LINK] ) pretty much sums up its awesomeness: BeOS was demonstrated to me during my senior year of college. The guy giving the talk played upwards of two dozen mp3s, a dozen or so movie trailers, the GL teapot thing, etc. simultanously. None of the apps skipped a beat. Then, he pulled out the showstopper. He yanked the plug on the box. Within 20 seconds or so of restarting, the machine was chugging away with all of its media files in the place they were when they were halted, as if nothing had happened. If you've never played with BeOS on an old Pentium II, it's hard to imagine the kind of performance it was able to squeeze out of that hardware. Here's a rough idea: [VIDEO]"},{"id":33605114,"author":"pcurve","month":"2022-11","story":"'Be' is nice, end of story","stance":"reference","why":"Obligatory 90s BeOS demo","quote":"Obligatory demo from the 90s","text":"New-ish video of actual BeBox running in HD video quality [LINK] I didn't know it had functional LED light meter in front of the case. very cool. Obligatory demo from the 90s [VIDEO]"},{"id":22002169,"author":"eb0la","month":"2020-01","story":"BeOS: The Alternate Universe's Mac OS X","stance":"reference","why":"Nostalgic recollection; points to demo with GL surface playing video","quote":"I installed BeOS a long time ago on a PC. It was something ahead of the times.","text":"I installed BeOS a long time ago on a PC. It was something ahead of the times. I still remember how incredible it was the rotating cube demo where you coud drag and drop images and videos on the cube faces... it worked without a glitch on my pentium. Just found out the demo video shows the application with a GL wave surface playing a video over it: [VIDEO]"},{"id":13201218,"author":"0xcde4c3db","month":"2016-12","story":"Haiku booting in UEFI mode","stance":"reference","why":"Illustrates BeOS media capability on modest 90s hardware","quote":"Keep in mind that this was almost 20 years ago","text":"For an illustration of this, see [1]. Keep in mind that this was almost 20 years ago, so the hardware he's running is probably something like a dual-socket Pentium Pro or Pentium II platform with a 66 MHz bus and core clock speeds around 10% of what we see on modern processors, and nothing like a modern GPU (I'd guess that the graphics hardware has accelerated blitting and an overlay for the live video input, though; it's unlikely that software is pushing every pixel in that demo). [1] [VIDEO]"},{"id":13204959,"author":"zamalek","month":"2016-12","story":"Haiku booting in UEFI mode","stance":"reference","why":"BeOS mid-90s demos as precedent for Haiku software media","quote":"It follows very strongly from the BeOS demos from the mid 90s","text":"You're missing the point entirely, it could be done in hardware (I'm sure they would be happy with a PR) - but Haiku can do stuff in software that other OSes simply can't; media is just one of the better demos. It follows very strongly from the BeOS demos from the mid 90s[1]. [1]: [VIDEO]"},{"id":11798211,"author":"crazyloglad","month":"2016-05","story":"I wrote a display server, a desktop environment, and a debugging/reversing tool","stance":"reference","why":"BeOS as personal influence, compared to a modern project","quote":"One of my stronger personal influences is BeOS though","text":"Futurama reference ;) [LINK] though apparently popular enough that some people have made all-rush playlists ( [LINK] ) On-topic - definitely a cool project and one that I haven't heard about actually, added to my queue of weekly source-code readings. I'd also consider DirectFB similar, and another project that I just have in my vaguest of memories (think they used a project-name like Cairo that got google-CVed out of existence from the more recent one) from the 2001-02ish era. They tried to attack X back then, but fell victims to the driver situation (but they had arbitrarily rotated windows!). One of my stronger personal influences is BeOS though - Compare [VIDEO] to [LINK] :)"},{"id":11323235,"author":"nickpsecurity","month":"2016-03","story":"Redox – A Unix-Like Operating System Written in Rust","stance":"reference","why":"BeOS demo as evidence microkernels can be fast","quote":"Tell me why microkernels are too slow when they can only do this on 90's era hardware","text":"If we're talking tech, then your post couldn't be more wrong given my BeOS and QNX examples. Performance was equal to or better than monoliths of the time. BeOS especially destroyed competition in concurrency performance due to its architecture. QNX runs at hardware speed basically with real-time properties and POSIX support. BeOS disappeared due to Microsoft monopoly with Haiku making a OSS clone. QNX was at $40 million a year in revenue when Blackberry bought it. Green Hills and VxWorks are doing OK, too, with VxWorks making more than QNX per quarter. Both have desktops virtualizing Windows, Linux, etc on microkernels w/ Gbps throughput. I don't see why we keep getting these theoretical counters given the proven results of microkernel performance in the field. Tell me why microkernels are too slow when they can only do this on 90's era hardware: [VIDEO] Then, tell us why we should sacrifice isolation of malice and faults in favor of kernel-mode code with properties like this: [LINK] Our side produced highly reliable and secure systems plus high-performance systems. It was always done with a small group with little time. The monoliths took a decade and thousands of man hours to do the same. It's up to you people to justify why those hours were well-spent. \"while for say an exokernel the opposite is mostly true.\" An exokernel is a microkernel..."},{"id":10957020,"author":"nickpsecurity","month":"2016-01","story":"Unikernels are unfit for production","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"","text":"Not organized to be able to just throw out a reference and many disappeared over time as old web faded. It's more something you see relative to other OS's than an absolute. I really need to try to integrate all the examples sometime. Here's a few, esp from past, that give you an idea. Historical look at quite a few [LINK] Note: A number were concurrency safe, had a nucleus that preserved consistency, or were organized in layers that could be tested independently. UNIX's was actually a watered down MULTIC's & he's harsh on it there. I suggest you google it too. Burrough's B5000 Architecture (1961-) [LINK] Note: Written in ALGOL variant, protected stack, bounds checks, type-checked procedure calls dynamically, isolation of processes, froze rogue ones w/ restart allowed if feasible, and sharing components. Forward thinking. IBM System/38 (became AS/400) [LINK] Note: Capability architecture at HW level. Used intermediate code for future-proofing. OS mostly in high-level language. Integrated database functionality for OS & apps. Many companies I worked for had them and nobody can remember them getting repaired. :) Oberon System [LINK] [LINK] Note: Brilliance started in Lilith where two people in two years built HW, OS, and tooling with performance, safety, and consistency. Designed ideal assembly, safe system language (Modula-2), compiler, OS, and tied it all together. Kept it up as it evolved into Oberon, Active Oberon, etc. Now have a RISC processor ideal for it. Hansen did simi"},{"id":6522737,"author":"agumonkey","month":"2013-10","story":"Experiment in booting Linux fast","stance":"reference","why":"Old BeOS demos that still amaze","quote":"I remember how I was amazed back in the days. I'm still amazed, more than before.","text":"I didn't know about BeOS booting process, but I stumbled upon good old demos[1] recently, I remember how I was amazed back in the days. I'm still amazed, more than before. Sad. [1] [VIDEO]"}],"r":[["w-WMn_zHCVo",1],["CesHr99ezWE",1],["hBMoPUAeLnY",1],["eiYoBbEZwlk",1],["qB76jxBq_gQ",1]]},"EcKxaq1FTac":{"wy":"HN lists it among favorite nerdy documentaries and enjoys hearing Stoll tell the story himself.","q":{"x":"It was really great, getting to hear it from Mr. Stoll's mouth directly.","i":20269929,"y":"2019"},"m":[{"id":20269929,"author":"otakucode","month":"2019-06","story":"Get an Acme Klein Bottle","stance":"rec","why":"Documentary with Cliff Stoll telling the story directly","quote":"It was really great, getting to hear it from Mr. Stoll's mouth directly.","text":"Oh, yes, that is the documentary I watched. It was really great, getting to hear it from Mr. Stoll's mouth directly. He was much less enthusiastic than he tends to be in Numberphile videos, I imagine that might be down to the director of the film. It is available on YouTube if anyone cares to give it a watch! [VIDEO]"},{"id":18086859,"author":"wmat","month":"2018-09","story":"Ask HN: What are some of the best documentaries you've seen?","stance":"rec","why":"Nerdy documentary on the Cuckoo's Egg story","quote":"Some of my favourite nerdy documentaries","text":"Some of my favourite nerdy documentaries: BBS: The Documentary [LINK] The KGB, the Computer, and Me [VIDEO] 8 Bit Generation: The Commodore Wars [LINK]"},{"id":19033518,"author":"gabrielblack","month":"2019-01","story":"The Cuckoo's Egg","stance":"reference","why":"NOVA episode based on The Cuckoo's Egg","quote":"","text":"\"The book was chronicled in an episode of WGBH’s NOVA entitled “The KGB, the Computer, and Me”, which aired on PBS stations on October 3, 1990\" I think it's this one: [VIDEO] Oh,me I'm ROFL: an actor is identical to one of X-Files \"Lone Gunmen\". When he explains the beeper to his wife I needed the oxygen. It's like \"Doctor Who vs hackers\" :-)"},{"id":12403006,"author":"meatsock","month":"2016-09","story":"Olduse.net: a 30-year delayed Usenet feed (2013)","stance":"reference","why":"Movie adaptation of The Cuckoo's Egg","quote":"its fun to watch and would probably make a good drinking game.","text":"cliff stoll made an ... interesting movie of this book. its fun to watch and would probably make a good drinking game. : [VIDEO]"}],"r":[["nO5vjmDFZaI",1]]},"Nwew5gHoh3E":{"wy":"HN uses it as the go-to rebuttal to irreducible complexity, showing that even simple photoreceptors are useful and stages exist in living animals.","q":{"x":"Showing quite clearly how incremental beneficial change leads to the development of complex features.","i":34407755,"y":"2023"},"m":[{"id":34407755,"author":"yarg","month":"2023-01","story":"Using paleogenomics to elucidate 10k years of immune system evolution","stance":"rec","why":"Rebuts irreducible complexity of the eye via incremental change.","quote":"Showing quite clearly how incremental beneficial change leads to the development of complex features.","text":"Dawkins invalidating the notion that the eye is irreducably complex: [VIDEO] . Showing quite clearly how incremental beneficial change leads to the development of complex features. But it will never matter to creationists: [LINK] . (Don't even bother with that last link.) They'll always just build another straw man."},{"id":11085070,"author":"adrusi","month":"2016-02","story":"Bacterial cells are small 'eyeballs', scientists discover","stance":"rec","why":"Dawkins explains the stages of eye evolution seen in living animals.","quote":"the various stages of its evolution can be seen in animals that exist today.","text":"Actually the history of the eye has been farily well understood, and the various stages of its evolution can be seen in animals that exist today. Dawkins explains it: [VIDEO]"},{"id":18727040,"author":"gleglegle","month":"2018-12","story":"A Lens-Less Camera Built Specially for AI and Computer Vision Programs","stance":"reference","why":"Shows even simple photo-receptors are useful.","quote":"where he shows the usefulness of even simple photo-receptor designs","text":"Makes me think of Dawkins' demonstration of eye evolution, where he shows the usefulness of even simple photo-receptor designs: [VIDEO]"},{"id":6377416,"author":"Slackwise","month":"2013-09","story":"First Mechanical Gear Found in a Living Creature","stance":"reference","why":"Eye evolution explained without a designer.","quote":"An organ of great complexity, but one that is very easily explained by logical evaluation","text":"Just because something looks \"engineered\", doesn't mean it is. A perfect example is the eye. An organ of great complexity, but one that is very easily explained by logical evaluation, as well as existing living creatures and fossils: [VIDEO] If you're a software engineer, I hope you understand how procedural systems and algorithms can transform data and functions. Similar processes take place via chemistry and biological interaction. Conway's Game of Life is a good example of a simple system giving rise to complex interaction and patterns. Complex beauty can arise from the interaction of systems, but if you say to yourself that it \"must have been engineered by something greater than me\", you settle in your mind, and shortcut around critical thinking and never progress toward understanding . And that's a great, great shame."}],"r":[["v0YEaeIClKY",1]]},"UAeJHAFjwPM":{"wy":"Commenters point to it as a clear explanation of the different types of night vision and why analog image intensifiers behave differently from digital ones.","q":{"x":"You will find Veritasium's video on night vision goggles [1] very interesting.","i":38555095,"y":"2023"},"m":[{"id":40596719,"author":"alberth","month":"2024-06","story":"Night-vision lenses so thin and light that we can all see in the dark","stance":"rec","why":"Covers the three types of night vision","quote":"Vertasium has a good video on the 3-different types of Night Vision.","text":"If you want to learn more ... Vertasium has a good video on the 3-different types of Night Vision. [VIDEO] 1. image intensification 2. active illumination 3. thermal imaging"},{"id":38555095,"author":"trash_cat","month":"2023-12","story":"An/PSQ-42","stance":"rec","why":"Explains how night vision works with pros and cons by generation","quote":"You will find Veritasium's video on night vision goggles [1] very interesting.","text":"You will find Veritasium's video on night vision goggles [1] very interesting. He explains well how they work, and the pros and cons of each implementation and generation. [1] [VIDEO]"},{"id":39253734,"author":"fragmede","month":"2024-02","story":"Car insurance in America is too cheap","stance":"reference","why":"Types of night vision and their regulation","quote":"","text":"[VIDEO] There are three types of night vision, and the consumer grade active illumination stuff using infrared light is covered by EAR (Department of Commerce’s Export Administration Regulations) instead of ITAR (which is still significant, but less strict). Which doesn't detract from your point, which is that night vision is hamstrung by regulations. With all the advances in technology, the consumer grade stuff should really be more accessible because it would save lives if drivers could see better in the dark."},{"id":39198606,"author":"chillacy","month":"2024-01","story":"Apple Vision Pro review: magic, until it's not","stance":"reference","why":"Shows how analog night vision goggles work","quote":"This veritasium video at 12:00 shows how these goggles work","text":"In case anyone else finds this interesting, the DOD has night vision goggles which have nearly-zero latency (all analog), amplify much better than digital cameras, and emit very little external light (hard to spot by adversaries). This veritasium video at 12:00 shows how these goggles work: [VIDEO] That said I wouldn't be surprised if someone in the DOD was buying them for general tech exploration though."},{"id":38556092,"author":"tivert","month":"2023-12","story":"An/PSQ-42","stance":"reference","why":"Digital infrared goggles lag while image intensifiers are analog","quote":"Image intensifying googles are totally analog so there's no lag to avoid that issue.","text":"I watched this video a month ago that might explain it: [VIDEO] . Basically it said infrared goggles use digital processing, so there's at least one frame of lag for every image, which can cause those issues. Image intensifying googles are totally analog so there's no lag to avoid that issue."}]},"_ArVh3Cj9rw":{"wy":"HN commenters cite it for the social theory that reasoning evolved as a collaborative activity, and one lists it as a favorite video.","q":{"x":"is a fascinating pre-AI-era breakdown of how human reasoning works","i":41524675,"y":"2024"},"m":[{"id":41914569,"author":"purgedreality","month":"2024-10","story":"Ask HN: What's your favorite YouTube video, ever?","stance":"rec","why":"Listed among commenter's favorite YouTube videos.","quote":"Future of Reasoning -","text":"Journey to the End of Time - [LINK] Veritasium on Thermite reaction - [LINK] Problems of a Shared Office - [LINK] History of the entire world, I guess - [LINK] Colin Furze Underground Activities - [LINK] Future of Reasoning - [VIDEO] Sex in Trek - [LINK]"},{"id":41524675,"author":"owenpalmer","month":"2024-09","story":"Learning to Reason with LLMs","stance":"rec","why":"Breakdown of how human reasoning works.","quote":"is a fascinating pre-AI-era breakdown of how human reasoning works","text":"\"The Future Of Reasoning\" by Vsauce [0] is a fascinating pre-AI-era breakdown of how human reasoning works. Thinking about it in terms of LLMS is really interesting. [0]: [VIDEO]"},{"id":40430650,"author":"Leftium","month":"2024-05","story":"Ask HN: Why are comments here overwhelmingly critical?","stance":"reference","why":"Source for social theory of reasoning.","quote":"This interesting theory might help explain: reasoning is a social activity humans evolved to save time and cognitive load via division of labor.","text":"This interesting theory might help explain: reasoning is a social activity humans evolved to save time and cognitive load via division of labor. It requires negative feedback. 1. An individual's reasoning workload is reduced by forming a bias. The gaps left by this bias can be filled with others' reasoning. For example if Gail focuses on why a business idea is good and Bob focuses on why that same idea is bad, they reduced the total amount of reasoning required by 50%. 2. If strong reasons are always prepared for everything, effort is wasted when others are easy to convince. So people start with minimal effort, producing weak reasons (why the business idea is good or bad). Gail and Bob keep responding to each other's reasons with stronger reasons only until necessary. After reaching consensus further reasoning is not required. Source: [VIDEO]"},{"id":39546074,"author":"Leftium","month":"2024-02","story":"Ask HN: Slow thinkers, how do you compensate for your lack of quick-wittedness?","stance":"reference","why":"Source for social theory of reasoning.","quote":"","text":"> my brain defaults to looking for flaws in arguments rather than reasons to support them May be due to how reasoning is a social activity humans evolved to save time and cognitive load via division of labor. 1. An individual's reasoning workload is reduced by forming a bias. The gaps left by this bias can be filled with others' reasoning. For example if Gail focuses on why a business idea is good and Bob focuses on why that same idea is bad, they reduced the total amount of reasoning required by 50%. 2. If strong reasons are always prepared for everything, effort is wasted when others are easy to convince. So people start with minimal effort, producing weak reasons (why the business idea is good or bad). Gail and Bob keep responding to each other's reasons with stronger reasons only until necessary. After reaching consensus further reasoning is not required. Source: [VIDEO]"}],"r":[["uD4izuDMUQA",1],["xuCn8ux2gbs",1]]},"au0QJYISe4c":{"wy":"HN commenters recommend it as a helpful way to conceptualize why light speed is a limit.","q":{"x":"I know a lot of people find this video helpful in conceptualizing things.","i":28104559,"y":"2021"},"m":[{"id":28104559,"author":"keithnz","month":"2021-08","story":"Very fast rocket","stance":"rec","why":"Helpful for conceptualizing why you can't go faster","quote":"I know a lot of people find this video helpful in conceptualizing things.","text":"you can't go faster. I know a lot of people find this video helpful in conceptualizing things. [VIDEO]"},{"id":27820878,"author":"danparsonson","month":"2021-07","story":"Positive Energy Warp Drive from Hidden Geometric Structures","stance":"rec","why":"Everything moves at c when movement through time is included","quote":"c isn't defined as the speed of light - light travels at c, but so does everything else, when you factor in movement through time.","text":"Try this: [VIDEO] c isn't defined as the speed of light - light travels at c, but so does everything else, when you factor in movement through time."},{"id":41895992,"author":"itishappy","month":"2024-10","story":"Time is a dimension, but not like space","stance":"reference","why":"Motion as an angle with the time axis","quote":"motion can be characterized by the angle it makes with the time axis.","text":"I've heard this described as \"we all move at the speed of light.\" Also, since another way to describe alignment of two vectors is an angle, motion can be characterized by the angle it makes with the time axis. [VIDEO]"},{"id":25110145,"author":"karmakaze","month":"2020-11","story":"A Spacetime Surprise: Time Isn’t Just Another Dimension","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"","text":"An interesting journey describing spacetime. > you can’t put space (which is a measurement of distance) and time (which is a measurement of, well, time) on the same footing without some way to convert one to the other. This idea about 'equal footing' is really a hazy way of asking if these dimensions can be considered 'independent', which the article then describes as being interdependent. > The key idea is that we’re all moving through the Universe, through both space and time, simultaneously. If we’re simply sitting here, stationary, and not moving through space at all, then we move through time at a very specific rate at which we’re all familiar: one second per second. This second/second rate is 'c' the speed of light, but through the 'time' dimension of spacetime. > However — and this is the key point — the faster you move through space, the slower you move through time. The other dimensions are not like this at all: your motion through the x dimension in space, for example, is completely independent of your motion through the y and z dimensions. This point is misleading. These dimensions are like time in that if I was traveling at the speed 'c' in only the x direction, then to move in y or z directions, movement in the x direction would need to be reduced. The main difference is that we're observing these x, y, z motions in non-relativistic scales but since 1 sec/sec is already 'c', any observation of change in motion through time is relativistic. > But your total motion"}],"r":[["4_qJptwikRc",1],["98LdFA-_zfA",1],["71eUes30gwc",1],["b1JlYZQG3lI",1]]},"cIQ36Kt7UVg":{"wy":"HN commenters cite it as evidence of Musk's engineering thinking, on questioning requirements, organizational errors mirroring product errors, and Conway's Law, and to argue about whether he is a strong engineer.","q":{"x":"Here's an interesting interview the author did with Elon Musk","i":21295395,"y":"2019"},"m":[{"id":24084582,"author":"jjoonathan","month":"2020-08","story":"Chrysler's Radical Space Shuttle Design","stance":"rec","why":"Musk explains why traditional engines with two stages beat aerospikes.","quote":"It's also worth highlighting the bit where he asks Elon Musk","text":"Agreed! It's also worth highlighting the bit where he asks Elon Musk: [VIDEO] I've internally asked this question so many times, like, guys, shouldn't we maybe do an aerospike? Musk focused on the high combustion efficiency of traditional combustion chambers, combined with the high efficiency of having two stages, as the reason why they won out. Jettisoning irrelevant fuel-containing mass as you go is great for efficiency and as a bonus it makes it easy to put sea-level-optimized nozzles on the bottom stage and vacuum-optimized nozzles on the top stage."},{"id":21134253,"author":"simonh","month":"2019-10","story":"Solving the Wrong Problem (2011)","stance":"rec","why":"Musk on questioning design constraints and deleting parts that shouldn't exist.","quote":"The Everyday Astronaut had a great interview with Elon Musk after the Starship presentation","text":"The Everyday Astronaut had a great interview with Elon Musk after the Starship presentation a few days ago. They spent a bit of time talking about how much progress the team had made in very little time, and Musk's take was that it took them an awful long time to finally ask the right questions - years. Once they had formulated exactly what they were trying to do and what the actual relevant constraints were, finding the right solutions and making very rapid progress was actually not all that complicated. He talked about the tendency really good engineers have to spend a lot of effort optimising the design of things that shouldn't be there at all. Finally he banged on hard about always questioning your design constraints. He pointed out that if you get given some design constraints by another team, you should always consider their validity since it;s very unlikely they are optimal. The converse assumption would be that they are always perfect, so looking at it that way there's no reason you should assume they are right from the start. For anyone who worries about the sunk cost fallacy, bear in mind it was only about a year ago they decided to throw away all the investment they'd put into building a composite body, including a huge main body tool. [VIDEO]"},{"id":21295395,"author":"blakes","month":"2019-10","story":"Are Aerospike Engines Better Than Traditional Rocket Engines?","stance":"rec","why":"Interview showing Musk's deep interest in rocket engineering.","quote":"Here's an interesting interview the author did with Elon Musk","text":"Here's an interesting interview the author did with Elon Musk: [VIDEO] Fun to see how interested Elon is with the engineering of rockets, looks like he could talk for days if he had the time."},{"id":43148975,"author":"tim333","month":"2025-02","story":"Elon Musk Biographer: No Evidence Billionaire Has Any Intellectual Achievements","stance":"reference","why":"Offered as evidence Musk is smart about rockets.","quote":"in that video from minute 1-7 you can pretty much see how he beats the likes of Boeing","text":"If you see video of him talking about spacex he's obviously reasonably smart eg. [VIDEO] In fact in that video from minute 1-7 you can pretty much see how he beats the likes of Boeing and gets stuff out faster and cheaper than NASA. It's hard to say - ah that's a good idea, that's how he beats the other rocket scientist guys and then argue he's stupid. At least when it comes to rockets. Politics I'm less convinced."},{"id":42223657,"author":"tim333","month":"2024-11","story":"Ask HN: What gives Elon Musk's companies their edge?","stance":"reference","why":"Offered as evidence Musk is a good manager and engineer.","quote":"You can see him on video here talking about getting Starship done and judge for yourself","text":"He seems to combine a number of skills. Everyone seems to agree he's a good salesman and fundraiser. And he hires good people. But contrary to many peoples view I think he's a good manager and engineer. You can see him on video here talking about getting Starship done and judge for yourself [VIDEO]"},{"id":29277780,"author":"Ajedi32","month":"2021-11","story":"Pangea Aerospace successfully hot fire tests the first MethaLox aerospike engine","stance":"reference","why":"Musk cites low combustion efficiency as aerospike drawback.","quote":"Elon mentioned[1] low combustion efficiency as one of the major disadvantages of aerospike engines","text":"In Tim's interview with Elon Musk, Elon mentioned[1] low combustion efficiency as one of the major disadvantages of aerospike engines compared to the more traditional bell nozzles used on Raptor. I wonder if Pangea has done anything in an attempt to solve that problem. [1]: [VIDEO]"},{"id":26433915,"author":"hallqv","month":"2021-03","story":"I Shipped, Therefore I Am","stance":"reference","why":"Musk on optimizing something that shouldn't have been built.","quote":"most common problem in engineering is optimizing something that shouldn’t have been built in the first place","text":"Elon said it well in an interview regarding Starship development, “most common problem in engineering is optimizing something that shouldn’t have been built in the first place”, [VIDEO] Broaden your view. You are not put on earth to write great code, but to solve real world problems. Software development is a means to an end, not the end itself."},{"id":26513409,"author":"Ajedi32","month":"2021-03","story":"What I learned in two years of moving government forms online (2018)","stance":"reference","why":"Musk on Conway's Law and mitigating it at SpaceX.","quote":"where he talks about Conway's Law and how they mitigate it at SpaceX","text":"Reminds me of this interview with Elon Musk, where he talks about Conway's Law and how they mitigate it at SpaceX: [VIDEO] It's very hard to design an efficient system when the people writing the requirements and the people implementing them don't really understand or talk to each other."},{"id":24158965,"author":"netsectoday","month":"2020-08","story":"The New ‘Gold Rush in Space’","stance":"reference","why":"Musk invites someone to prove aerospikes are the way to go.","quote":"If anyone can show us that aerospikes are the way to go then please, do it.","text":"> water-fueled space transport After watching an Elon Musk interview in front of his starship he was saying - If anyone can show us that aerospikes are the way to go then please, do it. It'll be a gift. [VIDEO] This other company Arca has a 25-video series starting 2 years ago about their journey to build a hydrogen/oxygen (water) areospike engine. Really worth the watch. Skip to the last videos if you want to see live engine tests. [LINK]"},{"id":21849505,"author":"tim333","month":"2019-12","story":"Fast","stance":"reference","why":"Musk on speeding up Starship by deleting parts and processes.","quote":"I found Musk interesting talking about how they get the Starship type projects to progress quickly","text":"Also Gmail 1 day for the first prototype. An an aside I found Musk interesting talking about how they get the Starship type projects to progress quickly: [VIDEO] >And one of the most fundamental errors made in advanced developments is to stick to a design even when it is very complicated, and to not strive to delete parts and processes. etc"},{"id":21603528,"author":"ilkkao","month":"2019-11","story":"Tesla Cybertruck","stance":"reference","why":"Musk seems relaxed at the end.","quote":"In the end of this video","text":"In the end of this video [VIDEO] he is more relaxed that I have ever seen. I don't think there is any acting in his presentations"},{"id":21607124,"author":"canada_dry","month":"2019-11","story":"Tesla Cybertruck","stance":"reference","why":"Musk more aligned when talking geek-to-geek.","quote":"At the end, when he's talking geek-to-geek","text":"Thanks, you're right. At the end, when he's talking geek-to-geek - so to speak - his thought process and speech seem much more aligned. [VIDEO]"},{"id":21136359,"author":"Tepix","month":"2019-10","story":"The Hitchhiker’s Guide to the Galaxy: 40 years of parody and predictions","stance":"reference","why":"Musk mentions being a Douglas Adams fan.","quote":"Elon Musk is also a huge fan of Douglas Adams","text":"Elon Musk is also a huge fan of Douglas Adams, as he mentioned again in his latest interview with Tim Dodd, the Everyday Astronaut: [VIDEO]"},{"id":21220118,"author":"jv22222","month":"2019-10","story":"Broken","stance":"reference","why":"Musk on product errors reflecting organizational errors.","quote":"Product errors reflect organizational errors.","text":"> The biggest problem is communication. No one fucking communicates. Elon Musk says something interesting about this here: \"Product errors reflect organizational errors.\" [VIDEO] He's specifically talking about how the product subsystems are effectively mapped out by the product departments and that they should try to interface with each other with minimal constraints. But, my take was that there needs to be a LOT of communication between departments and an ongoing debate between them as well. Edit: The more that I think about it... This might a big reason why Musk companies defy the odds, and why it is so difficult for incumbents to catch up. The over the air updates of Tesla are a good example of hardware & software departments working together to make something very difficult to compete with (if you're a regular old school siloed company)."}],"r":[["UKxQTvqcpSg",1],["hEUO6pjwFOo",1],["AJ-auWfJTts",1],["o1dgrvlWML4",1],["bJczLlwp-d8",1],["viJt_DXTfwA",1],["PLyEO0jNt6M",1],["kF9KretXqJw",1],["eviSykqSUUw",1]]},"fBt5NuNsoII":{"wy":"HN points to it as a solid, if long, introduction to B-epsilon trees and why BetrFS is built the way it is.","q":{"x":"It's a great example of theoreticians and systems researchers working together.","i":29403597,"y":"2021"},"m":[{"id":29403597,"author":"williamkuszmaul","month":"2021-12","story":"BetrFS: an in-kernel file system that uses Bε trees to organize on-disk storage","stance":"rec","why":"Intro to B-epsilon trees and BetrFS","quote":"It's a great example of theoreticians and systems researchers working together.","text":"The website doesn't seem to mention that several of the papers on the filesystem won best-paper awards at major conferences. The paper, Optimizing Every Operation in a Write-Optimized File System, in particular, won best-paper award at FAST '16. Also, if you're interested in learning more about B^\\epsilon trees, here's a talk given by Rob Johnson a few years ago at Microsoft Research: BetrFS: A Right-Optimized Write-Optimized File System [VIDEO] In general, I think it's really cool that there is a file system that exists today (i.e., BetrFS) that uses data structures which didn't exist 25 years ago. It's a great example of theoreticians and systems researchers working together."},{"id":18210233,"author":"loeg","month":"2018-10","story":"BetrFS: An in-kernel file system that uses Bε trees to organize on-disk storage","stance":"rec","why":"Explains why BetrFS modifies the Linux page cache","quote":"Yes, it's long, but it's a pretty good presentation.","text":"This isn't completely fair, although most of it is. Some of the reasons for modifying Linux are performance enhancements to the filesystem layer that only make sense with a Bε-tree filesystem (and are not possible without patching Linux). They cover this in the hour-long talk at MS research linked elsewhere in this thread.[1] (Yes, it's long, but it's a pretty good presentation.) E.g., they describe modifying the page cache to write-through small modifications to file data, rather than dirtying the entire page and writing it back later (a form of write amplification). [1]: [VIDEO]"},{"id":38820362,"author":"ww520","month":"2023-12","story":"How the append-only btree works (2010)","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"","text":"Here're what I found on the idea of adding buffers to branch nodes. L. Arge described the Buffer Tree idea in 1995 with (a,b)-Tree for other data structures. Brodal and Fagerberg formalized it for B+Tree in 2003. There's also the more modern Bε-Tree, the B-epsilon Tree, with similar idea, used in the BetrFS file system and various DBs. Tokutek built TokuDB using a similar idea and called it Fractal Tree (which was derived from Bε-Tree). Hitchhicker Tree is the append-only version of Fractal Tree, similar to the append-only B+Tree. * L. Arge. The buffer tree: A new technique for optimal I/O-algorithms. In Proc. 4th Workshop on Algorithms and Data Structures (WADS), volume 955 of Lecture Notes in Computer Science, pages 334–345, Springer Verlag, Berlin. 1995. [LINK] * G. S. Brodal, R. Fagerberg. Lower Bounds for External Memory Dictionaries. 2003. [LINK] * Bε-Tree, [LINK] , [VIDEO] . An Introduction to Bε-trees and Write-Optimization, [LINK] , [LINK] * Tokutek. How Fractal Trees Work. 2011. [LINK] * Hitchhiker Tree. 2016. [LINK]"},{"id":29407785,"author":"pengaru","month":"2021-12","story":"BetrFS: an in-kernel file system that uses Bε trees to organize on-disk storage","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"Interesting talk, I wonder how much of the perf. advantage diminishes in a finished, production-ready implementation though.","text":"> here's a talk given by Rob Johnson a few years ago at Microsoft Research: BetrFS: A Right-Optimized Write-Optimized File System [VIDEO] Interesting talk, I wonder how much of the perf. advantage diminishes in a finished, production-ready implementation though. Comparing an 80%-complete R&D prototype mule against crash-resilient posix-compliant production filesystems is basically never a fair perf. comparison. You might find that just implementing rename and hard-links properly alone is going to kill your perf. since you dispensed with on-disk inode equivalents. Nice to see people poking at these issues nonetheless, Linux needs better filesystem options."},{"id":18207387,"author":"espeed","month":"2018-10","story":"BetrFS: An in-kernel file system that uses Bε trees to organize on-disk storage","stance":"reference","why":"Rob Johnson's MSR talk on BetrFS","quote":"Talk given by Rob Johnson [1] at MSR a few years back...","text":"Talk given by Rob Johnson [1] at MSR a few years back... BetrFS: A Right-Optimized Write-Optimized File System [VIDEO] [1] [LINK]"},{"id":12060860,"author":"gbrown_","month":"2016-07","story":"Datastructures for external memory","stance":"reference","why":"Updated BetrFS talk with delete performance fixed","quote":"It looks like there's a more up to date presentation online[1] where the delete performance issues have been fixed.","text":"This reminded me of BetrFS presented at FAST 15. It looks like there's a more up to date presentation online[1] where the delete performance issues have been fixed. [1] [VIDEO]"}]},"fHsa9DqmId8":{"wy":"Cited in threads about recommendation algorithms and clickbait as a creator's inside view of why optimizing for click-through rate shapes content.","q":{"x":"He actually made a great video a while ago explaining why professional Youtubers don't have much of a choice when it comes to clickbaiting","i":25116519,"y":"2020"},"m":[{"id":28020675,"author":"jlokier","month":"2021-07","story":"As lockdowns lift, media firms brace for an “attention recession”","stance":"rec","why":"Covers creator burnout and the push for non-platform revenue.","quote":"This video from Veritasium goes into more about Youtuber burnout, self-blame, dynamics and the drive to non-platform revenue streams","text":"This video from Veritasium goes into more about Youtuber burnout, self-blame, dynamics and the drive to non-platform revenue streams: [VIDEO] If you find the apparent contradiction between Youtube success and creators needing to built out non-platform revenue streams strange, the video gives a pretty good theory explaining why. It's possible that a small number of creators are taking most of the revenue at any particular time, and the set of outstandingly successful creators keeps changing, which it might due to the promotion algorithm. That gives all of them reasons to build out non-platform revenue streams. I'm not saying Google/YouTube aren't taking too much. But hypothetically, even if they took 0% cut, the above could still happen."},{"id":25116519,"author":"eins1234","month":"2020-11","story":"A Spacetime Surprise: Time Isn’t Just Another Dimension","stance":"rec","why":"Explains why professional YouTubers must use clickbait titles and thumbnails.","quote":"He actually made a great video a while ago explaining why professional Youtubers don't have much of a choice when it comes to clickbaiting","text":"Long time Veritasium subscriber here. He actually made a great video a while ago explaining why professional Youtubers don't have much of a choice when it comes to clickbaiting with titles and thumbnails, because it's the only way to reliably get lots of impressions and views and grow their channel: [VIDEO] It made me gain a bit of empathy for creators using these tactics on Youtube and tolerate it as long as it's not being used to promote content that's devoid of any substance, which is pretty much never the case for Veritasium videos or any of the other educational channels I subscribe to."},{"id":36439596,"author":"shortcake27","month":"2023-06","story":"DeArrow – Solving Clickbait on YouTube","stance":"reference","why":"Cited for creators needing clickbait thumbnails or getting no views.","quote":"Veritasium has a video about this","text":"Veritasium has a video about this and I’ve seen other creators like GothamChess and maybe even Tom Scott talk about how they have to create clickbait thumbnails otherwise they get no views. The YouTube algorithm punishes creators who don’t use clickbait. [VIDEO]"},{"id":30558439,"author":"CSSer","month":"2022-03","story":"We're building computers wrong [video]","stance":"reference","why":"Video about adopting clickbait more.","quote":"He made a video about how they were going to start doing it more","text":"Committed to, actually. He made a video about how they were going to start doing it more: [VIDEO] . Also, more recently: [LINK] ."},{"id":30070927,"author":"spion","month":"2022-01","story":"Censorship by Algorithm Does Far More Damage Than Conventional Censorship","stance":"reference","why":"Cited for YouTube-specific explanation of engagement incentives.","quote":"Veritasium's youtube specific explanation","text":"This article is only half-right. Some of the other things you don't see because the algorithm doesn't promote them are: - scientists and educators that make 1 hour long videos to explain how something is incorrect. Example: [LINK] (less than 0.1% reach of the original) - long threads of detailed analysis with cross-references - long educational material that explaines e.g. confounders in order to explain why some statistical results observed are not possible to interpret in a certain way The reason you don't see this kind of content is simple: the level of its engagement is low. Its not clickbaity, its not exciting, it doesn't have a rebel perspective, it doesn't claim to uncover something nobody has ever seen before. Its often kind of a downer too, pointing out other people's mistakes. Social media sites make their money through engagement. The longer you stay on their site, the more ads they can show you. As such, their default algorithms promote engagement at any cost . It turns out that this trains creators to spread clickbaity, potentially misleading, false but exciting and novel \"information\" (see paper [1] as well as Veritasium's youtube specific explanation [2]) You know what other thing spreads a lot? Outrage. Extreme reactions to spread of misinformation are also engaging. People will fight over them due to strong feelings: one side frustrated by seeing the effort put into debunking having absolutely no effect (because it doens't get promoted by the algorithms, nobo"},{"id":30142957,"author":"spion","month":"2022-01","story":"Royal Society cautions against censorship of scientific misinformation online","stance":"reference","why":"Explains shift from subscriber model to algorithmic thumbnail manipulation.","quote":"Veritasium explains the shift that happened when youtube switched to an algorithmic model from the subscriber model","text":"Just like we recognized tabloids for what they were, we need to recognize social media for being the same phenomenon disguised in a \"grassroots\" cloak (i.e. crowdsourced tabloids) There is nothing organic about the speech that happens on YouTube. Veritasium explains the shift that happened when youtube switched to an algorithmic model from the subscriber model: [VIDEO] - you need to carefully manipulate people to click your thumbnail based on the picture and the video title. Making outrageous but believable shit up to sensationalize your content is a well known tactic of tabloids (which does continue today to some extent). YouTube and other social media incentivise exactly that."},{"id":29009925,"author":"rramadass","month":"2021-10","story":"Ask HN: Whatever happened to exploring the internet?","stance":"reference","why":"Offered as explanation of platform algorithm effects.","quote":"PS: Here is Veritasium explaining the insidious effects of","text":"You Sir, are a Gentleman and a Scholar :-) >Now everything on the internet is \"content,\" a hyper optimized form of human intellectual output that is devoid of art, minimal on information, excreted instead of created, measured to the lowest common denominator, monetized yet worthless, just enough bait on the hook for the next lizard brain click. I could not have said it better myself ! The Internet has become a place to publish/share every trivial thought that has ever popped into every mediocre mind. There is no self-regulation/self-censoring anymore. The Noise is just overwhelming and it is extremely mentally fatiguing just trying to get at any kind of Signal. You just give up and start numbing your mind with all the junk available. PS: Here is Veritasium explaining the insidious effects of \"Youtube Platform\" Algorithms - [VIDEO] Now extrapolate the same idea to other dominant platforms like Facebook, Google etc. and you realize that \"The Internet\" has become a platform for Expression and not necessarily Information. PPS: The book Networked Life: 20 Questions and Answers by Mung Chiang gives an inside algorithmic view of how the above platforms work."},{"id":28266993,"author":"TchoBeer","month":"2021-08","story":"Clickbait is unreasonably effective [video]","stance":"reference","why":"Veritasium discusses thumbnail and title testing near 15:50.","quote":"He talks about it in this video at about the 15:50-16:00 mark","text":"The Veritasium channel does this with all its videos; it lets Derek test which thumbnail/titles are effective and which aren't. He talks about it in this video [VIDEO] at about the 15:50-16:00 mark"},{"id":26539804,"author":"clouddrover","month":"2021-03","story":"YouTube and Patreon Still Aren’t Paying the Rent for Most Creatives","stance":"reference","why":"Derek Muller discusses success, luck and work behind his channel.","quote":"Derek Muller has a couple of videos talking about the success of his Veritasium YouTube channel","text":"> The way most entertainers get known is just through sheer luck or connections. Derek Muller has a couple of videos talking about the success of his Veritasium YouTube channel (and work, luck, and success generally): [VIDEO] [LINK]"},{"id":26210086,"author":"garaetjjte","month":"2021-02","story":"United B772 at Denver on Feb 20th 2021, engine inlet separates from engine","stance":"reference","why":"Video on why clickbait titles improve viewership.","quote":"Veritasium also had video on this topic","text":">but I saw a video of his talking about this. He doesn't actually like these titles, but claims to have proof they improve viewership counts Veritasium also had video on this topic: [VIDEO]"},{"id":23769326,"author":"ammar_x","month":"2020-07","story":"Analysis of all YouTube trending videos in US for 2019","stance":"reference","why":"Creator explains why he shifts toward clickbait titles to thrive.","quote":"In his video [0], that youtuber explains that and why he was going to shift toward using clickbait titles in his channel.","text":"Can't agree more except for the long-videos thing. The way YouTube works now encourages clickbait crying-for-a-click titles and silly-shocked-faces thumbnails because click-through-rate plays a huge role in video success now. In his video [0], that youtuber explains that and why he was going to shift toward using clickbait titles in his channel. Because otherwise, you would not thrive on YT. For long videos, I think sometimes even 10 minutes are not enough to convey a substantial message in a video. But I think you mean those youtubers that prolong their videos longer—without added value—just for more ads. [0]: [VIDEO]"},{"id":20819061,"author":"mxscho","month":"2019-08","story":"YouTube should stop recommending garbage videos to users","stance":"reference","why":"Footnote supporting recommendations maximizing click-through and watch time.","quote":"","text":"Regardless how to define what's garbage and what's not, YouTube seems to remove demonetized videos from recommendations. Since I don't think that good content is always necessarily advertiser-friendly, a lot of good videos are just not getting any attention because a few big companies decided that they're not helpful to market their products. Example: Historical or educational videos which are about controversial topics or just have the word \"war\" in their title. Basically, their recommendations algorithm favors profit from advertising over personalized recommendations because it seems to be designed to maximize click-through rate and watch time for videos with many ads. [1] [1] [VIDEO]"},{"id":20524410,"author":"jdietrich","month":"2019-07","story":"In Search of Lost Time on YouTube","stance":"reference","why":"Cited for creators tailoring videos to click-through-rate weighting.","quote":"Most professional YouTubers complain about having to tailor their videos to suit the recommendations algorithms.","text":"Immediate counter-arguments: Most professional YouTubers complain about having to tailor their videos to suit the recommendations algorithms. Historically, many short videos were padded out to just over 10 minutes in order to reach the minimum threshold to insert a midroll advertisement; today, many videos are as long as possible to take advantage of the algorithmic prioritisation of view time over view count. There is widespread dissatisfaction with the recent necessity to use clickbait thumbnails and titles, due to YouTube's increasing weighting of click-through-rate. [VIDEO] Most analysts believe that the high weight on view time is causing the YouTube algorithms to promote the kind of videos that their heaviest users watch . The problem is that these heavy users aren't representative of the general population - they're far more likely to be interested in conspiracy theories or extremist politics. [LINK] The vast majority of YouTube video views are as a result of algorithmic decisions by YouTube - what to show in the recommendations bar, what to prioritise on the home page, what to put at the top of search results. Those algorithms are not and cannot be neutral. These algorithms aren't just shaping which videos people watch; YouTube creators freely admit to tailoring the content of their videos to fit the recommendations algorithm."},{"id":20560760,"author":"mxscho","month":"2019-07","story":"YouTube faces creator backlash","stance":"reference","why":"Footnote supporting YouTube optimizing click-through rate and watch time.","quote":"","text":"What if YouTube just does the same, but the goal of the algorithm is not to maximize like/dislike ratios, but actual click-through rate and watch time? [1] It's an ad company after all. Also, the domain of TikTok videos seems very limited in comparison to YouTube videos, so your like-based algorithm might result in bad user retention with this broad strategy of showing random videos. So the question really is: If YouTube's algorithm is more sophisticated than TikTok's and TikTok is trying to do the same, would they still be as transparent about it? [1] [VIDEO]"},{"id":20185536,"author":"eganist","month":"2019-06","story":"YouTube’s Algorithm Incentivizes the Wrong Behavior","stance":"reference","why":"Veritasium's theory of the recommendation algorithm after a viral video.","quote":"Veritasium's theory on YouTube's recommendation algorithm","text":"This seems like it takes some notes from Veritasium's theory on YouTube's recommendation algorithm which he posted after his initial reservoir shade balls video went viral. (edited for clarity) [VIDEO] for his theory."},{"id":19979485,"author":"v8engine","month":"2019-05","story":"Gmail really wants me to say yes","stance":"reference","why":"Cited on algorithms optimizing click-through rate.","quote":"Veritasium very recently spoke about algorithm optimizing for click through rate","text":"Veritasium very recently spoke about algorithm optimizing for click through rate in the context of the bad. [VIDEO] It seems okay in this case though."},{"id":19981689,"author":"rasz","month":"2019-05","story":"Gmail really wants me to say yes","stance":"reference","why":"Cited as example of optimization leading to bad outcomes.","quote":"Optimizing something that affects how other people act","text":"Let me Godwin it up >We're optimizing for click through rate. and so was Hitler in ~1923. Optimizing something that affects how other people act (same happens on YT Veritasium:My Video Went Viral [VIDEO] ) leads to bad outcomes (radicalization, race to the bottom, catering to lowest denominator etc)."},{"id":27251929,"author":"crowf","month":"2021-05","story":"You Can't Prove Everything That's True","stance":"critical","why":"","quote":"from then on he will be making clickbait style videos","text":"veritasium used to be a really good channel, but a few years ago he tasted what it was like to have a viral video. since they he basically admitted (somewhere near the end of [VIDEO] ) that from then on he will be making clickbait style videos"}],"r":[["esPRsT-lmw8",1],["o_4EX4dPppA",1],["loCBvaj4eSg",1]]},"GL77oOnrPzY":{"wy":"Praised as a jargon-light talk on advanced physics that HN cites when arguing spacetime is not fundamental.","q":{"x":"Highly recommended for anyone interested in this topic.","i":33739019,"y":"2022"},"m":[{"id":33739019,"author":"ivan_ah","month":"2022-11","story":"What is spacetime made of?","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Jargon-free talk on advanced physics and the end of spacetime","quote":"Highly recommended for anyone interested in this topic.","text":"I couple of weeks ago, I watched a really good talk by Nima Arkani-Hamed on the same topic: [VIDEO] that I discovered via [LINK] I found Nima's lecture to be a masterclass on how to explain very advanced physics topics without using unnecessary jargon, or math. Highly recommended for anyone interested in this topic."},{"id":45073625,"author":"AIorNot","month":"2025-08","story":"God created the real numbers","stance":"reference","why":"Supports claim that spacetime is not fundamental","quote":"Umm SpaceTime is likely NOT to be fundamental or continuous","text":"Umm SpaceTime is likely NOT to be fundamental or continuous [VIDEO] Also Bells Therom implies no locality or non realism which to me furthers the nail on the coffin of spacetime"},{"id":34938368,"author":"robochat","month":"2023-02","story":"Something is wrong in the state of QED [pdf]","stance":"reference","why":"Researchers pursuing alternatives to complicated Feynman diagram calculations","quote":"My hope is that an alternative approach can be found that avoids these complicated calculations","text":"The paper is written in scathing tones, by page 11 I was laughing at each new report of QED's 'perfect' agreement with whatever was the latest experimental result for the electron's anomalous magnetic moment although as it continued as far as page 14, it became sobering. However, the recent discrepancies are way down in the decimal points of the result now; when these calculations require 1000s of complicated Feynman diagrams and pages and pages of calculation, errors seem inevitable. My hope is that an alternative approach can be found that avoids these complicated calculations and in fact, researchers already seem to be working on this [1]. [1] Nima Arkani-Hamed - The End of Spacetime (Sep 2022) [VIDEO]"}],"r":[["PFDu9oVAE-g",1],["tX4H_ctggYo",1],["t4vKPhjcMZg",1],["5v1Yg6XejyE",1],["eGKFTUuJppU",1]]},"hMtu7vV_HmY":{"wy":"HN points to it as an entertaining, jaw-dropping demonstration of exposed systems, including a French dam's controls.","q":{"x":"for complete madness and awe I recommend to watch this one","i":27718341,"y":"2021"},"m":[{"id":27718341,"author":"belter","month":"2021-07","story":"Thousands of unauthenticated databases exposed on the internet – study","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Shows absurd exposed systems, including a French dam's water gate controls.","quote":"for complete madness and awe I recommend to watch this one","text":"The presentation mentioned below in the thread is not bad.Sounds like its the one you were referring to, but for complete madness and awe I recommend to watch this one: \"115 batshit stupid things you can put on the internet in as fast as I can go\" by Dan Tentler [VIDEO] There is a point where they find a large Dam in France with water gates control available in the Internet. They use the US Embassy to contact the French government department responsible and urgently do something about it. They are told: \"Not right now, we are currently on holidays...\" :-)"},{"id":43823660,"author":"belter","month":"2025-04","story":"Widespread power outage in Spain and Portugal","stance":"reference","why":"Cites exposure of a French dam's water control system.","quote":"Even the water control system for a big French Dam","text":"Even the water control system for a big French Dam: \"115 batshit stupid things you can put on the internet in as fast as I can go by Dan Tentler\" - [VIDEO]"},{"id":17432938,"author":"saulrh","month":"2018-06","story":"Ask HN: Old CS lecturer looking for advice from current and recent students","stance":"reference","why":"Example of an engaging non-dry presentation on unprotected VNC endpoints.","quote":"the unprotected VNC endpoints presentation","text":"Not sure if it'll help, since I don't have any teaching experience, but coming at this from the perspective of a student, thinking of what your students might be thinking given what they're saying: First, \"not dry\" doesn't necessarily mean \"not prepared\" or \"spontaneous\". Consider things like the SR-71 story [1] or the unprotected VNC endpoints presentation [2]. Second, keep in mind what you can give students that they can't get from a textbook or another lecture that they find on youtube. The best professors I had weren't the ones that could quote the textbook; the best professors I had were the ones that could provide context for the textbook, or tell stories that illustrated important edge cases or institutional knowledge that isn't written in any textbook. There's a huge amount of wisdom that could never be \"professional\" enough to make it into a textbook but is nevertheless fantastic for helping a student understand or remember the material. For example, consider the \"Things I Won't Work With\" series; would never get past a textbook review committee, but absolutely riveting on top of the information transfer. Third, and finally, how these relate to helping students absorb knowledge. Consider spaced repetition and the method of loci [4] and what they mean about how the human brain forms knowledge: People remember networks of knowledge, not single facts. That's why I cite war stories above - giving students a story to wrap around an idea lets them link to it from multiple "},{"id":10959256,"author":"saboot","month":"2016-01","story":"Internet of Things security is so bad, there’s a search engine for sleeping kids","stance":"reference","why":"Shows the extent of insecure internet-connected devices like industrial controls.","quote":"the situation has not improved","text":"It's much worse than just passive webcams. Some devices which were never meant to be connected to the internet are out there. Stoplights? HVAC Systems? Carwashes? Ice Rinks? POWER PLANTS? Yes! [LINK] EDIT: I looked at his more recent talk from last November ... the situation has not improved \"115 batshit stupid things you can put on the internet in as fast as I can go by Dan Tentler\" [VIDEO] Featuring Spanish Chicken Controls"}],"r":[["Wp7GAKLSGnI",1],["ghtpJnrdgbo",1],["HaEPXoXVf2k",1],["QpAhX-gsHMs",1],["3LopI4YeC4I",1],["cbSu2PXOTOc",1],["75Ju0eM5T2c",1],["48sCx-wBs34",1]]},"uBsJgceM0KI":{"wy":"It is recommended as a must-see scene in threads about unusual geometry and animation, such as Mandelbulb and 4D visuals.","q":{"x":"if you haven't seen it cannot recommend it highly enough, just wait and watch the whole thing.","i":23120701,"y":"2020"},"m":[{"id":23120701,"author":"anigbrowl","month":"2020-05","story":"4D Toys: how objects bounce and roll in 4D – paper accepted to SIGGRAPH 2020","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Ending scene of the film Annihilation.","quote":"if you haven't seen it cannot recommend it highly enough, just wait and watch the whole thing.","text":"Annihilation (2018) Spoiler, obviously: [VIDEO] - if you haven't seen it cannot recommend it highly enough, just wait and watch the whole thing."},{"id":35710715,"author":"streakfix","month":"2023-04","story":"Netflix Loses 1M Users in Spain over Password Policing","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"I only noticed when I got to the third act of annihilation.","text":"Let me add a couple 100 more to that count. Did you know that netflix doesn't support resolutions higher than 720p on windows/linux? You could subscribe to the 1080p plan (like me) and the players will stream 720p videos to you with no indication whatsoever. The only way of checking the netflix video resolution is `document.querySelectorAll('video')[0].videoWidth`. There are FF extensions but they don't work for every title. [LINK] I only noticed when I got to the third act of annihilation. [VIDEO]"},{"id":28441353,"author":"isoprophlex","month":"2021-09","story":"The Unraveling of the Real 3D Mandelbulb (2009)","stance":"reference","why":"Mandelbulb animation with bassy soundtrack.","quote":"ends with a very neat and slightly scary Mandelbulb animation...","text":"The movie \"Annihilation\" ends with a very neat and slightly scary Mandelbulb animation... Very dope Burial-esque bassy soundtrack too [VIDEO]"}]},"uKxzK9xtSXM":{"wy":"Cited for a short talk that frames graphical interfaces as point-and-grunt versus language-based Unix, and for showing Moglen's regard for Stallman.","q":{"x":"only 10 minutes long but jam packed with wisdom","i":21105937,"y":"2019"},"m":[{"id":21105937,"author":"marble-drink","month":"2019-09","story":"Learn Just a Little Awk (2010)","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Short talk framing GUIs as point-and-grunt versus language-based Unix","quote":"only 10 minutes long but jam packed with wisdom","text":"There is a short talk by Eben Moglen [0] which is only 10 minutes long but jam packed with wisdom. One part which changed the way I think about using computers is how he describes GUI interfaces: as a \"point and grunt\" or \"caveman\" interface. Using UNIX, on the other hand, is to use language to communicate with the computer and comes with all the benefits that we have when using it to interact with people. [0] [VIDEO]"},{"id":26811838,"author":"d_tr","month":"2021-04","story":"Free Software Foundation and RMS issue statements on Stallman’s return","stance":"reference","why":"Cited as showing Moglen's regard for Stallman","quote":"","text":"> He's actively covering up Epstein's crimes. As Andrew_nenakhov said, you can repeat this all you want. Specifically this is the most ridiculous accusation which has been debunked to death. You might want to try going over the actual facts again. > All it does is taint the movement. If the perception of a \"taint\" is based on lies spread by a bunch of idiots on Twitter and some \"journalists\", this is not Stallman's fault. As for \"the movement\" itself, Stallman is a thought leader and an uncompromising idealist who left MIT to pursue it. He is the kind of person people like Eben Moglen [1] considered a giant. Stallman's statement speaks volumes about his character. Unless the damage and the harm inflicted upon him by the mob and the lies is fully repaired, I do not care about whatever actually valid reason someone might have to get him to step down. [1] [VIDEO]"},{"id":15266443,"author":"loup-vaillant","month":"2017-09","story":"Alan Kay is still waiting for his dream to come true","stance":"reference","why":"Source of the point-and-grunt caveman interface idea","quote":"","text":"> (and by execution I don't just mean invention of a prototype but actual productization and distribution) So, the STEPS project I mentioned above¹ doesn't count? Bret Victor's work wouldn't count? That's incredibly short sighted. You seem to have missed the part where it's really really hard to be successful with unnatural stuff, even when that stuff is unbelievably useful and valuable. Writing is unnatural, and took a long time to spread. It's still not there yet, and with the television and YouTube, we're actually reverting back a little bit. Computing is even less natural, so instead of a Victor-esque interactive wonder, we got the caveman interface, where you point and you grunt². Speaking of badmouthing, my heart breaks every time someone implies that recent Apple devices are cool. They're dystopian demons that seduced people into giving into thinking digital prisons are cool. The Apple ][ was cool. The iPad is crap (it could be cool, with the right OS). [1]: [LINK] [2]: [VIDEO]"}]},"yftBiNu0ZNU":{"wy":"Used in AI threads as a cautionary example of harm from LLM self-diagnosis, though some commenters cite it as part of broader criticism of AI.","q":{"x":"please watch this video from ChubbyEmu, a real doctor, about a case study from someone who self-diagnosed using AI","i":46539393,"y":"2026"},"m":[{"id":46539393,"author":"iLoveOncall","month":"2026-01","story":"Introducing ChatGPT Health","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Doctor's case study of self-diagnosis using AI gone wrong.","quote":"please watch this video from ChubbyEmu, a real doctor, about a case study from someone who self-diagnosed using AI","text":"Since there are a lot of positive comments at the top (not surprising given the astroturfing on HN), please watch this video from ChubbyEmu, a real doctor, about a case study from someone who self-diagnosed using AI: [VIDEO] For every positive experience there are many more that are negative, if not life threatening or simply deadly."},{"id":46576736,"author":"Imustaskforhelp","month":"2026-01","story":"Google: Don't make \"bite-sized\" content for LLMs","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Man replaced salt with sodium bromide on ChatGPT advice.","quote":"definitely worth a watch","text":"xD I am not even kidding but there is a guy who viewed twitter, found that table salt Aka sodium chloride is \"bad for health\" and the medical study recommends that if thats the case then they should less the consumption But he ends up asking chatgpt and it somehow recommends him the idea of sodium bromide instead of sodium chloride and it really ended up having him have so many hallucinations and so many other problems that the list goes on. I found this from a video, definitely worth a watch [VIDEO] A man asked AI for health advice and it cooked every brain cell"},{"id":46815496,"author":"Joel_Mckay","month":"2026-01","story":"My Mom and Dr. DeepSeek (2025)","stance":"reference","why":"Linked as an example of AI health advice harm.","quote":"","text":"Indeed, real doctors have the advantage of understanding how to treat humans that are incapacitated. =3 [VIDEO]"},{"id":46211174,"author":"lenerdenator","month":"2025-12","story":"I misused LLMs to diagnose myself and ended up bedridden for a week","stance":"reference","why":"Case review of bromism after consulting an LLM.","quote":"recently released a video about a man who suffered a case of brominism (which almost never happens anymore) after consulting an LLM.","text":"I think you'll see this happen a lot more. Not just in the US where docs cost money, but anywhere there's a shortage of docs and/or it's a pain in the butt to go to one. YouTuber ChubbyEmu (who makes medical case reviews in a somewhat entertaining and accessible format) recently released a video about a man who suffered a case of brominism (which almost never happens anymore) after consulting an LLM. [0] [0] [VIDEO]"},{"id":47264945,"author":"Imustaskforhelp","month":"2026-03","story":"US tech firms pledge at White House to bear costs of energy for datacenters","stance":"critical","why":"","quote":"A search engine which can make some pretty wrong cases which can literally lead to near death like scenarios","text":"> Average people seem to think they are fucking awesome Average people who wants to go home from work and game are fucking angry at AI for raising the ram prices. Average person who wants to own the stuff and not have things on cloud are fucking angry at AI for raising prices 5 times in such a short period of time. Have you talked to an average person and how they use AI? They use it as a glorified no-code editor and search engine. A search engine which can make some pretty wrong cases which can literally lead to near death like scenarios all while being completely trust me bro attitude. A man asked AI for health advice and it cooked every brain cell: [VIDEO] Normal people use AI to confide in it secrets, seek therapy somehow. And the same AI generates AI pyschosis. Now coming to tech industry: Tech industry is worried about that such levels of democratization just means that nobody is going to pay for them yet at the same time, we will see projects who are completely created by AI seek money. It's this weird mush where if you are a genuine guy who just loved computing, who loved tinkering, yeah we're offloading that capability to AI I have seen this even more and more with as agents want to get more autonomous or we are letting them be. The projects generated feel hollow to me. I don't consider myself a full fledged programmer right now and AI did supercharge me and made me have projects. Nowadays, it just feels like prompt ---> (Time) --> Output. It just feels hollow and AI"},{"id":46564821,"author":"Joel_Mckay","month":"2026-01","story":"The No Fakes Act Has a \"Fingerprinting\" Trap That Kills Open Source","stance":"critical","why":"","quote":"these LLM businesses are simply unsustainable fictions.","text":"These firms can do what they like if and only if they pay for every $7B reactor, the 30k year waste stewardship, and disconnect from community resources people paid for with taxes. However, currently the unethical burden cities with the endless bill for resources, contribute no actual value, and one may spot the data center waste heat signatures and industrial run-off from space. Consider most \"AI\" firms lost on average $4.50 for every new user, rely on overt piracy, and delusional boards sand-bagging for time... these LLM businesses are simply unsustainable fictions. Many problems don't have simple answers, but one may merely profit by their predictable nature. I would recommend volunteering with a local pet rescue society if you find yourself getting upset about trivia. Have a great day. =3 [LINK] [LINK] [LINK] [VIDEO] [LINK]"},{"id":46805633,"author":"Joel_Mckay","month":"2026-01","story":"Please Don't Say Mean Things about the AI I Just Invested a Billion Dollars In","stance":"critical","why":"","quote":"but it doesn't have to be \"AGI\" to cause real harm.","text":"LLM is not real AI, would take 75% of our galaxy energy to reach human level error rates, and is economically a fiction.... but it doesn't have to be \"AGI\" to cause real harm. [LINK] The damage is already clear =3 [LINK] [VIDEO] [LINK]"},{"id":46223284,"author":"Joel_Mckay","month":"2025-12","story":"Is it a bubble?","stance":"critical","why":"","quote":"Drives people insane","text":"LLM slop doesn't have aspirations at all, its just click bait nonsense. [LINK] Drives people insane: [VIDEO] And LLM are economically and technologically unsustainable: [LINK] These have already proven it will be unconstrained if AGI ever emerges. [LINK] The LLM bubble will pass, as it is already losing money with every new user. =3"},{"id":46274639,"author":"Joel_Mckay","month":"2025-12","story":"If AI replaces workers, should it also pay taxes?","stance":"critical","why":"","quote":"An imperfect mirror shows only the irrational what they wish to see.","text":"If a task involves zero workmanship, than the opportunity cost is near $0 anyway. To be clear, LLM is not real \"AI\", not sustainable, and already losing money with every new user. An imperfect mirror shows only the irrational what they wish to see. [VIDEO] I look forward to likely having a cluster of heavily discounted GPUs in a few years. =3"}],"r":[["XZ3w_jec1v8",1],["k1BneeJTDcU",1],["1sl5KJ69qiA",1]]},"21qPOReu4FI":{"wy":"Pointed to as a well-done introduction to constructive mathematics when it comes up in logic and proof assistant threads.","q":{"x":"Here are two versions of the same well-done talk, from Andrej Bauer","i":18413963,"y":"2018"},"m":[{"id":18413963,"author":"jimhefferon","month":"2018-11","story":"The weird and wonderful world of constructive mathematics (2017) [pdf]","stance":"rec","why":"Andrej Bauer's well-done talk introducing constructive mathematics","quote":"Here are two versions of the same well-done talk, from Andrej Bauer","text":"There are a lot of folks here asking for a bit more. Here are two versions of the same well-done talk, from Andrej Bauer: a video [VIDEO] , and a paper [LINK] (sorry, the paper link may not work for everyone, but the video contains basically the same content.)"},{"id":47924993,"author":"leonidasrup","month":"2026-04","story":"\"Why not just use Lean?\"","stance":"reference","why":"Andrej Bauer's IAS talk on constructive mathematics","quote":"A talk about constructive mathematics by Andrej Bauer at the Institute for Advanced Study.","text":"Five stages of accepting constructive mathematics: Denial Anger Bargaining Depression Acceptance A talk about constructive mathematics by Andrej Bauer at the Institute for Advanced Study. [VIDEO] [LINK]"},{"id":18861308,"author":"no_identd","month":"2019-01","story":"Unprovability comes to machine learning","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"","text":"That depends on what you mean by \"Church-Turing thesis\": * [LINK] The Interactive Nature of Computing: Refuting the Strong Church-Turing Thesis (Published version here: [LINK] ) * [LINK] See also a previous hacker news discussion of the Church-Turing thesis here: [LINK] albeit I take issue with the supposed \"proof\" by counter example via Compass & Straightedge construction given in the video there, due to results like these two: * [LINK] Kellison, Ariel; Bickword, Mark; Constable, Robert L. - Implementing Euclid’s straightedge and compass constructions in type theory [September 2018] (Code here: [LINK] ) * [LINK] (Numerous papers by Michael Beeson et al., just check the page, I won't link them all here) (Note that the authors of the two above works take very different yet similar approaches to the whole matter, it really pays off to compare both to each other.) However, there seem to exist at least two different spanners in the works: * [LINK] Makowsky, Johann A. - Can one design a geometry engine? On the (un)decidability of certain affine Euclidean geometries * [LINK] Makowsky, Johann A. - The Undecidability of Orthogonal and Origami Geometries (If the first of the two above papers makes you wonder \"well who DIDN'T overlook Ziegler's theorem... Well... [LINK] ) Oh and in case anyone here still believes Hilbert's old wives tale about /his/ Axiom of Choice, there's plenty of AC to go around in constructive mathematics: [LINK] But if that doesn't convince you yet, you might go "}]},"3MYEtQ5Zdn8":{"wy":"HN uses it as the source of the scout mindset vocabulary when discussing honest reasoning and conversation.","q":{"x":"This video might be informative","i":28136664,"y":"2021"},"m":[{"id":28136664,"author":"n8cpdx","month":"2021-08","story":"TikTok overtakes Facebook as most downloaded app","stance":"rec","why":"","quote":"This video might be informative","text":"As a queer person who uses TikTok, I think you should take some time to challenge your assumptions and get some more information. This video might be informative: [VIDEO] I should correct a mistake I made earlier: all of my numbers cited are likes not views. The TikTok promoting vaccines was seen 3.5 Million times. There are many queer creators with videos in the millions of views, and many more with videos in the hundreds of thousands. Still, I don't think its reasonable to expect niche content from obese people to perform as well as generic content from normative people. I also don't think its reasonable to expect trans content to perform as well, given that a substantial portion of the US population still doesn't want to recognize or legalize their existence. Yet, there is an abundance of queer content (content that sought me out, I never told TikTok that I'm gay), much of it quite niche. TikTok sends me enough gay male twink armpit fetish content that I don't realistically think you can say they're making a serious effort to suppress it. If you're focusing on hashtags and not algorithm suggestions, I think you don't have a strong grasp of how TikTok works. All of the cited sources are quite old, so perhaps it was too early for folks to actually have time to use and understand the service. Your claim that there is nothing threatening to China or TikTok economically is suspect, given the algorithm has also suggested Uighur muslim genocide content: [LINK] - there are many mo"},{"id":33710882,"author":"gnramires","month":"2022-11","story":"Effective Altruism Is about Getting Rich with a Clean Conscience","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"a few sources like Julia Galef (which is popular for the Scout Mindset[3])","text":"I consider myself close to rationality and EA, so maybe I have to explain myself here. First, I call myself rationalist because of the culture around the blog SlateStarCodex (and r/<same name>[1]), by Scott Alexander (now at AstralCodexTen[2] for interesting reasons), and a bit less lesswrong.com , and also a few sources like Julia Galef (which is popular for the Scout Mindset[3]) and some people around this movement. The ideas are fairly tame and simple around encouraging clear thinking and avoiding biases -- but they go fairly deep into promoting an understanding how our brains and minds work (frequently discussed in SSC). I am partly defined by this now, I learned a lot about myself and in some ways improved my writing and why not thinking (yes, thinking more effective in general terms is not as well defined as it seems, and not nearly as effective as it seems either -- but in a certain sense I think it's good). I have not went through EY's extensive work to critique or embrace it. I think this movement is partly a response to the very damaging ideological war environment of the late 2010s and still ongoing that creates divided societies and not bringing us closer to a move charitable, loving, understanding, good future (instead lost in endless culture wars -- that in a way became very real wars with millions of affected). EA is a nice sort of consensus result from (part?) of the community as how one should approach charity and ethics (but none of the original EAs came fro"},{"id":27814974,"author":"afarrell","month":"2021-07","story":"Tact Filters (1996)","stance":"reference","why":"Scout mindset as a signal to ease conversation","quote":"Send signals that you are in \"scout mindset","text":"If you want to encourage people to be less tactful with you, there is a wrong way to do so and there is a right way to do so. The wrong way is to get impatient with their attempts at tact and act as if they would given additional time and patience, be more manipulative. This assumes that manipulation requires more space & time because it is highly intentional. In my experience, most manipulative speech is a fast emotional (perhaps system 1) reaction to a situation where a fearful speaker is desperately trying to establish control of the outcome of an interaction. It often strives to protect the speaker’s own ego. Tact is exerting the mental effort to be clear about what one is not trying to say about someone else's ego while being clear about what one is trying to say about the facts of a situation. If you try to get others to be tactless by impatience, their fact-clarity will drop alongside their ego-preservation. So if you wish another to speak with less tact, the right way to do that is to set them at ease by saying things that indicate you have a low ego attachment to the topic and that you are willing to re-think your own beliefs. Then, they can redirect their mental RAM to creating increased fact-clarity. In short: Send signals that you are in \"scout mindset\" [VIDEO] ."},{"id":25239986,"author":"lukifer","month":"2020-11","story":"Most Americans Object to Government Tracking Their Activities Through Cellphones","stance":"reference","why":"Galef's scout versus soldier mindset","quote":"what Julia Galef refers to as \"scout mindset\" rather than \"soldier mindset","text":"To be clear: I was neither advocating for or against lockdowns. I'm advocating thoughtfulness of trade-offs, informed by (but not limited to) data; as opposed to rigid ideology, or knee-jerk gut reactions. And in particular: to recognize that even if one believes that the data of a specific real-world circumstance clearly favors one end of the spectrum, there are also circumstances where the data can break the other way. Therefore we need to be tolerant and open-minded to good-faith skepticism in either direction. The fact that a wrong answer can be fatal makes it all the more important to avoid what Daniel Schmachtenberger refers to as \"limbic highjacking\", or more colloquially, \"being triggered\" (which includes both fear of death, and fear of losing autonomy). > ends justifying the means... the trend of a growing security state It's also worth remembering that these are not mutually exclusive: that circumstances exist where the ends do justify the means, yet are also used as a cynical power grab with additional negative externalities. Again, the only way to know the difference is dispassionate discernment (what Julia Galef refers to as \"scout mindset\" rather than \"soldier mindset\" [0]). [0] [VIDEO]"}]},"3eyJIbSgdSE":{"wy":"HN uses it as a reference for Friedman's argument that generous welfare and open immigration are in tension.","q":{"x":"Milton Friedman discussed this fundamental contention between welfare and free immigration","i":8795013,"y":"2014"},"m":[{"id":12517054,"author":"abz10","month":"2016-09","story":"EU's Tusk Says May Expects to Start Brexit Talks by February","stance":"rec","why":"Friedman on welfare state versus immigration","quote":"Milton Friedmen convers it pretty well in","text":"It's fair to bemoan the general decline of your society with the desire to better your personal lot through immigrating. It's likely that they don't dislike foreigners but they do dislike being forced to subsidise them via taxes. It's a side effect of the welfare state. Milton Friedmen convers it pretty well in [VIDEO] . It's an old problem :/"},{"id":8795013,"author":"tolmasky","month":"2014-12","story":"The Canadian Mincome Experiment of 1974-79","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"Milton Friedman discussed this fundamental contention between welfare and free immigration","text":"One potential side effect of a minimum income is to make the U.S. (or any country implementing it), even more immigrant hostile. These programs usually only consider the effects in a vacuum, not with respect to much larger population that wants to get in. Either immigration needs to be restricted, or you have a \"lower\" non citizen class that doesn't get these benefits from the beginning (ironically perpetuating more inequality). Milton Friedman discussed this fundamental contention between welfare and free immigration, and it's coveted a little here: [VIDEO] ."},{"id":2495612,"author":"olalonde","month":"2011-04","story":"Why Immigrant Entrepreneurs Are Leaving the U.S","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"Some interesting thoughts on immigration from economist Milton Friedman","text":"Some interesting thoughts on immigration from economist Milton Friedman ( [VIDEO] ): I’ve always been amused by a kind of a paradox. Suppose you go around and ask people: ‘The United States before 1914, as you know, had completely free immigration. Anybody could get in a boat and come to these shores and if landed at Ellis Island he was an immigrant. Was that a good thing or a bad thing? You will find that hardly a soul who will say that it was a bad thing. Almost everybody will say it was a good thing. ‘But what about today? Do you think we should have free immigration?’ ‘Oh, no,’ they’ll say, ‘We couldn’t possibly have free immigration today. Why, that would flood us with immigrants from India, and God knows where. We’d be driven down to a bare subsistence level.’ What’s the difference? How can people be so inconsistent? Why is it that free immigration was a good thing before 1914 and free immigration is a bad thing today? Well, there is a sense in which that answer is right. There’s a sense in which free immigration, in the same sense as we had it before 1914 is not possible today. Why not? Because it is one thing to have free immigration to jobs. It is another thing to have free immigration to welfare. And you cannot have both. If you have a welfare state, if you have a state in which every resident is promises a certain minimal level of income, or a minimum level of subsistence, regardless of whether he works or not, produces it or not. Then it really is an impossible th"}]},"8Gv0H-vPoDc":{"wy":"HN mostly drops it jokingly in threads about grammar, apostrophes and language, and occasionally discusses the animated video's making.","q":{"x":"I think Weird Al's version is better and more educational than the original","i":22820479,"y":"2020"},"m":[{"id":22820479,"author":"DonHopkins","month":"2020-04","story":"Jarrett Heather presents: Word Crimes (2014)","stance":"rec","why":"Catchy, educational parody with behind-the-scenes animation story.","quote":"I think Weird Al's version is better and more educational than the original","text":"Jarrett Heather is the artist behind Weird Al's \"Word Crimes\" video released in 2014 (at 48.4 million views now). Word Crimes: [VIDEO] \"Word Crimes\" is Weird Al's spot-on parody of Robin Thicke's \"Blurred Lines\" with T.I. and Pharrell Williams. I think Weird Al's version is better and more educational than the original -- smart and catchy like a modern Schoolhouse Rock. Blurred Lines: [LINK] Weird Al contacted Jarrett Heather after being impressed by \"Shop Vac\", his previous work with kinetic text (typographic animation), which he made using animation tools like AfterEffects. Shop Vac: [LINK] This page on Jarrett Heather's web site tells the story and shows the art and technology behind the \"Word Crimes\" video. He's also published the Animatic storyboard-in-motion that took about 100 hours, to Weird Al's original home-made demo of the song! It's fascinating to compare them, and see how their ideas evolved from storyboard to final video. Jarrett Heather presents: Word Crimes: [LINK] >The Completed Music Video: In November 2013, \"Weird Al\" Yankovic asked me to direct an animated video for \"Word Crimes\", a parody of Robin Thicke's \"Blurred Lines\" about the supposed abuse of proper language. >The result of 500 hours of work in After Effects, Photoshop, Illustrator and Premiere goes by in 3 minutes, 44 seconds. I hope you find each one of them entertaining. The Animatic: [LINK] >This storyboard-in-motion took about 100 hours. Al signed off on this design on January 25th, 2014, onl"},{"id":38964005,"author":"DonHopkins","month":"2024-01","story":"Why Prince Changed His Name to an Symbol 30 Years Ago, and What Happened Next","stance":"reference","why":"Word Crimes lyric about Prince cited in tangential thread.","quote":"","text":"[LINK] [LINK] [VIDEO] [LINK] [LINK] jcl on April 9, 2020 | parent | context | favorite | on: Jarrett Heather presents: Word Crimes (2014) Love the song and video -- great to see the behind-the-scenes and in-jokes. One thing I'm still curious about is the lyric: \"You should never / write words using numbers / unless you're seven / or your name is Prince\" -- particularly, whether it was an intentional reference to the movie \"Se7en\". If it was, it seems like a missed opportunity in the video. DonHopkins on April 9, 2020 | prev [–] Funny, I thought the symbol for The Artist Formerly Known As Prince was informally pronounced and spelled \"BRUCE\", which is less cumbersome to speak and spell than the official alternative (which is unspeakable unspellable silence). His PR company sent this memorandum around to the press and industry, including step-by-step downloading, installation, and usage instructions for Macintosh and PC, of a special [BRUCE] font with just one unpronounceable [BRUCE] symbol, to be used when referring to The Artist in print. I guess Weird Al didn't get the memo. [LINK] >Why, the ``Bruce'' font? Because someone jokingly suggested that because it was way too cumbersome to say, ``that symbol guy'' or whatever, it'd be much easier to give the symbol a name, and that name should be ``Bruce.'' So there. >[BRUCE] Background: On June 7, 1993, mega-star Prince surprised fans and the entertainment industry when he announced that he was separating from his band New Power Ge"},{"id":35838769,"author":"DonHopkins","month":"2023-05","story":"Wikipedia users edits over 90k uses of “comprised of”","stance":"reference","why":"Behind-the-scenes story of the Word Crimes animated video.","quote":"","text":"Speaking of heroes who I could care less about: Jarrett Heather presents: Word Crimes (2014) (jarrettheather.com) Archive: [LINK] \"Weird Al\" Yankovic - Word Crimes: [VIDEO] >The Completed Music Video: In November 2013, \"Weird Al\" Yankovic asked me to direct an animated video for \"Word Crimes\", a parody of Robin Thicke's \"Blurred Lines\" about the supposed abuse of proper language. >The result of 500 hours of work in After Effects, Photoshop, Illustrator and Premiere goes by in 3 minutes, 44 seconds. I hope you find each one of them entertaining. Word Crimes Animatic: [LINK] >This storyboard-in-motion took about 100 hours. Al signed off on this design on January 25th, 2014, only 3 weeks after he gave me his homemade \"demo\" for Word Crimes, which you can hear on the animatic soundtrack. >If you watch very closely, you might notice a gag or two that didn't make it through to final animation or some very subtle changes in the lyrics. HN Discussion: [LINK] MrHeather on April 9, 2020 | parent | next [–] When I first met with Al about this project, I was quick to point out that linguists would disagree with about a third of the \"advice\" he's giving out. His immediate reply was \"WELL THEY'RE WRONG\"--really loudly in the \"Weird Al\" character voice. In my mind the joke is that the song's narrator is a know-it-all character that shouldn't be taken entirely seriously. But on the other hand, a lot of educators have contacted me to tell me they use the song as a learning tool. DonHopkins on"},{"id":22766444,"author":"DonHopkins","month":"2020-04","story":"Jarrett Heather presents: Word Crimes (2014)","stance":"reference","why":"Kinetic typography video behind Weird Al's Word Crimes.","quote":"Jarrett Heather is the artist behind Weird Al's \"Word Crimes\" video released in 2014","text":"Jarrett Heather is the artist behind Weird Al's \"Word Crimes\" video released in 2014 (at 48.4 million views now). Word Crimes: [VIDEO] Weird Al's spot-on parody of Robin Thicke's \"Blurred Lines\" with T.I. and Pharrell Williams: [LINK] Weird Al contacted him after being impressed by \"Shop Vac\", his previous work with kinetic text (typographic animation), which he made using animation tools like AfterEffects. Shop Vac: [LINK] This page on Jarrett Heather's web site tells the story and shows the art and technology behind the \"Word Crimes\" video. He's also published the Animatic storyboard-in-motion that took about 100 hours, to Weird Al's original home-made demo of the song! It's fascinating to compare them, and see how their ideas evolved from storyboard to final video. Jarrett Heather presents: Word Crimes: [LINK] >The Completed Music Video: In November 2013, \"Weird Al\" Yankovic asked me to direct an animated video for \"Word Crimes\", a parody of Robin Thicke's \"Blurred Lines\" about the supposed abuse of proper language. >The result of 500 hours of work in After Effects, Photoshop, Illustrator and Premiere goes by in 3 minutes, 44 seconds. I hope you find each one of them entertaining. The Animatic: [LINK] >This storyboard-in-motion took about 100 hours. Al signed off on this design on January 25th, 2014, only 3 weeks after he gave me his homemade \"demo\" for Word Crimes, which you can hear on the animatic soundtrack. >If you watch very closely, you might notice a gag or two tha"},{"id":41790008,"author":"bitwize","month":"2024-10","story":"Germans decry influence of English as 'idiot's apostrophe' gets approval","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"","text":"Say that you have i-t followed by apostrophe s, now what does that mean? You would not use \"it's\" in this case! As a possessive It's a contraction What's a contraction? Well, it's the shortening of a word or group of words by omission of a sound or letter. -- \"Weird Al\" Yankovic, \"Word Crimes\" [VIDEO]"},{"id":38309184,"author":"ShadowBanThis01","month":"2023-11","story":"Apostrophe Protection Society","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"And now, Weird Al has some words","text":"In a time when a more and more people can't spell \"too,\" it seems ambitious to school them on \"less\" vs. \"fewer.\" But when I do try to explain why \"less\" and \"fewer\" are not interchangeable, I usually just say, \"Do you want fewer syrup on your pancakes?\" Then there's \"transition\" as a verb: Do you illumination a room when it's dark? We can go on and on, but we can't leave this one out: \"would of.\" I always call that out in any comment forum. This just can't be let go. And now, Weird Al has some words: [VIDEO]"},{"id":23005648,"author":"DonHopkins","month":"2020-04","story":"Maybe visual programming is the answer, maybe not","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"","text":">Because the question is obvious. Who doesn't know Knuth? As for Scott Kim, and the rest, I don't know them [...] Let me get this straight: you were obviously being sarcastic about not knowing Donald Knuth, but you weren't being sarcastic about not knowing the other people you were criticizing. [\"I actually (honestly) haven't heard of them.\" -crimsonlucard] That's some implausibly nuanced retroactively selective sarcasm there! You make as much sense as Trump claiming he was being sarcastic about injecting bleach, but in the same rambling sentence he wasn't being sarcastic about sticking a UV flashlight where the sun doesn't shine. Do you believe him? >[...] and could care less. [VIDEO] >\"I hate these word crimes. Like \"I could care less\". That means you do care. At least a little. Don't be a moron. You'd better slow down. And use the right pronoun. Show the world you're no clown.\" -Weird Al"},{"id":22095994,"author":"Buge","month":"2020-01","story":"Most Amazon-Owned Private Label Brands Have No Warranties","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"","text":"When there's a word with 2 meanings that are almost opposites of each other, that's confusing. I think it's good to avoid using words in confusing ways like that. [VIDEO]"}],"r":[["v4Wy7gRGgeA",1],["RxD6H3ri8RI",1],["4nigRT2KmCE",1],["helScS3coAE",1],["KVN_0qvuhhw",1],["yMjPqr1s-cg",1],["z_dt7NG38V4",1],["whPWKecazgM",1],["pqQSwQLDIK8",1],["AdNJ3fydeao",1],["wpOxbesRNBc",1],["7hwO8Q_TyCA",1],["mZyvIHYn2zk",1],["I4flQ8XdvJM",1],["8QiPFmIMxFc",1],["1iAJPoc23-M",1],["Jd3-eiid-Uw",1]]},"8eT1jaHmlx8":{"wy":"Cited in hardware threads as a Keller talk on the different ways of doing parallel processing.","q":{"x":"Jim Keller had an interesting talk recently [1] about ways of doing parallel processing","i":23810093,"y":"2020"},"m":[{"id":23810093,"author":"tails4e","month":"2020-07","story":"Linus Torvalds on AVX512","stance":"rec","why":"Keller on scalar, vector, and GPU-style parallel processing approaches","quote":"Jim Keller had an interesting talk recently [1] about ways of doing parallel processing","text":"Jim Keller had an interesting talk recently [1] about ways of doing parallel processing to better us the billions of transistors we have - assuming the task is parallelizable. There's the scalar core (i.e the basic CPU) which is easy to program realtively. Then a scalar core with vector instructions - difficult to program efficiently. Then there are arrays of scalar cores, i.e. GPUs, so relatively easy to program again, and now a lot of startups with arrays of scalar cores each with vector engines, so expected to be most difficult to program. He didn't go into why vector instructions are hard to use efficiently, and hard for compiler writers, but I'd be interested if anyone here could explain that. 1. [VIDEO]"},{"id":25377958,"author":"metrognome","month":"2020-12","story":"Cameras and Lenses","stance":"reference","why":"Example of MURA pattern used in UV lithography","quote":"","text":"The pattern of holes technique in that link (MURA) is also used in UV lithography for IC fabrication (example: [VIDEO] )."},{"id":23494016,"author":"CalChris","month":"2020-06","story":"Jim Keller to Depart Intel","stance":"reference","why":"One of several Keller talks","quote":"And another","text":"Jim Keller giving a talk on Moore's Law at Berkeley, [LINK] And another [VIDEO] And another [LINK] DEC, PA Semi, Apple, AMD, Tesla, Intel. He's been everywhere. [LINK] The internal announcement memo: [LINK]"},{"id":23496810,"author":"cepth","month":"2020-06","story":"Jim Keller Resigns from Intel, Effective Immediately","stance":"reference","why":"Keller's presentation at the Matroid conference","quote":"Keller then did a presentation at the Matroid conference (held at the end of February) ( ).","text":"What makes this more bizarre than Keller’s typical short stints at previous companies is that he has done a ton of media in the last year. He’s probably given more time to journalists/interviewers in 2020 than in the previous 3 decades of his career combined. A Fortune piece from May of this year gave some insights into his plans (as well as provided a nice overview of his career) ( [LINK] ). > Keller won’t talk much about the massive chip redesign he’s overseeing—chip designers seldom do—and Intel’s new chip probably won’t be ready for another year or two. Still, both Intel and Keller have scattered some clues about how the chips might work. The new chips will cleanly separate major functions, to make it easier for the company to improve one section at a time—an approach that evokes the chiplet model Keller used at AMD. Keller also hints that Intel’s low-power Atom line of chips may figure more prominently in his future designs for PCs and servers. It doesn’t sound like at press time he was planning to leave. Keller also did a great interview on Lex Fridman’s podcast, which was released in February of this year ( [LINK] ). Keller then did a presentation at the Matroid conference (held at the end of February) ( [VIDEO] ). I hope he’s ok, since the Intel statement specifically mentioned “personal reasons”."}],"r":[["T647CGsuOVU",1]]},"AjSl4n_KdOY":{"wy":"Linked in discussions of diet, aging and cancer as a quick summary of how IGF-1 and mTOR growth signaling cut against longevity.","q":{"x":"video is replete with examples of the effects of low IGF-1/GH or high IGF-1/GH both in animal models and humans.","i":8440843,"y":"2014"},"m":[{"id":8440843,"author":"dpatrick86","month":"2014-10","story":"What will it take to run a 2-hour marathon?","stance":"rec","why":"Effects of low versus high IGF-1 in animals and humans","quote":"video is replete with examples of the effects of low IGF-1/GH or high IGF-1/GH both in animal models and humans.","text":"Speaking to your question about \"what the long term effects of these drugs are\": My wife put together a together a pretty interesting video about the \"faustian bargain\" which is IGF-1 (used as a proxy measure for growth hormone)... video is replete with examples of the effects of low IGF-1/GH or high IGF-1/GH both in animal models and humans. One of the more interesting points in the video is the fact that people with polymorphisms that make their IGF-1 receptor experience some slight loss of function actually live longer (in general). [VIDEO] The video also links to an article which has a bibliography if you're interested."},{"id":30625973,"author":"loudtieblahblah","month":"2022-03","story":"Could cancer cells’ iron addiction be their Achilles heel?","stance":"reference","why":"IGF-1 stimulates growth, good for youth, bad for aging","quote":"mtor/igf-1 stimulates growth. All growth. This is great for youth. Not so much for aging.","text":"[LINK] [LINK] Its pretty well know mtor/igf-1 stimulates growth. All growth. This is great for youth. Not so much for aging. [VIDEO]"},{"id":26704514,"author":"femiagbabiaka","month":"2021-04","story":"For 2M years, humans ate meat and little else: study","stance":"reference","why":"High IGF-1 from red meat diets associated with worse longevity","quote":"there's a lot of research showing that high levels of IGF-1 associated with diets high in red meat are not good for longevity","text":"1. Bigger groups === more and better data. I didn't know anyone growing up who was interested in ketogenic or carnivorous diets, now it's much more common. 2. Huge difference between the diet of a westerner following carnivore and traditional tribal carnivorous diets 3. The Inuits weren't particularly healthy, I'm not sure why their diet matters: [LINK] 4. And most importantly, there's a lot of research showing that high levels of IGF-1 associated with diets high in red meat are not good for longevity: [VIDEO]"}],"r":[["-R-eqJDQ2nU",1]]},"NvDvESEXcgE":{"wy":"HN finds Erik Singer's accent breakdowns informative and entertaining, and links them in discussions of accents and speech.","q":{"x":"Erik Singer's videos on accent work in particular fall right into the 'informative and entertaining' basket for me","i":20946797,"y":"2019"},"m":[{"id":20946797,"author":"caf","month":"2019-09","story":"Postmortem: Every Frame a Painting (2017)","stance":"rec","why":"Informative and entertaining accent breakdowns by Erik Singer.","quote":"Erik Singer's videos on accent work in particular fall right into the 'informative and entertaining' basket for me","text":"Technique Critique - Erik Singer's videos on accent work in particular fall right into the 'informative and entertaining' basket for me, anyway. [VIDEO]"},{"id":14383758,"author":"m0nty","month":"2017-05","story":"‘The Wire’ – Game Day","stance":"reference","why":"A dialect coach discusses Idris Elba's accent.","quote":"Discussion of his accent here from a dialect coach, \"This is amazing","text":"Idris Elba and Dominic West both caught me by surprise, given that I'm English and I was convinced they were both American. Then I heard Idris in another production talking proper London ... Discussion of his accent here from a dialect coach, \"This is amazing\": [VIDEO]"},{"id":14136908,"author":"undershirt","month":"2017-04","story":"Pink Trombone: Speech Synthesis Simulation in JavaScript","stance":"reference","why":"Dialect coach describing accents.","quote":"I would love to be able to refer to this when watching Erik Singer describe accents","text":"Would this be a good way to learn a language? Seems like a good way to show how to pronounce consonants/vowels that we may have trouble discerning from similar sounds. for example, I would love to be able to refer to this when watching Erik Singer describe accents: [VIDEO]"}],"r":[["NSDTZQdo6H8",1]]},"T39QHprz-x8":{"wy":"HN cites it as encouraging evidence that deserts can be regenerated and greened.","q":{"x":"A few I've enjoyed","i":32729094,"y":"2022"},"m":[{"id":32729094,"author":"aarondf","month":"2022-09","story":"Mangroves on a Landfill","stance":"rec","why":"Community regenerating desert landscape in Saudi Arabia","quote":"there is some really encouraging stuff going on out there!","text":"If this interests you, I've been watching a lot of permaculture and regenerative agriculture videos on YouTube and there is some really encouraging stuff going on out there! I have no idea how to make the restorative stuff go more mainstream, but it's really impressive to watch small communities or determined individuals entirely change a landscape in a few decades. A few I've enjoyed: - [VIDEO] - [LINK] - [LINK] - [LINK] - [LINK]"},{"id":35218208,"author":"sampo","month":"2023-03","story":"Greening the desert: the architect regenerating Jordan’s native forests","stance":"reference","why":"Identifies the Al Baydha project from the thread","quote":"You are probably talking about Al Baydha.","text":"> The same thing happens in Saudi Arabia where they had some amazing results by (oversimplified, yes), digging some swales and putting some rocks on steep hills. The grass was coming back, trees were growing. And that's it, cut, no more info. You are probably talking about Al Baydha. As far as I understand, the project ran 2009-2016, and last information in Wikipedia is from 2020. [LINK] [VIDEO]"},{"id":33685037,"author":"worldsayshi","month":"2022-11","story":"We need more water than rain can provide: refilling rivers with desalination","stance":"reference","why":"Water traps turning desert green","quote":"While it cannot possibly(?) solve water shortages everywhere it can turn some deserts green","text":"My favourite solution to water scarcity is water traps. It seems like such an amazingly simple concept with underrated potential: Make sure that whatever water rains down in a place stays long enough to nudge the micro-climate in the right direction - by digging some water barriers in the right places. While it cannot possibly(?) solve water shortages everywhere it can turn some deserts green: [VIDEO]"}],"r":[["ZRV3pBuPxEQ",1],["ZSPkcpGmflE",1]]},"Tct38KwROdw":{"wy":"HN cites it as the go-to example of how impossible it is to get prices in advance for US medical care.","q":{"x":"Here's a great video that I think is related to this topic about the near-impossibility of finding out the cost of giving birth","i":14666940,"y":"2017"},"m":[{"id":14666940,"author":"elicash","month":"2017-06","story":"Thoughts on Insurance","stance":"rec","why":"Near-impossibility of finding out the cost of giving birth.","quote":"Here's a great video that I think is related to this topic about the near-impossibility of finding out the cost of giving birth","text":"Here's a great video that I think is related to this topic about the near-impossibility of finding out the cost of giving birth: [VIDEO] I'd imagine the reasons for the complexity are the same as an injury. Edit: Just a thought... What if enough consumers went line-by-line AFTER the fact and shared what the specific breakdown of every item cost? So then you'd be able to say, okay, at this hospital them giving us an Advil cost $X and them doing this procedure cost $Y. Some way of making the master price list for how much individual items cost public and grouping together ones that generally appear together..."},{"id":31462162,"author":"AlotOfReading","month":"2022-05","story":"Surgery estimated cost $1,300. Then the Bill Came: $229,000","stance":"reference","why":"Journalists' futile attempt at price estimation for routine procedures.","quote":"journalists have documented their futile attempts to do price estimation for routine procedures. Here's one example","text":"You can try to comparison shop all you want, but no hospital will give you details in the chargemaster if they aren't legally obligated to. Even if you are in a position to shop around, journalists have documented their futile attempts to do price estimation for routine procedures. Here's one example: [VIDEO] There have been recent laws that requiring publishing some of that information (which are being heavily contested), but comparison shopping remains effectively impossible in the US."},{"id":25658008,"author":"elliekelly","month":"2021-01","story":"US doctor forgives $650k in medical bills for cancer patients","stance":"reference","why":"A reporter's attempt to get a hospital quote for childbirth.","quote":"Reminds me of the Vox reporter who tried to get a hospital quote for how much it would cost his wife to have a baby","text":"Reminds me of the Vox reporter who tried to get a hospital quote for how much it would cost his wife to have a baby: [VIDEO] I’m sure you can guess the outcome."},{"id":19796450,"author":"empath75","month":"2019-05","story":"A Common Blood Test Can Cost $11 or Almost $1k","stance":"reference","why":"Hospitals will not disclose delivery costs, so shopping around is impossible.","quote":"Hospitals won’t tell you the costs of treatment ahead of time, even for easily predictable items like baby delivery.","text":"Hospitals won’t tell you the costs of treatment ahead of time, even for easily predictable items like baby delivery. It’s impossible to shop around. [VIDEO]"},{"id":19714585,"author":"drchewbacca","month":"2019-04","story":"Ask HN: What is your money-making side project outside programming?","stance":"reference","why":"Example of failing to get pricing in advance for a medical procedure.","quote":"Here's a video of someone failing to get pricing in advance for a medical procedure","text":"I find it surprising that the US is seen as strongly free market when things like this crop up all over the place. Here's a video of someone failing to get pricing in advance for a medical procedure and an article about how many simple tasks need licences. The fact that a lot of areas only have 1 broadband provider blows my mind. [VIDEO] [LINK] [LINK]"},{"id":15881302,"author":"elicash","month":"2017-12","story":"Emergency rooms are monopolies","stance":"reference","why":"Relevant example of opaque hospital pricing.","quote":"Relevant","text":"Relevant: [VIDEO] The \"existing legislation and regulation\" section is pretty interesting for wikipedia: [LINK]"},{"id":13324985,"author":"pwinnski","month":"2017-01","story":"'Legitimate' US healthcare pricing could reduce costs by a third","stance":"reference","why":"Example of being unable to get a price for care.","quote":"","text":"You seem to be literally the only person who has been able to get straight answers from anyone on anything related to health care. As you can probably see from this thread, most of us would have the same reaction: impotent frustration. I can go to another dealer if I don't get straight answers. I can walk away at any point up until the final signature. This is simply not an analogous situation, as evidenced by the repeated testimony of everyone here. And this guy: [VIDEO]"}]},"Vy5Ky50eGJs":{"wy":"Referenced in drone threads as a demonstration that variable pitch quadcopters fly well with little drift.","q":{"x":"A nice variable pitch quad","i":12722843,"y":"2016"},"m":[{"id":12722843,"author":"RealityVoid","month":"2016-10","story":"1000W LED on a Drone [video]","stance":"rec","why":"Variable pitch quad flying with little drift","quote":"A nice variable pitch quad","text":"The example you posted has far more things in common with a multirotor than with a helicopter. What amazes me at those little things is how little side-to-side drift they have, considering that's all depending on the CG being well centered. A nice variable pitch quad: [VIDEO]"},{"id":12370782,"author":"jakemoshenko","month":"2016-08","story":"Plasma wings could change the way airplanes are designed and flown","stance":"reference","why":"MIT variable pitch quadcopter demonstration","quote":"MIT has also demonstrated a variable pitch quadcopter.","text":"Manned multicopters have already happened. [1] MIT has also demonstrated a variable pitch quadcopter. [2] If they can use plasma laminar detachment and reattachment to somehow simulate a variable pitch propeller, while still getting some weight savings over a design with multiple swashplates, then yes I think it should help the quadcopter design scale up to larger propellers. That's a big if though, since to simulate a negative angle of attack I'm guessing your propeller design would have to be pretty inefficient with all plasma turned off. I'm not entirely sure why you want this though, since the single large rotor is more efficient. There was a novel design a few years ago with one large rotor for lift and three smaller ones to counter torque and provide control. [3] [1] [LINK] [2] [VIDEO] [3] [LINK]"},{"id":6835339,"author":"jonmrodriguez","month":"2013-12","story":"Amazon Promises Package Delivery By Drone: Is It for Real?","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"","text":"Are there other types of drones other than traditional quadcopters, for example variable-pitch quadcopters [1], that can get more airspeed and thus cover more distance in 15 min? What about using airplane-style drones (like traditional RC planes) + a parachute to land the package? And this way you could use nitro gas fuel to get better energy density (since you can use one big gas engine instead of having to use four tiny motors) [1] [VIDEO]"}],"r":[["36myc8wQhLo",1],["BnSUk0je6oo",1]]},"c33AZBnRHks":{"wy":"Cited as a fun example of how algorithmic changes can yield enormous performance gains.","q":{"x":"Quick google yields very good examples in huge improvements on a single algorithm level","i":37099454,"y":"2023"},"m":[{"id":34040953,"author":"db48x","month":"2022-12","story":"Ask HN: Is there a code golf community but for performance instead of size?","stance":"rec","why":"Fun community game of speeding up code","quote":"It can be a really fun game!","text":"See also “Someone improved my code by 40,832,277,770%” < [VIDEO] > by Matt Parker. It can be a really fun game!"},{"id":37099454,"author":"high_priest","month":"2023-08","story":"Squeeze the hell out of the system you have","stance":"reference","why":"Example of huge improvement from a single algorithm change","quote":"Quick google yields very good examples in huge improvements on a single algorithm level","text":"Quick google yields very good examples in huge improvements on a single algorithm level: [VIDEO] Easy to imagine how even smaller improvements across 10-100 steps of data processing, can become even better."},{"id":34049754,"author":"CRConrad","month":"2022-12","story":"A Neat XOR Trick","stance":"reference","why":"Example of code made billions of times faster","quote":"Drastically' in this context can mean the slower approach taking billions of times longer.","text":"> 'Drastically' in this context can mean the slower approach taking billions of times longer. Standupmaths - \"Someone improved my code by 40,832,277,770%\": [VIDEO] (OK, 41 billion percent is only 0.41 billion times better, but still.)"},{"id":34440313,"author":"otikik","month":"2023-01","story":"A New Formula for the Determinant","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"there’s people out there that will take your code and make it 40.363.757.265% more efficient","text":"Also keep in mind that there’s people out there that will take your code and make it 40.363.757.265% more efficient (perhaps not in this case) [VIDEO]"}],"r":[["YE8MVNMzpbo",1],["aeWyp2vXxqA",1],["FlQYU3m1e80",1],["wymmCdLdPvM",1],["6JwEYamjXpA",1]]},"hE2lna5Wxuo":{"wy":"Cited in threads on calorie counting as a quick, visual demonstration that labeled calories can be well off.","q":{"x":"Casey Neistat made a fun little video[1] that explores this a bit.","i":12607369,"y":"2016"},"m":[{"id":12607369,"author":"GnarlyWhale","month":"2016-09","story":"What 2,000 Calories Looks Like (2014)","stance":"rec","why":"Surprising inaccuracy of calorie counts on some foods","quote":"Casey Neistat made a fun little video[1] that explores this a bit.","text":"Casey Neistat made a fun little video[1] that explores this a bit. Without spoilers, it was a bit surprising just how off some of the food is. [1] [VIDEO]"},{"id":30626434,"author":"virtuallynathan","month":"2022-03","story":"A beginner's guide to intermittent fasting (2012)","stance":"reference","why":"Calorie labeling accuracy questioned; counting is not very helpful","quote":"Calorie counting, while not useless, is not very helpful.","text":"Do you know how many calories you burn? Do you actually know how many calories are in your food? I have a metabolic cart, and the answer surprises me sometimes on the former - just standing at my desk all day I burn >3300kcal. I actually went to talk to the guy in the Casey Neistat video about food labels, and almost bought a bomb calorimeter. Calorie counting, while not useless, is not very helpful. [VIDEO]"},{"id":11796789,"author":"thesimon","month":"2016-05","story":"How do food manufacturers calculate the calorie count of packaged foods? (2003)","stance":"reference","why":"Calorie labels in the US can be inaccurate, plus restaurant counts","quote":"The video also deals with calorie count in restaurants.","text":"And apparently in the US you can be off by whatever you want [VIDEO] The video also deals with calorie count in restaurants. The NYC regulation to require calorie counts directly on the menu really seems like a good idea. Other than the people already knowing the rough calorie count, who checks the obscure and difficult tables on the website?"}]},"hKqVvXTanzI":{"wy":"HN cites it as evidence that mining does not meaningfully degrade GPUs, so used mining cards are generally fine buys.","q":{"x":"Linus tech tips had an episode on it, and largely found that the performance degradation was insignificant","i":32870041,"y":"2022"},"m":[{"id":32912042,"author":"scambier","month":"2022-09","story":"In 2019 RX470 were $70, RX580 $100","stance":"rec","why":"Used cards do not perform worse than new ones.","quote":"There's a nice Linus Tech Tips video about this","text":"There's a nice Linus Tech Tips video about this: [VIDEO] The tl;dw is that used cards don't perform worse than new cards - your card either works, or it doesn't. But like any electronics, they have an \"expiration date\", and if it's been used for 2 years, it's 2 years closer to dying. It's up to you to decide if the discount of a used card might be worth it."},{"id":32985484,"author":"bentcorner","month":"2022-09","story":"Nvidia in the Valley","stance":"rec","why":"Mining does not affect card lifespan; clean cards run faster.","quote":"LTT goes over it pretty well (IMO)","text":"LTT goes over it pretty well (IMO): [VIDEO] tldr: Cards (like any piece of other electronics) do have a lifespan, but mining doesn't affect that. Cards that are kept clean and in better working conditions will run faster."},{"id":32870041,"author":"ulrikrasmussen","month":"2022-09","story":"GPU mining no longer profitable after Ethereum merge","stance":"reference","why":"LTT found performance degradation from mining insignificant; dust cooling risk.","quote":"Linus tech tips had an episode on it, and largely found that the performance degradation was insignificant","text":"Linus tech tips had an episode on it, and largely found that the performance degradation was insignificant: [VIDEO] He did however note that the cards that he tested had been used in a relatively clean and dust-free environment. The biggest risk to buying a card that has been running 24/7 for years is that the cooling system is busted due to build-up of dust."},{"id":32915770,"author":"belthesar","month":"2022-09","story":"GeForce RTX 40 Series","stance":"reference","why":"LMG testing found mining GPUs worked fine.","quote":"Linus Media Group did some testing and found that mining GPUs worked just fine.","text":"This is but one test, but Linus Media Group did some testing and found that mining GPUs worked just fine. Fan time and thermal pad degradation are about the only real detractors from buying a used GPU. [VIDEO] Of course, if a card's running at thermal throttle for long periods of time, that has a chance to damage the card, but given that most cards are running underclocked, the likelihood that you'll run into that problem is low."},{"id":31788865,"author":"kevingadd","month":"2022-06","story":"Bitcoin Falls Below $20k for the First Time Since 2020","stance":"reference","why":"Tests show mining does not meaningfully damage GPUs.","quote":"haven't managed to find any evidence that it damages the card more than regular use.","text":"A few different people have done tests to verify whether GPUs used for eth mining took any damage from doing so, and haven't managed to find any evidence that it damages the card more than regular use. Running cards under severe load nonstop for years could potentially shorten their lifespan by a tiny bit due to electromigration or wear out the fans, but that's about it. Of course, any used GPU could be defective, so you need to be careful buying one no matter what. [VIDEO] [LINK]"},{"id":29117719,"author":"porb121","month":"2021-11","story":"Intel’s Alder Lake big.little CPU design, tested: it’s a barn burner","stance":"critical","why":"","quote":"","text":"how do you explain benchmarks showing no difference between used and new gpu performance? if a card posts its age is not a concern [LINK] [VIDEO]"}],"r":[["_rBO8neWw04",1],["Z1PCtIaM_GQ",1]]},"rEoc0YoALt0":{"wy":"Pointed to as an approachable layperson's explanation whenever video magnification or vibration visualization comes up.","q":{"x":"Steve Mould's layman's video","i":24371060,"y":"2020"},"m":[{"id":24371060,"author":"djmips","month":"2020-09","story":"Eulerian Video Magnification","stance":"rec","why":"Layman-friendly explanation of video magnification","quote":"Steve Mould's layman's video","text":"I found this other video on the topic that I wanted to share. [LINK] and Steve Mould's layman's video [VIDEO]"},{"id":32502786,"author":"mgdlbp","month":"2022-08","story":"Extracting Audio from Visual Information","stance":"reference","why":"Overview of motion magnification research for visualizing vibrations","quote":"overview of research, and discussions on HN","text":"There is indeed research in doing that -- \"motion magnification\" visualizing vibrations in machinery: [VIDEO] overview of research, and discussions on HN: [LINK] TED talk: [LINK] edit: TFA: > from the change of a single pixel’s color value over time, it’s possible to infer motions smaller than a pixel. > the researchers borrowed a technique from earlier work on algorithms that amplify minuscule variations in video,"},{"id":29015107,"author":"davidb_","month":"2021-10","story":"DIY Acoustic Camera","stance":"reference","why":"Explainer on motion amplification for equipment","quote":"Explainer Youtube video about Motion Amplification","text":"Combining this with Motion Amplification/Video Magnification [1] could result in some very interesting visuals and applications for factory equipment. [1] [VIDEO] Explainer Youtube video about Motion Amplification"},{"id":24370713,"author":"djmips","month":"2020-09","story":"Eulerian Video Magnification","stance":"reference","why":"Steve Mould explains Eulerian video magnification","quote":"I saw a Steve Mould video on this recently.","text":"I saw a Steve Mould video on this recently. [VIDEO] The guy who used it seems to imply his version had a bit of 'special sauce'"}],"r":[["bRCNenhcvpw",1],["pSHj5UKSylk",1],["3MghYhf-GhU",1],["0ifChJ0nJfM",1],["CUjt36SD3h8",1],["BFld4EBO2RE",1],["HyznrdDSSGM",1],["fKSSY1gzCEs",1]]},"sJtm0MoOgiU":{"wy":"HN cites it as the stunt behind 'copyright every melody' in threads about music copyright, takedowns and AI authorship.","q":{"x":"They did a great Tedx talk here","i":35870019,"y":"2023"},"m":[{"id":35870019,"author":"adamredwoods","month":"2023-05","story":"Ed Sheeran Demonstrates How Copyright Is Destroying Musicians","stance":"rec","why":"Explains the accidental-infringement problems musicians face with melody copyright.","quote":"They did a great Tedx talk here","text":"They did a great Tedx talk here, and the person effectively describes the problems that Sheeran has run into: [VIDEO]"},{"id":48701728,"author":"alwa","month":"2026-06","story":"AI Is Designing Radio Chips That Humans Couldn't Even Imagine","stance":"reference","why":"Lawyer explains his stunt of algorithmically generating all melodies under CC-0.","quote":"He and programmer Noah Rubin algorithmically generated every possible melody","text":"I’m reminded of lawyer Damien Riehl’s (performative) reaction to the Sam Smith infringement decision, back in 2019/2020. He and programmer Noah Rubin algorithmically generated every possible melody (within a certain combinatorial space, in MIDI format as I recall), and purported to release them under CC-0 license [0]. He went on to attract some attention and explain his argument at a regional TEDx event [1]. I seem to recall legal commentators reacting with an eyeroll—apparently judges split much finer hairs than these for a living—but it was a cute stunt. [0] [LINK] [1] [VIDEO] and [LINK]"},{"id":37746330,"author":"rainbowzootsuit","month":"2023-10","story":"California’s Math Misadventure Is About to Go National","stance":"reference","why":"Talk about copyrighting all melodies to avoid accidental infringement.","quote":"","text":"I hope this has been tested by now, but not sure: Copyrighting all the melodies to avoid accidental infringement | Damien Riehl | TEDxMinneapolis [VIDEO]"},{"id":37349800,"author":"jasrys","month":"2023-09","story":"U.S. Copyright Office RFC: Artificial Intelligence and Copyright","stance":"reference","why":"Example of someone attempting to copyright every song melody.","quote":"someone already tried copyrighting every song melody [3]","text":"1. lots of established law and case law (at least in the US), this is already a well-settled problem and folks have the tools and proper venue to bring infringement claims. Yes, federal copyright infringement litigation is prohibitively expensive for many issues. There is a now a \"small claims court\" for smaller issues. [1] 2. Those works cannot be copyrighted (at least in the US). [2]. And hey, someone already tried copyrighting every song melody [3] [1]: [LINK] [2]: [LINK] [3]: [VIDEO]"},{"id":30709247,"author":"conradev","month":"2022-03","story":"US Copyright Office refuses application with AI algorithm named as author","stance":"reference","why":"Talk on brute-force generating every melody and releasing it to the public.","quote":"generate every possible melody and release it under the Creative Commons","text":"There is also the brute force approach (from 2020) to generate every possible melody and release it under the Creative Commons: [VIDEO]"},{"id":27004816,"author":"NiceWayToDoIT","month":"2021-05","story":"Piano teacher gets copyright claim for Beethoven’s Moonlight Sonata [video]","stance":"reference","why":"Talk about copyrighting all melodies to avoid infringement","quote":"That is the reason these guys decided to Copyrighting all the melodies to avoid accidental infringement","text":"That is the reason these guys decided to Copyrighting all the melodies to avoid accidental infringement [VIDEO] TED talk"},{"id":25071858,"author":"NiceWayToDoIT","month":"2020-11","story":"Show HN: A 1000 auto-generated hexagonal SVG logos","stance":"reference","why":"Similar case of preventing accidental infringement in music","quote":"","text":"Is this beginning of a Copyright trolling campaign or preventing accidental infringement as similar case in music [VIDEO] ?"},{"id":24875106,"author":"simcup","month":"2020-10","story":"YouTube-dl has received a DMCA takedown from RIAA","stance":"reference","why":"Talk on copyrighting every melody","quote":"found the ted talk","text":"You wouldn't even need ML. Iirc there was this guy who coded a script that played every permutation of 12 notes in a 5 minute period and then made all the Melodys public domain. Ianal so I don't know if this would hold up in court, but technically that should invalidate every copyright on Melodys after that point in time. E: [VIDEO] found the ted talk"}]},"xK4MBzp5YwM":{"wy":"Cited in debates about campus culture, free speech and workplace diversity controversies as a window into a particular mindset.","q":{"x":"There's a good short video here that explores what's been happening at Yale","i":19567194,"y":"2019"},"m":[{"id":19567194,"author":"repolfx","month":"2019-04","story":"What happens after rich kids bribe their way into college? I teach them","stance":"rec","why":"Explores what has been happening at Yale with students and administrators","quote":"There's a good short video here that explores what's been happening at Yale","text":"The issue of administrators who repeatedly side with students even in absurd situations seems to be slowly reaching awareness. There's a good short video here that explores what's been happening at Yale: [VIDEO] Trigger warning - it has clips of students attacking professors and acting in ways that are completely delusional. I found it almost physically hard to watch and from the comments, seems I wasn't the only one. In a scene where a professor is literally surrounded by students yelling at him and having meltdowns, it's shown that one of the adults at the back of the group is an actual university administrator: he watches passively and does nothing to intervene. The presenter argues the problem is that the administrators are often just as extreme as the students and benefit from cowtowing to almost any level of immature nonsense because the students demands inevitably translate into growth of administrative departments."},{"id":14954023,"author":"rustynails77","month":"2017-08","story":"Google Fires Employee Behind Controversial Diversity Memo","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"To understand this mindset, you really need to watch this video on Yale.","text":"This is an amazing discussion and it really needs to occur. What scares me is how much this conversation is being ruled by an angry emotive mob, at the expense of democracy and free speech. There is almost no fact in any of these comments. Why MUST we have 50% of male and female in any industry? Have you noticed how almost every media outlet ignores female dominated industries (such as veterinary science) [0], where over 85% of vets in the western world are female? The strongest argument in this thread appears to be that there must be equal numbers for equality, but it's only EVER applied to male dominated industries (Google it if you doubt me). However, not only is this argument unjustified, it's continually selectively applied by most mainstream media outlets, again and again. There is no challenge or balance to any of these arguments from the mainstream media. I am disappointed that Google sacked this engineer. To understand this mindset, you really need to watch this video on Yale. People being shut down from talking because they're male or white [1] or because angry mobs don't like what they have to say. People in this thread talk about sexism and yet they struggle to provide any evidence (while I can point at truck loads of evidence of misandry, eg. domestic violence, veterinary science, etc). This Yale video talks about this exact problem i'm describing. We have a society where you are no longer allowed to express selective views (even with evidence). Google have confi"},{"id":13969905,"author":"Banthum","month":"2017-03","story":"Reasons YouTubers keep imploding, from a YouTuber","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"","text":"\"I don't think you should have the same rights as me because your sexuality/gender/class/religion is different\" Quite the opposite. Typically safe space warriors are demanding that people have different rights in public based on their genetics, not the same rights. E.g. \"You're a white man so shut up, I should have a special right to speak over you because of [XYZ].\" Here are some examples: [VIDEO]"}]},"1_SdC8LVODY":{"wy":"Cited mainly for Carmack's offhand survey asking how many people had spent $20 on Android apps, used in debates about app store economics.","m":[{"id":2859191,"author":"enobrev","month":"2011-08","story":"Developing Android Apps: it's really not so bad","stance":"reference","why":"Carmack's informal survey on paying for apps.","quote":"I'm reminded of John Carmack's ad-hock survey of iOS and Android users to find how many people would have spent at least $20 for apps[1]","text":"I take some issue with this argument. Not necessarily to say that it's false, just to say that it's not a strong argument or at the very least holds little foresight. As an example I recently downloaded Words With Friends on the Android, became immediately annoyed with the ads and was at a loss when I tried to find a pay version. I emailed them and although they replied within hours (fantastic!) they said they don't have a paid version at the moment (it's in the works). I'm reminded of John Carmack's ad-hock survey of iOS and Android users to find how many people would have spent at least $20 for apps[1], and then he explains in more detail why we won't be working with Android at that time. His reasoning is solid, but that survey puts an exclamation on the same point you're making, which is unfortunate. It's easy to claim nobody pays for things on Android if there's nothing there worth paying for. It seems to me this would make Android a prime market rather than a poor one. I've personally spent at least $40 on Android apps in the last year or so (mostly games) and will spend plenty more as developers come and give me reason (or at least a chance) to give them money. 1: [VIDEO]"},{"id":2445489,"author":"pmh","month":"2011-04","story":"John Carmack Interview","stance":"reference","why":"Carmack's QuakeCon question about spending on Android apps.","quote":"The question he asked at Quakecon 2010 was actually \"who has spent at least $20 on the Android app store?\"[1], not just on a single game.","text":"The question he asked at Quakecon 2010 was actually \"who has spent at least $20 on the Android app store?\"[1], not just on a single game. Your catch-22 scenario still holds, though I think the quantity and quality of free apps in the Android app store has also raised the bar for pay apps. [1] [VIDEO]"},{"id":2557659,"author":"Kylekramer","month":"2011-05","story":"Android Tablets: Hardware and OS are Getting Better, but Apps are Still MIA","stance":"critical","why":"","quote":"a guy asking a leading question","text":"I don't know if a guy asking a leading question (Skip to 8:40 here: [VIDEO] ) trying to justifying his own decisions is 'nuff said."}]},"5uxsFglz2ig":{"wy":"Cited as a concrete example of how thermite is used for track welding in discussions of rail and thermite.","q":{"x":"Railroad workers doing track maintainence routinely use thermite to repair damaged sections.","i":15308258,"y":"2017"},"m":[{"id":39228353,"author":"philip1209","month":"2024-02","story":"Check Out These Self-Soldering Sleeves from World War II","stance":"reference","why":"Thermite used for welding, producing magnetic iron.","quote":"It's used for welding, and the output is iron (magnetic)","text":"Depends. It's used for welding, and the output is iron (magnetic): [VIDEO] I assume it would be too difficult to trigger the thermite reaction in the field with WWII tech, among other problems."},{"id":32120573,"author":"jpollock","month":"2022-07","story":"Why rails buckle in Britain","stance":"reference","why":"Example of thermite track welding for continuous welded rail.","quote":"Thermite track welding","text":"This drove me down a rabbit hole, so I thought I would share. Track used to be separate pieces (seemingly before 1950), but they are now continuous welded rail [1][3]. This was done to allow trains to go faster, and lowers maintenance on the trains. This confused me because trains go \"clack clack clack\" - I guess that's foley now. However, it means track is more prone to buckling, since there aren't any expansion joints, like we have on bridges. Slack is added/removed from the rails by stressing the track during installation [2]. They heat (or mechanically stretch) the track to the length at a chosen \"neutral\" temperature. Then they install the track. This allows them to calculate the max expansion and contraction the track will see and offset those forces with the sleepers (horizontal forces?) and the ballast (vertical forces?). The rail can still buckle - this is engineering. If the ballast fails, or the temperatures exceed the maximum allowed for, then it will buckle. It also looks like it must be continually destressed by going along the rail, cutting it and re-welding it? [4] [1] [LINK] [2] [LINK] [3] Thermite track welding: [VIDEO] [4] [LINK]"},{"id":15308258,"author":"Laforet","month":"2017-09","story":"Anatomy of a Moral Panic","stance":"reference","why":"Shows railroad workers using thermite to weld track.","quote":"Railroad workers doing track maintainence routinely use thermite to repair damaged sections.","text":"I might be wrong about this, but I think thermite is used more often as a simple welding method. Railroad workers doing track maintainence routinely use thermite to repair damaged sections. [VIDEO]"}]},"8ucCxtgN6sc":{"wy":"HN uses it as a light reference for how old software looks to a new generation, often as a half-joke.","m":[{"id":23016957,"author":"Gravityloss","month":"2020-04","story":"Internet Explorer Is Evil (2002)","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"","text":"It was such a powerful generational phenomenon that kids have heard of it even if they have no direct experience: [VIDEO]"},{"id":15645606,"author":"finchisko","month":"2017-11","story":"Almost everything on computers is perceptually slower than it was in 1983","stance":"reference","why":"Teens' reactions to old software","quote":"Or check teens reactions to Windows 95.","text":"I think what guy is actually experiencing strong feeling of nostalgia for older times. And I understand that. I see it on myself more and more with coming years too. We were simply born in some era, which holds strong memories in us. We hold sentiment to old things (programs), because somehow they're connected with best memories from time we were much younger. And the best memories are usually bounded to age around 20 - 30. Ask senior to tell you a story from his time and there is high probability that the story will be from their younger times, than from older ones. My point is, that maybe todays software is not all that bad. We just don't feel the same needs as 20 year old guy somewhere at Google who programmed it. And it also works apposite way too. Show 20 year guy Visicalc, what he will think about it. Or check teens reactions to Windows 95. [VIDEO]"},{"id":11254699,"author":"d0m","month":"2016-03","story":"AlphaGo beats the world champion Lee Sedol in first of five matches","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"Here's a video where teens discover Windows 95","text":"Here's a video where teens discover Windows 95: [VIDEO] It gives a visual analogy of what you're saying about the new generation and AI!"}]},"HeOVbeh2yr0":{"wy":"HN mostly uses it as a joke or relevant comedy reference when corporate wrongdoers escape punishment that street criminals would not.","m":[{"id":34208619,"author":"dingusdew","month":"2023-01","story":"Epstein's sex trafficking was aided by JPMorgan, US Virgin Islands lawsuit says","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"relevant","text":"That's literally the fucking problem. Two legal systems, one for \"elite\" where they get a fucking slap on the wrist, and one for the \"poors\" where you get the living shit beat out of you and end up with a hospital bill and a charge for \"resisting\" arrest when they twisted your arm so hard that you involuntarily resisted the movement. When it comes to poor people cops will do shit like throw a baton on the ground, put a suspects hand on it, and then claim they had gone for their weapon, beating them and taking them in for arrest right away. When it comes to the \"elite\" they make a nice phone call and ask them when it would be convenient to come in for \"questioning\" because \"we couldn't possibly arrest such an upstanding citizen\" which is code for \"we don't fuck with this guy because he butters our bread.\" relevant: [VIDEO]"},{"id":30388007,"author":"rkk3","month":"2022-02","story":"Amazon sent the FBI to take my family’s bank accounts","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"Reminds me of an old Chappelle's show skit.","text":"Very few cases where you actually need or want a heavily armed police force to break down doors. Probably shouldn't have a system like this if we wouldn't want it applied to ourselves... Reminds me of an old Chappelle's show skit. [VIDEO]"},{"id":20820701,"author":"spodek","month":"2019-08","story":"Sacklers Would Give Up Ownership of Purdue Pharma Under Settlement Proposal","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"Dave Chappelle's sketch on if we treated corporate criminals like street drug dealers is relevant (and funny)","text":"Dave Chappelle's sketch on if we treated corporate criminals like street drug dealers is relevant (and funny) [VIDEO] . Yet more relevant when the corporate criminals are drug dealers."}]},"IRELLH86Edo":{"wy":"Used as a pop-culture analogy for mass surveillance technology that now looks plausible.","q":{"x":"The rest of the tech imagined in that scene is plausible today too","i":40100739,"y":"2024"},"m":[{"id":40100739,"author":"hangsi","month":"2024-04","story":"Senate passes reauthorization of key US surveillance program after midnight","stance":"reference","why":"Fictional surveillance tech that is plausible today","quote":"The rest of the tech imagined in that scene is plausible today too","text":"\"Beautiful. Unethical. Dangerous.\" So says Morgan Freeman's character Lucius Fox in 2008 in The Dark Knight[0]. The rest of the tech imagined in that scene is plausible today too, considering the density of WiFi/5G and research demonstrating the potential for its use as passive radar [1]. That paper metions a cooperative base station, but I am wondering if there is any value gained in knowing exactly what the traffic is (such as some of the intelligence community does) in modelling how the waves propagate and performing an even more passive observation. [0] [VIDEO] [1] Samczyński et al. 2021 [LINK]"},{"id":25151086,"author":"netsharc","month":"2020-11","story":"Beirut Port Explosion","stance":"reference","why":"Film scene of sonar-based surveillance as an analogy","quote":"The Dark Knight's sonar","text":"Like Deja Vu ( [LINK] ), The Dark Knight's sonar ( [VIDEO] ) or the series Devs. I've always thought it'd be amazing to be able to view an event from different angles via crowd-sourced videos, and that it was very doable. From an information scientist point of view, I'm glad to see it's been done."},{"id":45474737,"author":"koolba","month":"2025-10","story":"Flock's gunshot detection microphones will start listening for human voices","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"The logical next step is replace the microphones with the ones we carry around in our pockets every day","text":"The logical next step is replace the microphones with the ones we carry around in our pockets every day: [VIDEO]"}]},"VQPIdZvoV4g":{"wy":"Posted as a joke in threads about monitoring, observability and alarms.","m":[{"id":47484206,"author":"BLKNSLVR","month":"2026-03","story":"Iran war energy crisis is a renewable energy wake-up call","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"","text":"Legit question (and one that I need to answer for myself as well): Would it be cheaper to keep the EV and rent a car for when you need to do longer trips? (also taking into account the additional hassle of renting a petrol/diesel car) Only speaking for myself, I'd seriously consider renting a (combustion) car for an interstate driving holiday if it's a rare occurrence, like once a year or once every two years. It will become an exercise in accounting[0]. My silly-ish analogy is: I don't own a plane because I fly rarely enough that it's not worth buying a plane to allow me to fly wherever, whenever I want. [0]: [VIDEO]"},{"id":40612068,"author":"bouvin","month":"2024-06","story":"Alarms in medical equipment","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"","text":"Fascinating. Interesting to hear the differentiation in severity in the same class of error. Though a bit disappointing that there is no machine that goes PING! [1] [1] [VIDEO]"},{"id":39755679,"author":"nunez","month":"2024-03","story":"Redefining Observability","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"","text":"Ah, yes, the SLO Dashboard™ that makes at least eight gagillion dollars for Datadog every year that looks super pretty for execs but is amazingly useless for SREs that actually need to fix shit at 0330. i.e. basically this: [VIDEO]"}]},"Xa_fNuaSE_I":{"wy":"Used mainly as evidence that Swift is being used for a real Windows application.","q":{"x":"The Browser Company (The one that is hyping the Arc Browser) is writing their Browser in Swift to support Windows.","i":36027593,"y":"2023"},"m":[{"id":46842750,"author":"alwillis","month":"2026-02","story":"Swift is a more convenient Rust","stance":"reference","why":"Arc's Windows app built with Swift","quote":"","text":"Arc, TBCNY's browser for macOS and Windows uses Swift [1] [2]. Their newer Dia browser which is meant to be cross-platform also uses Swift. [1]: [LINK] [2]: \"How we're building the Arc Browser Windows app with Swift \" -- [VIDEO]"},{"id":36220827,"author":"patmorgan23","month":"2023-06","story":"Swift Ownership Manifesto","stance":"reference","why":"Arc team building the Windows app in Swift","quote":"","text":"There's a group called \"The Browser Company of New York\" building a new web browser called arc in swift and they're targeting Windows first. How were building the ARC windows all with Swift - [VIDEO] [LINK]"},{"id":36027593,"author":"rvz","month":"2023-05","story":"Apple is rewriting Foundation in Swift","stance":"reference","why":"Evidence of Swift being used for a Windows browser app","quote":"The Browser Company (The one that is hyping the Arc Browser) is writing their Browser in Swift to support Windows.","text":"> No one outside Apple chooses Shift unless they want to build something in Apple's ecosystem. “No one” Yet The Browser Company (The one that is hyping the Arc Browser) is writing their Browser in Swift to support Windows. [0] which that is their main product. The Browser Company is not “No one”. [0] [VIDEO]"}]},"YScIPA8RbVE":{"wy":"Cited as the author's own talk and a way to go deeper on type algebra, with a note that the audio is rough at the start.","q":{"x":"Real types are numbers that obey type algebra, which C/Java/Go have no idea exists.","i":9466483,"y":"2015"},"m":[{"id":9466483,"author":"innguest","month":"2015-04","story":"A Rust Contributor Tries Their Hand at Go","stance":"reference","why":"Explains types as numbers obeying type algebra.","quote":"Real types are numbers that obey type algebra, which C/Java/Go have no idea exists.","text":"I very much agree with you. A zip code is a string even though it looks like a number. Likewise C/Java/Go \"types\" are tags even though they look like types. Real types are numbers that obey type algebra, which C/Java/Go have no idea exists. The Algebra of Algebraic Data Types: [VIDEO]"},{"id":5808994,"author":"crntaylor","month":"2013-06","story":"The Algebra of Algebraic Data Types","stance":"reference","why":"Author's talk on algebraic data types; audio poor at first.","quote":"(shitty audio for the first minute or 2 - it gets better)","text":"Author of the blog post here. It's amusing to see this submitted by Don Stewart, since it was his explanation[0] of algebraic data types that first made them \"click\" for me! I think I forgot to mention him in this post, but I remembered in the talk[1]. If anyone's interested in this and where else it might go, I recommend read Don's SO answer[0], this SO question and the related answers[2] and these papers [3,4] on derivatives and dissections by Conor McBride. There are also several good blog posts[5] and a paper[6] by Brent Yorgey on the topic of combinatorial species, and of course Dan Piponi's blog[7] is a treasure trove of interesting insights at the intersection of math, physics and computer science. [0] [LINK] [1] [VIDEO] (shitty audio for the first minute or 2 - it gets better). [2] [LINK] [3] [LINK] [4] [LINK] [5] [LINK] [6] [LINK] [7] [LINK] Edit: It should go without saying, but because a few people last time around seemed to think I was trying to pass this off as my own ideas, let me state that there is nothing original in this blog post. It's a repackaging of the ideas and work of lots of other people, all of whom are way smarter than I am."},{"id":5689603,"author":"nbouscal","month":"2013-05","story":"Learn You a Haskell for Great Good","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"","text":"There are two very different types of arguments typically made as to why to learn the more esoteric languages (Haskell, Lisp, Forth, others). The first is the more common: learning these languages exposes you to other ways of programming, which in turn makes you a better programmer no matter what language you're programming in. This is unquestionably true. Mainstream languages are constantly learning new things from these languages, which means you will be able to apply the techniques you learn from them to your code no matter what language you write in. I have the misfortune[1] of writing a lot of PHP at my day job, but it turns out even PHP has support for filter and map over arrays. If I hadn't learned functional programming, I would never have even looked for them. The other argument, which is made less often, is that you can actually write great software in these languages. This argument is made less often because we can obviously look at the market and the TIOBE index and see that Haskell is way down at #30 with 0.323% of the market share. However, there are really great programs made in Haskell (xmonad, for one). There are also tons of great programs made in Lisps, especially Clojure of late. So, this second argument is not entirely a dead end either. Revisiting something I mentioned in the first argument for a moment though, I would actually make a third argument that I rarely see. As I said, other languages are constantly learning new things from these languages. Has"},{"id":5196824,"author":"crntaylor","month":"2013-02","story":"The Algebra of Algebraic Data Types","stance":"reference","why":"Author's own talk on the algebra of algebraic data types.","quote":"Derivatives of types to come in part II or III (or just watch the talk on YouTube","text":"\"This guy who is discovering it by himself\" is me (the OP) about a year ago! Derivatives of types to come in part II or III (or just watch the talk on YouTube: [VIDEO] )"}]},"_oJs8-I9WtA":{"wy":"Cited as evidence that Betamax was not meaningfully better than VHS.","q":{"x":"Technology Connections did a teardown of why Betamax wasn't better than VHS","i":45994759,"y":"2025"},"m":[{"id":49142428,"author":"leoc","month":"2026-08","story":"Atom is better than RSS, in ways that matter","stance":"reference","why":"Quality comparison of Beta and VHS.","quote":"Quality comparison","text":"While a worse format surely could have won something like the video wars, it doesn't seem that VHS was in fact the worse format overall: [LINK] (Part 2: [LINK] . Quality comparison: [VIDEO] ) Beta was basically optimised for one thing, reasonable cassette size, and the tradeoffs turned out probably not to be worth it."},{"id":45994759,"author":"gwbas1c","month":"2025-11","story":"The lost cause of the Lisp machines","stance":"reference","why":"Teardown of why Betamax wasn't better than VHS.","quote":"Technology Connections did a teardown of why Betamax wasn't better than VHS","text":"This just reminds me of the people who whine about Betamax (or CCS) being better. FWIW: Technology Connections did a teardown of why Betamax wasn't better than VHS: [VIDEO] And the whole series if you actually enjoy watching these things: [LINK]"},{"id":45168787,"author":"philistine","month":"2025-09","story":"How RSS beat Microsoft","stance":"reference","why":"Tests found Beta's quality difference negligible.","quote":"the difference was so small as to be inconsequential","text":"In tests done by Technology Connections, the difference was so small as to be inconsequential. It was technically better at its slowest speed, but you could barely perceive the difference and more importantly Sony disabled the feature in the vast majority of machines sold. People wanted more than 60 minutes out of one tape. They wanted 2 hours. [VIDEO]"},{"id":30956917,"author":"philistine","month":"2022-04","story":"Is the DTS vs. Dolby war effectively over?","stance":"reference","why":"Shows Betamax was not clearly higher quality.","quote":"It was not clearly higher quality.","text":"We've all collectively fallen for Sony's marketing for Betamax. It was not clearly higher quality. [VIDEO]"}]},"b1XGPvbWn0A":{"wy":"Cited in discussions of street harassment as an illustration of what women experience in public.","m":[{"id":28272249,"author":"js2","month":"2021-08","story":"On the link between great thinking and obsessive walking","stance":"reference","why":"Offered as evidence women feel vulnerable outside.","quote":"","text":"> Women have been, for valid reasons and patriarchal overprotectiveness, been trained to feel vulnerable and exposed when walking outside. Been trained? “10 Hours of Walking in NYC as a Woman”: [VIDEO]"},{"id":21794517,"author":"js2","month":"2019-12","story":"37-Year-Old Mom Spent Seven Days Online as an 11-Year-Old Girl","stance":"reference","why":"Illustrates what women experience walking in public.","quote":"","text":"> it's very complicated to fully understand what women goes through. “10 Hours of Walking in NYC as a Woman” [VIDEO]"},{"id":17677802,"author":"maym86","month":"2018-08","story":"France Bans Street Harassment, Approving Hefty Fines for Catcallers","stance":"reference","why":"Illustrates street harassment women face daily.","quote":"","text":"> If you greet someone and smile, and they interpret your greeting or your smile as sinister, does this law make you a criminal? No that's not harrasment. You're reducing a clear issue that women face daily to your concern about being falsly accused over a smile. [VIDEO]"},{"id":15042038,"author":"no897nhtoeui","month":"2017-08","story":"An 80s Girl Talks About Computers: A Reminiscence","stance":"reference","why":"Women harassed constantly while walking in New York City.","quote":"","text":"> That's so blatant that I have a hard time believing it. That's what I thought about those videos of guys following a woman around as she walked down the streets of New York City minding her own business.[0] I had never witnessed that sort of thing and thought, \"There's no way this goes on that often or I'd see it.\" And yet, dozens of female friends confirmed that it happens all the time. I was like, \"Why have you never mentioned this?\" They just said it happens too often and there's not a lot you can do about it, plus half the time other people don't believe you. [0] [VIDEO]"}]},"bErPsq5kPzE":{"wy":"Cited as an example of LED volume virtual production, as used for Star Wars shows, in threads about VFX.","m":[{"id":32456799,"author":"jiggawatts","month":"2022-08","story":"Hollywood’s visual effects crisis","stance":"reference","why":"In-camera LED backgrounds as solution to compositing problems","quote":"The backgrounds are","text":"This is a “solved problem” now with technologies like this: [VIDEO] The backgrounds are “real” and can be captured in-camera, so there is no need for special effects to blend hair. Similarly, reflective surfaces aren’t a problem either. This was used to good effect for the Mandalorian series where the protagonist wears shiny metal armour."},{"id":32555531,"author":"firefoxd","month":"2022-08","story":"Why does everything on Netflix look like that?","stance":"reference","why":"Shows the circular LED volume setup used for Star Wars shows","quote":"","text":"For Netflix, I know what he means, but the article has a hard time describing it. It's kinda like you know what a tiktok video looks like, even when the logo is absent. But here is a better phenomenon. Star wars tv series are all shot in a circular room. Everything is arranged in a virtual circle even when they are outdoors. When you see it, you can't unsee it. Ps: having a hard time finding images from the shows. But here is the set. [1] [LINK] [2] [VIDEO]"},{"id":32076167,"author":"Kye","month":"2022-07","story":"Ask HN: Examples of large acquisitions that did not ruin the acquired company?","stance":"reference","why":"Disney's innovative filming approach for Disney+","quote":"","text":"Now it seems like Disney is doing the innovating with the way they film stuff for Disney+. [LINK] [VIDEO] Though how much of that is Disney and how much is the companies they worked with is unknown to me."},{"id":30776826,"author":"rdsubhas","month":"2022-03","story":"Hyper-realistic digital humans in Unity","stance":"reference","why":"Example of Unreal expanding into film production","quote":"","text":"Although you mean well, this sounds a lot like \"why does<X poor country> launch satellites or do science when their population is starving\". Doing one doesn't contradict the other. Can't afford to just keep narrowing down on existing customer base. They need to keep expanding and competing, for e.g. with Unreal [VIDEO]"},{"id":32458951,"author":"josteink","month":"2022-08","story":"Hollywood’s visual effects crisis","stance":"critical","why":"","quote":"when seeing that initial shot I could immediately tell the background was fake","text":"> This is a “solved problem” now with technologies like this: [VIDEO] The backgrounds are “real” and can be captured in-camera, so there is no need for special effects to blend hair. Similarly, reflective surfaces aren’t a problem either. While at the same time, when seeing that initial shot I could immediately tell the background was fake. Like in any fake-ass marvel-movie. And I’m not into VFX tech, I’m not into the industry as whole. I’m just a casual movie-watcher (going from being a hard-core movie-watcher), and my involvement in these movies is getting lower and lower as the movies increasingly gets less and less real. The last truly great movie I saw was Mad Max Fury Road. It has you engaged all the way through, because everything which matters there actually is real, something your mind can tell. I recently saw Bullet Train. It was OKish because it was mostly about characters, and it had a semi-decent story to tie it all together. All the excessive VFX bits at the end was pretty “meh“ though. It would have been a better movie without it."}]},"bsHryqnvyYA":{"wy":"HN cites it to show that a quadcopter can keep flying after losing a rotor, though under ideal lab conditions.","q":{"x":"You're wrong about a quadcopter not being able to fly with 3 rotors, see this video","i":13287449,"y":"2016"},"m":[{"id":20621606,"author":"asteli","month":"2019-08","story":"Swiss Post Suspends Drone Delivery Service After Second Crash","stance":"reference","why":"Spinning control mode trades yaw for altitude control","quote":"I recall a quadcopter control mode that spins the entire craft quite rapidly, exchanging yaw control for altitude control.","text":"I recall a quadcopter control mode that spins the entire craft quite rapidly, exchanging yaw control for altitude control. Found a vid: [VIDEO]"},{"id":13287449,"author":"avar","month":"2016-12","story":"Larry Page is pouring millions into flying cars","stance":"reference","why":"Quadcopter can fly with only three rotors","quote":"You're wrong about a quadcopter not being able to fly with 3 rotors, see this video","text":"The Ehang 184 has 8 propellers, two for each of the four quad points. I'd assume the reason is safety, that it can limp to the ground with just 7. You're wrong about a quadcopter not being able to fly with 3 rotors, see this video: [VIDEO] That looks like it would be quite a ride with a human on-board, but would probably suck less than crashing."},{"id":9616009,"author":"FatalLogic","month":"2015-05","story":"Damage Recovery Algorithm Could Make All Robots Unstoppable","stance":"reference","why":"Quadcopter recovers from losing one rotor by spinning","quote":"this quadcopter drone recovering from the loss of 1 rotor","text":"I wonder if the algorithm could be applied to flying robots or other flying vehicles? If there was sufficient height maybe? It reminds me of this quadcopter drone recovering from the loss of 1 rotor, [VIDEO] Although the drone in that video uses a pre-computed strategy, I think, not a general experiment and learning approach. And it recovers by spinning around on its axis, which saves the drone, and the environment, from further damage, but makes the damaged drone useless for most applications."},{"id":7008346,"author":"skorgu","month":"2014-01","story":"Even if the Feds Let Them Fly, Amazon's Delivery Drones Are Still Nonsense","stance":"reference","why":"Quadrocopter surviving propeller loss in lab conditions","quote":"Granted this is in ideal laboratory conditions but","text":"Aha! I couldn't find the video quickly for my first post but I finally stumbled on it. Granted this is in ideal laboratory conditions but: [VIDEO]"}]},"buqtdpuZxvk":{"wy":"HN people drop it as a joke in threads about stars, galaxies, and cosmic scale.","m":[{"id":40341269,"author":"GJim","month":"2024-05","story":"100k Stars","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"","text":"> It’s actually comforting to non-believers in a way that religion is to believers Summed up rather nicely in the Galaxy Song. [VIDEO]"},{"id":21179681,"author":"sago","month":"2019-10","story":"Not long ago, the center of the Milky Way exploded","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"it's worth learning to sing I find","text":"Give the (Monty) Python headline on today's HN, let me add that it's worth learning to sing [VIDEO] I find. The exact numbers are not quite exact, although they are usually in the right ballpark. \"We are 30,000 light-years from Galactic central point.\" But I found it so useful knowing that song over the years."},{"id":9657031,"author":"vacri","month":"2015-06","story":"How Much Do Skyscrapers Actually Move?","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"","text":"It's unbalanced forces you feel, not unbalanced acceleration per se. If you have a constant acceleration, it's because there's a consistent force on you. It's why this part of the article is wrong: > Humans are also terrible at perceiving velocity at a constant speed. [not perceiving sway] is why, when you’re traveling on a train at a steady fifty miles an hour, your body believes you might as well be sitting perfectly still. Humans in a train moving steadily are enclosed in an environment where everything is moving at the same speed as them - seats, air, everything. There are no unbalanced forces to feel in the first place. Just the same as we don't perceive the earth's movement around the sun (well, in a moving-body sense) or the sun's movement around the galaxy (which is blisteringly fast on a human scale); because our frame of reference moves with us. Of course, in the real world, trains do sway side-to-side and also up and down a little where the rails meet up (click-click click-click...), and we all feel that. But we're talking about a theory train here, and only looking at forward velocity :) Edit: Wikipedia says the sun orbits the galaxy at 220km/s. Monty Python's \"Galaxy Song\" says 40,000mph, which works out to 18km/s. Either way, it's pretty zippy for us humans. Galaxy Song: [VIDEO]"}]},"eAswvg60FnY":{"wy":"Posted as a joke when threads discuss unhelpful gadgets and smart devices.","m":[{"id":39453925,"author":"Lio","month":"2024-02","story":"Tech has graduated from the Star Trek era to the Douglas Adams age","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"","text":"There's one bit of Douglas Adams tech that fits closely with this: The Nutrimatic Drinks Dispenser[1], which dispenses something almost, but not quite entirely, unlike tea[2]. Reminds me of some of the code I've got from ChatGPT that almost, but not quite entirely, doesn't work. :P 1. [LINK] 2. [VIDEO]"},{"id":22691290,"author":"DonHopkins","month":"2020-03","story":"Unity Learn platform free for three months","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"","text":"I totally agree that JavaScript sucks, but that's not a good enough reason to boycott it, because it's absolutely ubiquitous and so well and widely supported. And no matter how bad it is as a language, it still beats compiled languages as a dynamic extension and scripting language. Another non-technical advantage: Apple allows iOS apps on their app store to dynamically download and execute JavaScript code, as long as you run it in Apple's Safari browser component (which UnityJS does). But even though it's technically possible, you aren't allowed to do that with Lua, Python, TCL, Visual Basic, Pascal, C++, C#, or any other interpreted or compiled language. So JavaScript occupies a special spot in the universe, the web ecosystem, and also the iOS ecosystem, that makes it not matter if it sucks. Plus a lot of smart people and big projects are working hard to mitigate its suckyness, make it run faster, and integrate it with all kinds of different platforms and APIs, like TypeScript, WebAssembly, AssemblyScript, React Native, Chrome Embedding Framework, Electron, and all the apps like PhotoShop you can script in JavaScript. Even Adobe finally figured out they should use the real thing instead of jerking you around with ActionScript or some other weird language like AppleScript. UnityScript sucks much much more than JavaScript, and has none of its advantages of tooling, libraries, running in every browser, etc. It's a terrible idea to make or use yet another language that's almost "},{"id":10175148,"author":"rwmj","month":"2015-09","story":"We don’t need a remote-controlled iKettle","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"","text":"If you've got a robot that can fetch water, pretty much all other problems are solved. Particularly if it can understand a limited set of spoken commands like \"make me a cup of tea\". Of course it could go horribly wrong: [VIDEO]"}]},"eFTLKWw542g":{"wy":"Mostly used as a passing reference or joke in threads about social problems being inherited from earlier generations.","m":[{"id":31270252,"author":"somenameforme","month":"2022-05","story":"Teen mental health is plummeting and social media is a major contributing cause [pdf]","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"","text":"Consider the early 20th century. Some ostensibly irrelevant political figure was assassinated which rapidly spiraled into the \"war to end all wars\" in which about 20 million people were killed, in a world population of 1.7 billion - so more than 1% of people. In relatively short order this led to the Great Depression in which over 10 years the global economy collapsed leading to mass suicide, starvation, and disorder. And we were just warming up. After that it turns out that the war to end all wars was just the pregame show. An eccentric vegetarian artist with a knack for riling up crowds decided to declare a literal genocidal war on the world, and for some time it looked very much like he was going to win. It only ended after another 80 million deaths (about 3% of the world population this time), the deployment of nuclear weapons, and countless war crimes. And then? Decades of people got to live in the great times where every day there was a real and legitimate concern of imminent nuclear war (which did nearly blast off multiple times) to the point that nuclear drills were a part of the classroom, and people building their own nuclear doomsday bunkers was not only not eccentric but just plain ole pragmatism. The point of this is not just to emphasize that the past was unimaginably and exponentially worse than the present in every imaginable way, but rather that there was no widespread mental health collapse. And there was no \"shielding\" kids from anything, when the world was"},{"id":16832901,"author":"TangoTrotFox","month":"2018-04","story":"Young Japanese are surprisingly content","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"Great song.","text":"> \"Ask people in their 20s and 30s what they think about the world, and over half of them will say the world's going to shit in one way or another. I know people on both sides of the political spectrum that will say that. This isn't a good sign for society.\" Do you know the song, \"We didn't Start the Fire\"? [1] Great song. It was released in 1989 - check out the lyrics. Even more interesting though is why the song was written. [2] Billy Joel was in a recording studio talking to a friend of John Lennon's son, who was 21 at the time. They had a conversation: - Friend: It's a terrible time to be 21 [because the world's going to shit] - Billy Joel: Yeah, I remember when I was 21 – I thought it was an awful time and we had Vietnam, and y'know, drug problems, and civil rights problems and everything seemed to be awful. - Friend: Yeah, yeah, yeah, but it's different for you. You were a kid in the fifties and everybody knows that nothing happened in the fifties. - Billy Joel: Wait a minute, didn't you hear of the Korean War or the Suez Canal Crisis? [1] - [VIDEO] [2] - [LINK]"},{"id":30028527,"author":"oramit","month":"2022-01","story":"Former Labor Secretary Found What Work Is Like Now","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"we didn't start the fire, but we can try to fight it.","text":"Another comment somewhere else in this thread mentioned 2008 and the bailouts for banks, bankruptcy for the public, as the place we should look at. With big society wide trends like this it's impossible to have a clear cause and effect. It's all a muddle which is why I've listed a few different ideas all of which contribute in their own way. Covid is such a unique situation though and I think it will serve as a clear turning point where preexisting trends were shattered and others were turned up to 11. But ultimately, we didn't start the fire, but we can try to fight it. [VIDEO]"},{"id":28662032,"author":"kongin","month":"2021-09","story":"Everyone Wants to Be an Influencer","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"some musical accompaniment","text":"That's because you were young and had no idea what was going on. A hilariously depressing exercise is just reading old news paper headlines from whatever decade you want to pick and seeing what the world was like then. I've yet to see a decade where the average person wasn't a hairs breadth away from dying in various unpleasant ways, some musical accompaniment: [VIDEO]"}]},"k5xf40KrK3I":{"wy":"HN mentions it in MrBeast threads as the source of allegations, often in a critical or skeptical context, not as a recommendation.","m":[{"id":45348158,"author":"SilverElfin","month":"2025-09","story":"MrBeast Failed to Disclose Ads and Improperly Collected Children's Data","stance":"critical","why":"","quote":"","text":"Does anyone remember when this person who worked for Mr Beast outed their fraudulent tactics? You couldn’t bring it up in a comment on their videos at all. They had a team continuous censoring all honest discussion on their videos. I find the whole phenomenon around Beast to be gross. [VIDEO]"},{"id":45348221,"author":"SilverElfin","month":"2025-09","story":"MrBeast Failed to Disclose Ads and Improperly Collected Children's Data","stance":"critical","why":"","quote":"","text":"It’s funny how he’s admired by Chamath and other Silicon Valley types for his entrepreneurship or good deeds or whatever when the core of how his channel works is deceiving viewers [VIDEO]"},{"id":45348245,"author":"SilverElfin","month":"2025-09","story":"MrBeast Failed to Disclose Ads and Improperly Collected Children's Data","stance":"critical","why":"","quote":"","text":"I just pass on this video from a former Mr Beast employee that tends to open people’s eyes up. Mr Beast has tried every tactic to bury and suppress this. In particular deleting all mention of it on any social media where his team can delete comments / replies. [VIDEO]"},{"id":41552252,"author":"blackeyeblitzar","month":"2024-09","story":"How to succeed in MrBeast production (Leaked PDF)","stance":"critical","why":"","quote":"","text":"In case people aren’t aware of the “fraud” accusations mentioned in this comment, here’s a video: [VIDEO] There are many more discussing all that has come out recently about that channel."},{"id":41552263,"author":"blackeyeblitzar","month":"2024-09","story":"How to succeed in MrBeast production (Leaked PDF)","stance":"critical","why":"","quote":"It alleges that many of the “contests” are staged and artificially manipulated","text":"See [VIDEO] It alleges that many of the “contests” are staged and artificially manipulated and potentially violate laws around such games. I think to many that might feel like fraud."},{"id":41321879,"author":"everforward","month":"2024-08","story":"The YouTube like button glows when you say \"smash that like button\" [video]","stance":"critical","why":"","quote":"That’s the original video that kicked it off.","text":"[VIDEO] That’s the original video that kicked it off. It’s pretty mundane as far as internet controversy goes. The lotteries were run poorly and as such were probably illegal, not exactly uncommon on YouTube. The show largely targets kids, so there’s some accusations of it effectively being gambling for kids. After that people dug into his whole life and found out one of his employees was convicted of statutory rape and he was aware, which is a bad look for a channel mostly watched by children. It’s like a 3/10 on internet controversy scales. Doing something common and dumb but not obviously malicious, and then just stuff that looks bad without concrete evidence anything bad actually happened. It’s not terribly interesting, I only saw because of a comedian making fun of it."}]},"nkuOuxRD1Bc":{"wy":"HN uses it as a joke reference whenever a startup pitch or market-sizing claim sounds naive or absurd.","m":[{"id":8100335,"author":"7Figures2Commas","month":"2014-07","story":"Uber vs. Car Ownership","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"Your approach to market sizing is eerily reminiscent of Ali G's[1].","text":"> Literally everyone. Everyone needs to move around their city. The size of the market is the size of the total city scale transportation market... Your approach to market sizing is eerily reminiscent of Ali G's[1]. Amusing, but not credible by any standard. [1] [VIDEO]"},{"id":3184376,"author":"thesash","month":"2011-11","story":"The Type of Companies That Publish Future Concept Videos","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"It reminds me of this clip from Ali G","text":"> These videos are made so that the delivery manager can understand what the people around him are doing, and so he can explain it to the bean counters. The irony is that concepts that intentionally ignore reality create the impression among those same people who \"don't get it\" that these concepts not only are grounded in reality, but are as trivial to create as the concept video makes it look. It reminds me of this clip from Ali G: [VIDEO]"},{"id":3028759,"author":"yarone","month":"2011-09","story":"What can entrepreneurs say to investors that marks them as amateurs?","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"Sasha baron Cohen pitched his ice cream glove to a bunch of vc's...","text":"Sasha baron Cohen pitched his ice cream glove to a bunch of vc's... [VIDEO] (not exactly what you had in mind...)"},{"id":2795268,"author":"jonmc12","month":"2011-07","story":"Two special startup types: the Twitter and the Hoverboard","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"Here is Ali G pitching a hoverboard to a VC.. great stuff","text":"Here is Ali G pitching a hoverboard to a VC.. great stuff: [VIDEO] - go to 5:20 on the video. Ali G: \"Its a hoverboard\" (holding a skateboard with no wheels) Investor: \".. but it doesn't do that\" Ali G: \"Well, it doesn't do it yet. Thats where you come in - with your science\""},{"id":925430,"author":"adriand","month":"2009-11","story":"Hacker news, but for ideas","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"This reminds me of the Ali G episode where he presents his idea for the ice cream glove","text":"The \"Cheap custom suits for Men\" idea is described simply as \"$100 custom suits for men.\". This reminds me of the Ali G episode where he presents his idea for the ice cream glove ( [VIDEO] ). The Venn Diagram for $100 custom suits would have \"all the people without much money\" in one circle, and \"all the people who want to be really well-dressed\" in the other. The intersection represents a huge market, which means this idea is nothing short of brilliant."},{"id":926116,"author":"jamesbressi","month":"2009-11","story":"Venture Capital For A 5th Grader","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"LOL, I love this!","text":"LOL, I love this! Ironic that someone on HN brought up this Ali G video [VIDEO] (where he invents the \"ice cream glove\" and Donald Trump thought he was going to say ice cream that doesn't melt) in response to an article on the front page today."}]},"o4TdHrMi6do":{"wy":"Referenced in threads about the two-billion-fps camera and equivalent-time sampling as an example of filming light moving.","m":[{"id":49348037,"author":"apt-get","month":"2026-08","story":"Using the railway network as a flatbed scanner","stance":"reference","why":"Filming light at 2 billion fps with single pixel.","quote":"","text":"Related - filming light at 2 billion fps with a somewhat similar setup in concept (at the single pixel scale): [VIDEO]"},{"id":47448450,"author":"nomel","month":"2026-03","story":"From Oscilloscope to Wireshark: A UDP Story","stance":"reference","why":"Equivalent-time sampling used to film light in a garage.","quote":"","text":"By definition, you can't interpolate a sample. A sample is a measured value. What you can do, if and only if you have an exactly repeating signal triggering at the same point within a cycle, is change the delay between the trigger and sample, and repeat. In other words, sample at different times within the same signal (since it's exactly repeating), to build up samples in time, of that waveform, to whatever time resolution you want. Of course, you're limited to any noise in the trigger, variation in the signal, etc. This is how you can record light moving through your garage [1]! [1] [VIDEO]"},{"id":45775899,"author":"westurner","month":"2025-10","story":"Man Builds Two Billion FPS Camera That Records at the Speed of Light","stance":"reference","why":"Laser pointer filmed at 2 billion fps.","quote":"","text":"OT video: \"A laser pointer at 2 billion fps makes the speed of light look... kinda weird\" (2025; AlphaPhoenix) [VIDEO]"}]},"onXNnlCYJ4Y":{"wy":"HN cites it as evidence in debates over the treadmill's design flaw and parental responsibility, often with a warning that it is disturbing.","m":[{"id":27051598,"author":"gvb","month":"2021-05","story":"Peloton recalling all treadmills after reports of injuries, one death","stance":"reference","why":"CPSC video of child pulled under machine","quote":"the video is horrifying to watch","text":"The U.S. Consumer Product Safety Commission released a video of a child being sucked under the machine. The child was not injured in this instance, but the video is horrifying to watch. [VIDEO]"},{"id":27051655,"author":"Johnny555","month":"2021-05","story":"Peloton recalling all treadmills after reports of injuries, one death","stance":"reference","why":"Describes what the widely circulated video shows","quote":"","text":"Did you watch the video that was widely circulated in the news? A child is playing with a ball (around 12\" in diameter), it gets sucked under and traps his hands, then the treadmill pulls his entire body under as the treadmill itself (which weighs 300-450lb) moves on top of him. In this case the child was lucky that the ball didn't burst so he was able to climb back out. [VIDEO]"},{"id":26846861,"author":"rgbrenner","month":"2021-04","story":"Regulators urge safety recall of Peloton treadmill after child dies","stance":"reference","why":"Shows child pulled under treadmill, confirming missing guard","quote":"Edit: confirmed (warning: video shows a child being injured)","text":"Most treadmills have a guard or plastic covering on the bottom that stops the tread from pulling something underneath it. From pictures, the peloton treadmill doesnt have this... so I would guess if anything gets between the back of the device and the floor, it'll pull it under the machine. Edit: confirmed (warning: video shows a child being injured): [VIDEO]"},{"id":26850464,"author":"omnicognate","month":"2021-04","story":"Regulators urge safety recall of Peloton treadmill after child dies","stance":"reference","why":"Shows the accident other treadmill designs would prevent","quote":"in case you haven't seen the video","text":"If you do a google images search for \"running treadmill\" you'll see most of them have some design aspect that would prevent the accident shown in the video, most commonly a bar or guard of some sort that would stop a child or large object being dragged under. Edit: in case you haven't seen the video: [VIDEO]"},{"id":26852033,"author":"hagy","month":"2021-04","story":"Regulators urge safety recall of Peloton treadmill after child dies","stance":"reference","why":"Cites CPSC video while arguing parental responsibility over regulation","quote":"two young children playing on an active treadmill unsupervised","text":"I agree. The CPSC graphic video [0], graphically demonstrates the \"safety failure\" of a Pelton treadmill with two young children playing on an active treadmill unsupervised. While I certainly agree Pelton and other manufactures should attempt to mitigate the dangers of such a negligent situation, I was astonished that parents would allow this to happen. Imagine the same video, but replace the treadmill with a loaded bench press. Pelton highlights that there is a safety key required to operate the machine and that parents are instructed to store it in a secure location inaccessible to children. [1] No treadmill manufacturer can ever fully eliminate the dangers of an active treadmill to young children and pets, and therefore I think this safety key is the essential piece. I find this similar to locking power tools away from curious young hands. I don't want this to lead to additional regulation and compliance. Such regulation could even mislead consumers as to the danger of a treadmill. E.g., requiring some autodetection of a stuck belt to stop the motor. A foolproof mechanism would be not only expensive, but require regular inspection and maintenance to guarantee it performs this function. One could trust this mechanism while neglecting maintenance and therefore be further negligent in not securing the safety key away from children. [0] [VIDEO] [1] [LINK]"},{"id":30262134,"author":"29athrowaway","month":"2022-02","story":"Peloton replaces CEO and lays off 2,800 people","stance":"critical","why":"","quote":"Why I will never buy any Peloton product for my home","text":"Why I will never buy any Peloton product for my home... Peloton Tread+ Treadmill Safety Incident: [VIDEO] (viewer discretion is advised)"},{"id":27280480,"author":"fastball","month":"2021-05","story":"Peloton to invest $400M to build its first U.S. manufacturing facility in Ohio","stance":"critical","why":"","quote":"It is absolutely a massive design flaw that this treadmill lacks a protective guard","text":"Yes, a plastic guard creates opportunities for catching fingers and clothing, but (unsurprisingly) people think this is preferable to their child's head getting caught in the treadmill[1]. A child was killed by one of these things. It is absolutely a massive design flaw that this treadmill lacks a protective guard and/or an automatic cutoff that triggers when it sees an unexpected level of resistance. You know, like if a child's head gets caught in it. [1] [VIDEO]"},{"id":26849210,"author":"romwell","month":"2021-04","story":"Regulators urge safety recall of Peloton treadmill after child dies","stance":"critical","why":"","quote":"it got that attention because it is an unsafe treadmill","text":"There are many treadmills out there. And those treadmills don't tell to pull people underneath them , like this device did. Let's not be facetious here, Peloton didn't get CPSC's attention because it is a treadmill -- it got that attention because it is an unsafe treadmill. As far as treadmills go, preventing being pulled underneath has been a solved problem. EDIT: compare and contrast: what Peloton does [1] vs. a $700 treadmill design [2]. [1] [VIDEO] [2] [LINK]"}]},"spw176TZ7-8":{"wy":"HN mostly cites it critically as evidence that early Waymos drove hesitantly, though one commenter points to the full video for context on a braking incident.","m":[{"id":18708672,"author":"cjhopman","month":"2018-12","story":"Anthony Levandowski: Pronto AI","stance":"reference","why":"Full video explains the Waymo braking incident","quote":"the waymo car stopped because a car off-camera ran the red into the intersection","text":"> In this video, the Waymo isn't avoiding a crash so much as potentially causing a rear-ender: [LINK] wtf. This is a shitty example. If you either (1) read the captions on the video or (2) watch their full video where they talk about this, you'd realize that the waymo car stopped because a car off-camera ran the red into the intersection and that braking actually was avoiding a crash (or rather, decreasing the severity of a crash). [VIDEO]"},{"id":42438765,"author":"KennyBlanken","month":"2024-12","story":"Waymo will bring autonomous vehicles to Tokyo","stance":"critical","why":"","quote":"Your cars barely able to make a lane change under the most ideal circumstances","text":"[LINK] > One of the world’s first fully-driverless taxi firms has been blocked from expanding its business in California, where several of its vehicles have recently been involved in accidents. > Waymo, which is the driverless car division of Google parent Alphabet, currently operates in parts of San Francisco. Attempts to roll-out its robotaxi service to Sunnyvale and Los Angeles were suspended for up to 120 days following a ruling by the California Public Utilities Commission’s Consumer Protection and Enforcement Division (CPED). Then there's the federal investigation for Arizona crashes: [LINK] Your cars honking at each other in their own parking lot, at 4AM: [LINK] Your cars getting confused by a traffic cone: [LINK] Your cars barely able to make a lane change under the most ideal circumstances: [VIDEO] Unable to respond to the instructions of a police officer and it taking a minute and a half for employees to manually control the car: [LINK] Blocking a fire truck: [LINK] Breaking the law and getting pulled over by police for driving in the oncoming lane: [LINK] One of your cars cutting off another one of your own cars in a wildly unsafe, illegal maneuver: One of your cars running a red light: [LINK] ...and then a few minutes later, illegally entering an intersection and stopping, then continuing to block the intersection because someone made a u-turn 30 feet in front of it while it was stopped: [LINK] And here's the icing on the cake: your company harassing reporters, ca"},{"id":42438799,"author":"KennyBlanken","month":"2024-12","story":"Waymo will bring autonomous vehicles to Tokyo","stance":"critical","why":"","quote":"They can barely change lanes","text":"They can barely change lanes: [VIDEO] ...and are completely flummoxed by a couple of traffic cones closing off a lane: [LINK]"},{"id":19861969,"author":"tim333","month":"2019-05","story":"GM Cruise raises $1.15B at a $19B valuation from Softbank and Honda","stance":"critical","why":"","quote":"the Waymos seemed to have some issues","text":"They have some quite impressive demo videos. Also they've been operating in demanding conditions compared to Waymo. eg NYC rush hour [LINK] Lane change in heavy traffic [LINK] while when reporters \"Followed a Waymo Self-driving Car for Miles' the Waymos seemed to have some issues [VIDEO]"}]},"vt9kRLrmrjc":{"wy":"HN commenters cite it as an example of claims that government can access digital communications and that none is secure.","m":[{"id":6151682,"author":"espeed","month":"2013-08","story":"NSA Collects 'Word for Word' Every Domestic Communication, Says Former Analyst","stance":"reference","why":"CNN follow-up interview on government capturing communications.","quote":"CNN Follow-Up Interview","text":"Reposting this from a previous thread ( [LINK] )... I remember during the Boston bombing investigation, Tim Clemente, a former FBI counter-terrorism agent, told Erin Burnett on CNN that they could go back and get access to the content of the calls between the deceased bomber and his wife, Katherine Russell. After some Googling, I found a partial transcript of the CNN interview... \"Almost immediately Erin Burnett, the host of CNN's Outfront, wanted to know how the government knew. Aren't phone calls supposed to be private? She interviewed Tim Clemente, a former FBI counter-terrorism agent on May 1, asking:\" Is there any way … they [the federal investigators] can try to get the phone companies to give that up … It’s not a voice mail. It's just a conversation. There’s no way they can actually find out what [was said on the call], right, unless she tells them? Clemente: There is a way. We certainly have ways in national security investigations to find out exactly what was said in that conversation. It's not necessarily something that the FBI is going to want to present in court, but it may help lead the investigation … we certainly can find that out. Burnett: So they can actually get that? … that is incredible. Clemente: Welcome to America. All of that stuff is being captured as we speak, whether we know it or like it, or not. Source: [LINK] CNN Interview Clip ( [LINK] ) CNN Follow-Up Interview ( [VIDEO] ) When I initially saw this on CNN, my first thought was, do they also have "},{"id":6004653,"author":"fossuser","month":"2013-07","story":"A thorough PGP tutorial","stance":"reference","why":"Cited as example of claims that no digital communication is secure.","quote":"","text":"I agree with your first two points and if the complexity of using pgp were reduced to that of simply using email then I think it'd become more widely used. I didn't mean to imply that cryptocat itself is a reasonable compromise (I don't know enough), but that a centralized system that implements encryption for its users might be which leads into your fourth point. Yes javascript client-side encryption is fundamentally flawed in its current form from targeted attacks, but I'm thinking of untargeted and immediate wide spread adoption. If you're collecting everything from everyone in perpetuity the risk for abuse I see is when someone becomes interesting to the government they can query their data set and use it against them. I'd think there would be a way to use client side encryption by these companies to protect a user's data until they become 'interesting' at which point they'd have to use more fundamentally secure methods (which is how it currently is anyway). Seems like the easiest way to get the most people generally protected from abuse. Is using https enough? If that's the case then it seems like most communication would be protected anyway. I got the impression that this wasn't true - not because I don't trust google, but because of something else (it seemed google genuinely didn't give access to everything yet people made comments about no digital communication being secure: [VIDEO] )."},{"id":5866854,"author":"lurchpop","month":"2013-06","story":"Shia LaBeouf: phone surveillance whistleblower? ","stance":"reference","why":"FBI agent says all digital communications are recorded.","quote":"No digital communication is secure","text":"There's also this: FBI agent for the second time says all communications are recorded. \"No digital communication is secure\" [VIDEO]"},{"id":5897903,"author":"espeed","month":"2013-06","story":"Journalists Need To Start Asking About Storage, Not Access","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"","text":"I remember during the Boston bombing investigation, Tim Clemente, a former FBI counter-terrorism agent, told Erin Burnett on CNN that they could go back and get access to the content of the calls between the deceased bomber and his wife, Katherine Russell. After some Googling, I found a partial transcript of the CNN interview... \"Almost immediately Erin Burnett, the host of CNN's Outfront, wanted to know how the government knew. Aren't phone calls supposed to be private? She interviewed Tim Clemente, a former FBI counter-terrorism agent on May 1, asking:\" Is there any way … they [the federal investigators] can try to get the phone companies to give that up … It’s not a voice mail. It's just a conversation. There’s no way they can actually find out what [was said on the call], right, unless she tells them? Clemente: There is a way. We certainly have ways in national security investigations to find out exactly what was said in that conversation. It's not necessarily something that the FBI is going to want to present in court, but it may help lead the investigation … we certainly can find that out. Burnett: So they can actually get that? … that is incredible. Clemente: Welcome to America. All of that stuff is being captured as we speak, whether we know it or like it, or not. Source: [LINK] When I initially saw this on CNN, my first thought was, do they also have access to all photos and videos that are taken and transmitted online? And if so, couldn't they stitch together a mult"}]}});
